[
 {
  "id": "E1-001",
  "page": "A1",
  "section": "News",
  "title": "What's News",
  "dek": null,
  "byline": null,
  "body_en": "Business & Finance\n\n- Prediction markets, such as Polymarket, have allowed betting on criminal cases, including the Lindsay Clancy murder trial, creating unusual challenges to the justice system. (A1)\n\n- Amazon.com is in talks to acquire AI startup Decart in a deal that could be valued at about $7 billion. (B1)\n\n- Electric vehicle sales in the U.S. are down by half compared with last year, but charging companies are on track to install about as many public EV plugs this year as in 2025, the industry's record high. (B1)\n\n- United Auto Workers President Shawn Fain and his allies have claimed an easy victory in the union's election, cementing Fain's leadership of one of the most powerful forces in American organized labor. (B3)\n\n- Jaguar has introduced the showroom-ready version of the EV it hopes will usher in a new, ultraluxury era for the British carmaker, leaving aside some of the provocative notes it sounded nearly two years ago when it announced its new strategy. (B3)\n\n- Advertisers from Macy's to Levi Strauss have announced that a portion of the multimillion-dollar tariff refunds they received from the federal government will go to boost marketing spending as the holiday shopping season nears. (B3)\n\n- Dutch Bros, the fast-growing, drive-through coffee chain known for its colorful drinks and outgoing baristas, has a strategy to attract pedestrian traffic: walk-up-only windows. (B3)\n\nWorldwide\n\n- President Trump pushed Russia and Ukraine to agree to halt strikes on energy infrastructure, as he continued to face bipartisan criticism over moves in recent days to ease U.S. pressure on Moscow and shift blame for the continued fighting to Kyiv. (A1)\n\n- The Saudis' escalating fight with Iran-backed Houthi militants in Yemen is piling new challenges on the kingdom's security and Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman's plans to transform the country's oil-dependent economy. (A1)\n\n- California Gov. Newsom, who has long opposed a ballot measure that would tax billionaires' wealth, is endorsing a different measure aimed at prohibiting wealth taxes. (A3)\n\n- A federal judge ordered the Trump administration to halt any further airing of taxpayer-funded television ads touting the president, ruling they are likely illegal. (A3)\n\n- The Justice Department is investigating whether TV networks violated antitrust laws when they briefly suspended a rotation of broadcast journalists covering the White House last month following CNN's removal from a planned assignment. (A3)\n\n- Christa Pike, the inmate who survived Tennessee's attempt to execute her by lethal injection, has been discharged from the hospital and is back in prison. (A5)\n\n- India locked down the capital in a bid to thwart a mass protest over an effort to clean up voter lists that critics say is stripping millions of the right to vote. (A9)",
  "priority": "P3",
  "type": "index_digest",
  "turn_to": null,
