 {
  "id": "E1-003",
  "page": "A1",
  "section": "News",
  "title": "Kenyan, Ethiopian Win Chicago Marathon",
  "dek": null,
  "byline": null,
  "body_en": "RACE DAY: Runners compete Sunday during the 2026 Chicago Marathon, with more than 55,000 entrants registered. Amos Kipruto of Kenya finished first, with a time of 2:01:09, while Ethiopia's Hawi Feysa defended her women's title in 2:13:33. (Photo: KAMIL KRZACZYNSKI/AFP/GETTY IMAGES)",
  "priority": "P3",
  "type": "photo_caption",
  "turn_to": null,
  "terms": [
   {
    "en": "Amos Kipruto",
    "zh_suggested": "阿莫斯·基普鲁托",
    "note": "肯尼亚马拉松选手"
   },
   {
    "en": "Hawi Feysa",
    "zh_suggested": "哈维·费萨",
    "note": "埃塞俄比亚马拉松选手，待查"
   }
  ],
  "final_id": "003",
  "title_cn": "肯尼亚和埃塞俄比亚选手分别获得芝加哥马拉松赛的冠军。",
  "dek_cn": null,
  "body_cn": "比赛日：在2026年芝加哥马拉松比赛中，超过55000名选手参加了比赛。肯尼亚选手阿莫斯·基普鲁托以2小时01分09秒的成绩获得冠军，而埃塞俄比亚选手哈维·费萨以2小时13分33秒的成绩成功卫冕女子组冠军。（图片来源：KAMIL KRZACZYNSKI/AFP/GETTY IMAGES）",
  "context_cn": null,
  "context_attr": null
 },
 {
  "id": "E1-004",
  "page": "A1",
  "section": "News",
  "title": "Trump's Russia Energy Deal Gets Pushback",
  "dek": "As president presses a Ukraine ceasefire, senators call his export plan illegal",
  "byline": "BY MARCUS WALKER AND ROBBIE GRAMER",
  "body_en": "President Trump pushed Russia and Ukraine to agree to halt strikes on each other's energy infrastructure, as the White House continued to face bipartisan criticism over moves in recent days to ease U.S. pressure on Moscow and shift blame for the continued fighting to Kyiv.\n\nTrump on social media Sunday said there was an immediate \"ENERGY CEASEFIRE\" between Ukraine and Russia, despite Moscow's rejection of such a deal so far and no sign that there was an agreement in place.\n\nThe latest Trump statement came as he encountered widespread pushback on Capitol Hill to his decision to allow Russia to export oil to the U.S. and world markets and his harsh remarks toward Ukraine's President Volodymyr Zelensky. On Sunday, a group of Democratic and Republican senators issued a joint statement calling Trump's export plan illegal and contrary to a 2022 law that banned Russian energy imports.\n\nTrump is moving quickly to try to ease energy prices with the U.S. midterm elections just weeks away, with high gasoline and diesel prices driven by the Iran war and the Russia-Ukraine conflict contributing to voter discontent toward Republicans. But analysts said the impact of new Russian supplies on lowering prices would likely be minimal.\n\n[Continued on page A8 under the headline \"Russian Oil Deal Faces Pushback\":]\n\nRussian President Vladimir Putin has rebuffed proposals for an energy ceasefire, and the Kremlin didn't respond to a request for comment. Trump previously announced such a ceasefire on Sept. 14; Russia and Ukraine continued their strikes.\n\nZelensky reiterated on Sunday that he is open to stopping airstrikes against Russian oil refineries if Moscow stops bombing Ukraine's energy grid, but he has staved off Trump's pressure for Kyiv to cease its strikes unilaterally. He said Ukraine's skies had been \"infested\" with Russian drone and bomb strikes throughout the day.\n\nThe White House didn't respond to a request for comment on the senators' statement.\n\nRussia continued to bomb Ukraine's energy grid and other targets in Kyiv on Sunday, with explosions audible throughout the day and power cuts continuing.\n\n\"Ukraine wants peace. The question is whether Russia does,\" Zelensky said in a social-media post. \"We are awaiting clear details of the dialogue between America and Russia—and we expect Putin to follow through on what President Trump said.\"\n\nDemocrats and some Republicans have objected to Trump's efforts to ease economic pressure on Russia, which launched its full-scale invasion of Ukraine in February 2022.\n\nThe U.S. \"should not provide Putin with a financial lifeline while Russia continues to kill Ukrainian civilians. The administration must immediately reverse course,\" a bipartisan group of senators said in a statement on Sunday. The group is made up of Republicans Susan Collins of Maine, Lisa Murkowski of Alaska, John Curtis of Utah and Thom Tillis of North Carolina and Democratic Sens. Jeanne Shaheen of New Hampshire, Ron Wyden of Oregon and Richard Blumenthal of Connecticut.\n\nThe senators said the administration failed to consult with Congress or submit the justification required under the 2022 law barring Russian energy imports to the U.S.\n\nTrump's statement Sunday came after he lambasted Zelensky on Saturday, calling for his removal as Ukrainian leader and blaming him for Russia's continuing war on Ukraine—as well as for pushing up fuel prices, which have risen globally since the U.S.-Iran war disrupted Middle East energy exports.\n\nWith no end to the conflict with Iran in sight, Trump presented Russia as part of a solution, announcing a deal with Putin on Friday to boost the supply of Russian diesel to the U.S. and world markets.\n\nEnergy experts say the deal, if implemented, could benefit the Kremlin's coffers but is unlikely to provide significant relief to global energy markets.\n\n\"Our president is trying to find every answer possible,\" said Rep. Tom Emmer (R., Minn.) on ABC News on Sunday, defending the deal on Russian diesel.\n\nDemocrats saw it as a last-ditch effort to bring down prices ahead of the elections. \"Literally, Trump has made a deal with the devil. He will do anything for Republicans to win\" the midterms, said Rep. Donald Norcross (D., N.J.).\n\nRussia has been planning to resume exports of diesel anyway, said Janis Kluge, head of Eastern Europe research at the German Institute for International and Security Affairs, a think tank in Berlin. \"There was no concession from Putin in this deal,\" he said.\n\nExtra Russian diesel is unlikely to relieve the stress in global fuel markets. \"The Middle East is much more important,\" he said.\n\nTrump's comments in recent days have shown the limits of U.S. leverage in the war between Russia and Ukraine, as Europe, rather than the U.S., has become the mainstay of support for Kyiv's defense against Moscow's invasion.\n\nUkraine has greatly expanded domestic output of drones and other weaponry, while relying on European allies for financing and military aid. Trump has ended nearly all U.S. aid for Ukraine's defense, though the U.S. continues to share valuable satellite and other intelligence with Kyiv.\n\nBombing Russian refineries is the strongest card that Ukraine holds to force Putin to stop the war he launched nearly five years ago.\n\nRussia is bombarding Ukraine's energy infrastructure in an attempt to cut off heating, electricity, water and other essential services ahead of winter. In the past week, Russia has attacked Ukraine's electricity system 196 times and its oil and gas infrastructure 20 times, Zelensky said in an interview with ABC News broadcast on Sunday.",
  "priority": "P1",
  "type": "article",
  "turn_to": "A8",
