  "title_cn": "特朗普与俄罗斯的能源协议遭遇阻力",
  "dek_cn": "在总统推动乌克兰停火的同时，参议员们称其出口计划违反法律。",
  "body_cn": "特朗普总统敦促俄罗斯和乌克兰同意停止对彼此能源基础设施的袭击，此前，白宫因近几日采取的措施，即减轻美国对莫斯科的压力，并将持续冲突的责任归咎于基辅，而持续面临两党的批评。\n\n特朗普周日在社交媒体上表示，乌克兰和俄罗斯之间立即达成“能源停火”协议，尽管莫斯科迄今为止一直拒绝达成此类协议，并且没有迹象表明双方已达成协议。\n\n特朗普的最新声明是在他决定允许俄罗斯向美国和世界市场出口石油，以及他对乌克兰总统弗拉基米尔·泽连斯基发表严厉批评后，在国会山遭遇了广泛的反对。周日，一群民主党和共和党参议员发表联合声明，称特朗普的出口计划是非法的，并且违反了2022年的一项法律，该法律禁止进口俄罗斯能源。\n\n随着美国中期选举仅剩数周，特朗普正迅速采取行动，试图降低能源价格，而伊朗战争和俄罗斯-乌克兰冲突导致汽油和柴油价格飙升，这加剧了选民对共和党的反感。但分析人士表示，新的俄罗斯供应对降低价格的影响可能微乎其微。\n\n[请参见A8页，标题为“俄罗斯石油协议面临阻力”:]\n\n俄罗斯总统弗拉基米尔·普京已拒绝了关于能源停火的提议，克里姆林宫也未对置评请求做出回应。特朗普此前于9月14日宣布了此类停火；俄罗斯和乌克兰继续进行袭击。\n\n泽连斯基周日重申，如果莫斯科停止轰炸乌克兰的能源网络，他愿意停止对俄罗斯炼油厂的空袭，但他抵制了特朗普要求基辅单方面停止袭击的压力。他说，乌克兰的天空一整天都被俄罗斯的无人机和炸弹袭击所“侵扰”。\n\n白宫未对参议员的声明置评。\n\n周日，俄罗斯继续轰炸乌克兰的能源网络和其他位于基辅的目标，爆炸声一整天都能听到，并且电力中断仍在持续。\n\n泽连斯基在社交媒体帖子中说：“乌克兰想要和平。问题是俄罗斯是否也想要。”“我们正在等待美国和俄罗斯之间对话的明确细节——我们希望普京兑现特朗普总统所说的话。”\n\n民主党人和一些共和党人反对特朗普试图减轻对俄罗斯的经济压力，因为俄罗斯于 2022 年 2 月对乌克兰发动了全面入侵。\n\n一份由参议员们发布的声明称，美国“不应给普京提供财政生命线，而俄罗斯仍在继续杀害乌克兰平民。政府必须立即改变政策。”这份声明由来自缅因州的苏珊·柯林斯、来自阿拉斯加州的丽莎·穆尔科斯基、来自犹他州的约翰·柯蒂斯和来自北卡罗来纳州的汤姆·蒂利斯等共和党参议员，以及来自新罕布什尔州的珍妮·沙欣、来自俄勒冈州的罗恩·怀登和来自康涅狄格州的理查德·布卢门撒尔等民主党参议员共同签署。\n\n这些参议员表示，政府未能与国会进行磋商，也没有提交根据 2022 年法律要求提供的理由，该法律禁止俄罗斯向美国出口能源。\n\n特朗普在周日发表声明之前，曾在周六严厉批评了泽连斯基，呼吁将其罢免乌克兰领导人职务，并指责他应对俄罗斯对乌克兰持续的战争负责——以及对全球燃料价格上涨负责，自美伊战争扰乱中东能源出口以来，全球燃料价格一直在上涨。\n\n由于与伊朗的冲突看不到结束的迹象，特朗普将俄罗斯描述为解决方案的一部分，并在周五宣布与普京达成协议，以增加俄罗斯向美国和世界市场供应柴油。\n\n能源专家表示，如果该协议得以实施，可能会使克里姆林宫的财政收入增加，但不太可能对全球能源市场提供显著的缓解。\n\n在周日的 ABC 新闻节目中，众议员汤姆·埃默（共和党，明尼苏达州）为该柴油协议辩护，他说：“我们的总统正在努力寻找一切可能的解决方案。”\n\n民主党人认为，这是在选举前夕，为了降低价格而采取的最后一次尝试。众议员唐纳德·诺克罗斯（民主党，新泽西州）说：“从字面上看，特朗普与魔鬼达成了协议。他会不惜一切代价，以确保共和党赢得中期选举。”\n\n德国国际与安全事务研究所（柏林一家智库）的东欧研究负责人亚尼斯·克卢格表示，俄罗斯原本就计划恢复柴油出口。“普京在这项协议中没有做出任何让步，”他说。\n\n额外的俄罗斯柴油不太可能缓解全球燃料市场的压力。“中东地区更为重要，”他说。\n\n特朗普最近的言论表明，美国在俄乌战争中的影响力有限。欧洲而非美国，已成为支持基辅对抗莫斯科入侵的主要力量。\n\n乌克兰大幅增加了无人机和其他武器的国内产量，同时依赖欧洲盟友提供资金和军事援助。特朗普已经停止了几乎所有美国对乌克兰防御的援助，但美国仍在继续向基辅提供有价值的卫星和其他情报。\n\n轰炸俄罗斯的炼油厂是乌克兰用来迫使普京停止这场他五年前发动的战争的最强有力手段。\n\n俄罗斯正在轰炸乌克兰的能源基础设施，试图在冬季来临之前切断供暖、电力、供水和其他基本服务。泽连斯基在接受美国广播公司（ABC）新闻采访时表示，在过去一周里，俄罗斯对乌克兰的电力系统发动了 196 次袭击，对乌克兰的石油和天然气基础设施发动了 20 次袭击。",
  "context_cn": "特朗普周日在社交媒体上宣称俄乌已达成“立即能源停火”，但莫斯科此前已拒绝此类提议，且他 9 月 14 日宣布的停火并未阻止双方继续互袭。两党参议员依据 2022 年禁止进口俄罗斯能源的法律，称其允许俄罗斯石油出口的计划违法，且政府未按法律要求向国会提交理由。背景是中期选举仅剩数周，美伊战争推高了汽油和柴油价格；乌克兰方面称，过去一周俄方对其电力系统发动了 196 次袭击、对油气设施 20 次。",
  "context_attr": "原文有"
 },
 {
  "id": "E1-005",
  "page": "A1",
  "section": "News",
  "title": "Saudi Crown Prince's Burden Grows Heavier as Houthis Strike",
  "dek": "Kingdom is facing myriad dilemmas as Iran-backed rebels intensify attacks on the kingdom's security and",
  "byline": "BY SUMMER SAID AND GEORGI KANTCHEV",
  "body_en": "DUBAI—Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman started the year on a high note. He'd just visited the White House where he was embraced by President Trump. Even after the war with Iran kicked off, Saudi Arabia looked relatively well positioned: It could export oil through a pipeline that bypassed the blocked Strait of Hormuz.\n\nBut the Saudis' escalating fight with Iran-backed Houthi militants in neighboring Yemen now piles new challenges on the kingdom's security and the crown prince's ambitious but already struggling plans to transform the country's oil-dependent economy.\n\nOn Saturday, a Houthi missile slammed into the Saudi capital's international airport, killing at least 12 people and injuring hundreds more, many seriously. It was the single deadliest attack on the U.S. or its Gulf allies in the conflicts related to the Iran war.\n\nA 28-year-old Egyptian flight attendant bled to death after her arm was blown off, one of several victims to lose limbs, officials briefed on the attack said.\n\nTwo terminals were struck and their roofs partially collapsed, people familiar with the damage said. Video verified by Storyful, which like The Wall Street Journal is owned by Dow Jones, showed passengers rolling suitcases past a trail of blood on the floor as the airport was evacuated. Authorities reported new attacks on the shuttered airport Sunday, saying a projectile caused a fire in Terminal 4 and led to injuries.\n\nAs recently as this summer, Saudi officials briefed journalists that the Houthi situation was under control. But the militants' escalating attacks, which they say are retaliation for Saudi actions against them, have hit airports and energy facilities—including the kingdom's main east-west pipeline and refineries—showing the growing threat along the southern border.\n\nThe conflict is jeopardizing Saudi efforts to build travel and tourism into a pillar of the economy, including with a marquee international investment conference scheduled for\n\n[Continued on page A7 under the headline \"Challenges Piling Up For Saudis\":]\n\nthe end of the month that looks increasingly in doubt.\n\nThe costs of the war have already widened the budget deficit and threaten to push the economy into contraction.\n\nHow the crown prince handles this test will have consequences beyond the kingdom. Saudi Arabia is a major source of funding for the global tech industry and artificial-intelligence projects. It also provides basing and overflight rights to the U.S. military.\n\nTrump told reporters over the weekend that the U.S. was in communication with the Houthis but might join Saudi strikes on the militant group.\n\nOn Sunday, a Houthi military spokesman reiterated the group's warning that Saudi airports are targets. Saudi officials said they are running low on interceptors needed to fend off further strikes.\n\nAttacks on Riyadh—about 500 miles from Houthi territory in Yemen—have transformed parts of the Saudi capital into a battle zone, with residents taking cover as explosions ring out across the city. Debris from an intercepted ballistic missile hit a kindergarten and a medical clinic complex Thursday. Workers in the city's financial district, students at some international schools and diplomats were ordered to evacuate or take shelter.\n\nThe 41-year-old crown prince is shuttling between palaces equipped with bunkers and is guarded by a handpicked security team, officials familiar with his movements said. His last public appearance was Tuesday.\n\nHe has few good options, said Kristian Coates Ulrichsen, a Persian Gulf expert and fellow with Rice University's Baker Institute. \"Any military response risks getting drawn in deeper to an asymmetric conflict.\"\n\nSaudi press officials didn't respond to a request for comment.\n\nThe crown prince has plowed funds over the past decade into a slate of futuristic projects with extraordinarily complex engineering requirements aimed at signaling the kingdom's modernization and drawing investment and tourism. They include the futuristic city of Neom, an enormous cube-shaped building in Riyadh and a desert ski resort. The total cost ran to the trillions of dollars—aggressive for a government with an annual budget of around $350 billion.\n\nThe costs and economic damage of war with Iran are making that already unlikely math impossible to balance.\n\nForeign investment fell in the first two quarters of the year and is running well behind the crown prince's 2030 target. Rising defense and infrastructure costs threaten to siphon away money needed for the economic diversification push. Even the kingdom's push to become an AI hub now carries new risks, with data centers emerging as targets.\n\nMany of the projects pushed by the crown prince are stalled or under review.\n\nThe non-oil side of the country's economy was smaller in the second quarter than in the first, according to Capital Economics. The Saudi Finance Ministry now expects GDP to shrink by 3.6% in 2026.\n\nThe kingdom was able earlier in the year to pump oil across the desert to the Red Sea to escape Iran's chokehold on the Strait of Hormuz. But in July, the Houthis announced a maritime blockade of Saudi Arabia, putting Red Sea shipping at risk. In September, an attack on the East-West pipeline struck at the kingdom's main escape route from Hormuz.\n\nThe crown prince got Saudi Arabia involved in Yemen's civil war in 2015, backing the country's internationally recognized government in a fight against the Houthis. It carried out airstrikes and inflamed the conflict, but also invited Houthi attacks on its oil facilities and cities including Riyadh. The sides fought to a stalemate and a ceasefire in 2022.\n\nThe new outbreak came as the crown prince was working to contain the confrontation with Iran. Attacks by Tehran early in the war demonstrated the risk to energy facilities and economic assets like airports across the Gulf. Saudi Arabia was also struggling to cover the vast country with air defense and was shifting batteries around to try to protect palaces and key energy infrastructure, people familiar with the kingdom's deliberations said. While supportive of continued economic pressure on Iran, the crown prince was telling his advisers to keep the diplomatic channel open with Iran and make clear the kingdom's intentions weren't confrontational, the people said.\n\nThe kingdom has called for help from its military ally Pakistan and is scouring the globe for air defenses, the people said. It is also looking to recruit foreign fighters to help its Yemeni allies, they said.",
  "priority": "P1",
  "type": "article",
  "turn_to": "A7",
