  "final_id": "005",
  "title_cn": "胡塞武装发动袭击，沙特王储重担加重",
  "dek_cn": "沙特王国正面临着诸多困境，伊朗支持的反叛武装正在加剧对该国安全的袭击。",
  "body_cn": "迪拜——沙特王储穆罕默德·本·萨勒曼以积极的姿态开始了新的一年。他刚刚访问了白宫，并受到了特朗普总统的热情欢迎。即使在与伊朗的战争爆发后，沙特阿拉伯的处境也相对较好：它可以通过一条绕过受阻的霍尔木兹海峡的管道出口石油。\n\n但沙特与伊朗支持的也门胡塞武装分子在邻国也门日益激烈的冲突，现在给该国的安全以及王储雄心勃勃但已面临困境的改造该国依赖石油的经济的计划带来了新的挑战。\n\n周六，一枚胡塞导弹击中了沙特首都的国际机场，造成至少 12 人死亡，数百人受伤，其中许多人伤势严重。这是伊朗战争相关冲突中，针对美国或其海湾盟友造成的单次伤亡最严重的袭击。\n\n据知情官员透露，一名 28 岁的埃及空姐在手臂被炸断后失血过多而死亡，她是袭击事件中失去肢体的一些受害者之一。\n\n据熟悉情况的人士称，两座航站楼遭到袭击，屋顶部分坍塌。Storyful（一家视频验证机构）验证的视频显示，乘客在机场疏散时，拖着行李箱，从地面上一道血迹旁经过。Storyful 与《华尔街日报》一样，都由道琼斯公司所有。当局报告称，周日，关闭的机场再次遭到袭击，称一枚弹药在 4 号航站楼引发火灾，并造成人员受伤。\n\n就在今年夏天，沙特官员曾向记者表示，胡塞武装的情况已经得到控制。但武装分子日益激烈的袭击，他们声称这是对沙特采取的行动的回应，袭击了机场和能源设施——包括该国的主要东西部管道和炼油厂——表明南部边境的威胁正在增加。\n\n这场冲突正在危及沙特将旅游业发展成为经济支柱的努力，包括一项计划于本月底举行的备受瞩目的国际投资会议，而该会议能否如期举行如今越来越成疑。\n\n战争的成本已经扩大了预算赤字，并有可能导致经济萎缩。\n\n王储如何应对这次考验，将对沙特王国以外的地区产生影响。沙特是全球科技产业和人工智能项目的重要资金来源，同时，它还为美国军队提供基地和空域通行权。\n\n特朗普在周末告诉记者，美国正在与胡塞武装进行沟通，但可能会加入沙特对该武装组织的打击。\n\n周日，一名胡塞武装的军事发言人重申，沙特机场是该组织的袭击目标。沙特官员表示，他们的拦截导弹库存告急，难以防御进一步袭击。\n\n对利雅得的袭击——该市距离也门胡塞武装控制区约 500 英里——已将沙特首都的部分地区变成了战场，居民们在爆炸声中四处躲避。周四，一枚被拦截的弹道导弹的碎片击中了一所幼儿园和一座医疗诊所综合楼。该市金融区的工人、一些国际学校的学生和外交官被要求撤离或寻找避难所。\n\n据熟悉王储动向的官员透露，这位 41 岁的王储正在穿梭于配备有防空洞的宫殿之间，并由一支精选的安保团队保护。他最后一次公开露面是在周二。\n\n克里斯蒂安·库茨·乌尔里克森表示，这位王储几乎没有好的选项；他是波斯湾问题专家，同时也是莱斯大学贝克研究所的研究员。“任何军事行动都有可能使沙特更深入地卷入一场不对称的冲突。”\n\n沙特新闻官员没有回应置评请求。\n\n在过去十年中，这位王储投入了大量资金，用于一系列具有未来感的项目，这些项目具有极其复杂的工程要求，旨在展示沙特王国的现代化进程，并吸引投资和旅游。这些项目包括未来之城 Neom、位于利雅得的一座巨大的立方体建筑以及一座沙漠滑雪胜地。总成本高达数万亿美元——对年度预算仅约 3500 亿美元的政府而言可谓野心勃勃。\n\n与伊朗的战争所带来的成本和经济损失，使得原本就不太可能实现的目标更加难以实现。\n\n今年前两个季度，外国投资有所下降，并且远低于王储设定的 2030 年目标。不断上升的国防和基础设施成本可能会挤占用于推动经济多元化的资金。即使是沙特王国努力成为人工智能中心，现在也面临新的风险，数据中心正逐渐成为袭击目标。\n\n许多由王储推动的项目都已停滞或正在接受审查。\n\n根据凯投宏观的分析，该国经济中非石油领域的规模在第二季度低于第一季度。沙特财政部现在预计，2026年国内生产总值将下降3.6%。\n\n今年年初，该国设法将石油通过管道输送到红海，以摆脱伊朗对霍尔木兹海峡的控制。但在7月，胡塞武装宣布对沙特阿拉伯实施海上封锁，使红海航运面临风险。9月，对东西管道的袭击直接威胁到该国从霍尔木兹海峡逃离的主要通道。\n\n2015年，王储使沙特阿拉伯卷入了也门内战，支持该国得到国际承认的政府，与胡塞武装作战。沙特阿拉伯发动了空袭，加剧了冲突，但也招致了胡塞武装对其石油设施和包括利雅得在内的城市发动的袭击。双方陷入僵局，并在2022年达成了停火协议。\n\n新一轮冲突爆发之际，王储正努力控制与伊朗的对抗。战争初期，德黑兰发动的袭击表明，海湾地区的能源设施和机场等经济资产面临风险。据了解沙特阿拉伯内部情况的人士称，沙特阿拉伯也在努力在广阔的国土上部署防空系统，并正在调动防空部队，以保护宫殿和重要的能源基础设施。这些人士表示，虽然王储支持继续对伊朗施加经济压力，但他也在指示他的顾问，要与伊朗保持外交沟通渠道畅通，并明确表示该国的意图并非对抗。\n\n这些人士称，该国已向其军事盟友巴基斯坦寻求援助，并正在全球范围内寻找防空系统。他们还表示，该国也在寻求招募外国战士，以帮助其在也门的盟友。",
  "context_cn": "王储 2015 年主导沙特介入也门内战，支持获国际承认的政府打击胡塞武装，双方 2022 年停火。本轮冲突中，胡塞 7 月宣布对沙特实施海上封锁，9 月袭击了沙特绕行霍尔木兹海峡的东西输油管道，周六的导弹袭击造成利雅得国际机场至少 12 人死亡。王储过去十年投入数万亿美元建设 Neom 等未来项目，而政府年度预算仅约 3500 亿美元；沙特财政部预计 2026 年 GDP 将收缩 3.6%。",
  "context_attr": "原文有"
 },
 {
  "id": "E2-002",
  "page": "A1",
  "section": "News",
  "title": "Video Cameras Are Hacked for War",
  "dek": "Combatants hijack vulnerable devices to track, surveil and attack the enemy",
  "byline": "Daniel Michaels, Anat Peled and Milàn Czerny",
  "body_en": "As U.S. and Israeli forces bombed Iran early this year, a poultry farmer in northern Israel decided to check on his chickens. When he launched the app on his phone for the webcam outside his coop, he was alarmed to see that its language had been switched to Arabic.\n\nHe contacted Israel's National Cyber Directorate, which found that hackers had taken control of the Chinese-made camera.\n\nThey weren't interested in chickens. The camera's orientation on the coop offered it a line of sight to the movements of Israeli security forces along the nearby border with Lebanon.\n\nThe cyber directorate said it helped the farmer harden\n\n[Continued on page A10.]\n\nthe camera's security and adjust its viewing angle to stop capturing military operations.\n\nThe farmer's webcam was one of an estimated 1.2 billion internet-connected cameras worldwide, according to cybersecurity consultants S-RM.\n\nThe devices have become a background fixture of daily life in the form of security systems, doorbells, traffic controls and even baby monitors—and potentially any of them are vulnerable to snooping, say cybersecurity experts and hackers.\n\n“We are now seeing the conversion of civilian surveillance into wartime intelligence collection,” said Alexander Leslie, a national-security expert at threat-intelligence consultants Recorded Future, who sees a global “soft underbelly” in omnipresent digital eyeballs.\n\nThe Wall Street Journal spoke with intelligence agencies, cybersecurity experts, military officials and hackers to scope out the extent and impact of the invasive surveillance. The Journal also reviewed dozens of hours of footage and hundreds of images, much of it made public by hackers, and independently verified their authenticity, by geolocating their locations and checking for any signs of digital manipulations.\n\nHidden gems\n\nIntelligence agencies scour the internet for vulnerable cameras and robotically tap in to them by testing millions of passwords. Now, AI has made it even easier to hack into them and analyze their data at scale, transforming cameras that were designed to boost safety into tools for combatants to track, surveil and attack the enemy—often surpassing the abilities of human intelligence analysts.\n\nAlgorithms digest endless reams of footage to find hidden gems and patterns for agents to monitor.\n\n“AI changed everything,” said Kristopher Kortright, leader of Team OneFist, an international hacker group supporting Ukraine in its war with Russia.\n\nRussia and Ukraine have ramped up camera-hacking in their war, aiming to spy on each other, find new targets and assess damage from their attacks. Israel, long a master of digital spycraft, hacked into Iranian traffic cameras to help forces collect intelligence used in the assassination of Iranian Supreme Leader Ali Khamenei earlier this year. Iran and its proxies have struck back by breaking into hundreds of Israeli webcams and security cameras that Israeli officials consider sensitive to national security, like the one on the chicken coop.\n\nCondo association\n\n“They can't look through binoculars” to see Israel from Iran, said Eliav Noori, a cybersecurity expert at Israel's cyber directorate. “So they take advantage of cameras that are exposed to the internet and turn them into lookout points.”\n\nIn 2024, Russian hackers commandeered residential webcams in Kyiv, including one installed by a condominium association to monitor the neighborhood.\n\nUkraine's security service, the SBU, said that Russia intended to use the hacked video feeds to find targets and “to adjust missile attacks on Ukraine,” refining their aim for subsequent strikes. The SBU said it has identified and secured, reoriented or blocked roughly 40,000 video cameras that posed potential security risks since Russia's 2022 invasion.\n\nRussia's efforts extend beyond the war zone. Dutch intelligence agencies warned in July that “Russian state actors are systematically conducting digital espionage operations” by hacking into cameras across Europe. The operations aim to collect data on military transport routes, arms deliveries to Ukraine and the whereabouts of Ukrainian military personnel, the agencies said.\n\nCybersecurity consultants CloudSEK recently discovered an exposed server they suspect belongs to a Russian-speaking hacker that contained hundreds of webcam images of Ukrainian facilities involved in defense, government, energy and communications, plus assorted views of daily life. CloudSEK said many of the images came from locations near Ukraine's borders with NATO ally Romania.\n\nWar with Iran\n\nWebcams are also playing an increasingly important role in the war with Iran. An Iran-linked group called Moses Staff hacked into a Jerusalem security camera to monitor the 2022 bus stop bombings by a Palestinian militant that killed two people and injured more than 20 others, Israel's cyber directorate said.\n\nThis year, Iranian-linked groups have hacked into more than 250 sensitive Israeli security cameras in incidents that pose immediate danger to national security, according to Israel's cyber directorate, more than in previous years. Compromised cameras include license-plate readers and security cameras near strategic sites and places where soldiers gather, including military outposts near the border with Lebanon.\n\nThe total number of cameras breached in Israel since the start of the Iran war is far higher, the cyber directorate said, adding that out of hundreds of thousands of cameras connected to the internet there, tens of thousands have vulnerabilities that Iranian hackers could take advantage of like default passwords.\n\nHacked cameras are taking over roles in targeting, damage assessments and other combat intelligence-gathering that were previously supplied by aerial reconnaissance or on-the-ground spies. Penetrating a well-placed camera can get analysts closer to targets and carries less risk than infiltrating the canniest secret agent.\n\nRestricted areas\n\nVideo cameras operate silently, often in restricted areas. Ukraine's penetration of Russian camera feeds from unreachable locations have helped Kyiv strike Moscow's troops and infrastructure.\n\nEarly in the Ukraine war, Team OneFist watched feeds from cameras on Russia's Kerch Strait Bridge to Crimea, providing images that helped refine attack plans—including the fact that Russia had installed motion detectors underneath the bridge to thwart waterborne attacks.\n\nInformation from hacked cameras “can shorten decision cycles in exactly the way modern militaries care about,” said Recorded Future's Leslie.\n\nWhile private individuals and enterprise-network managers are increasingly attuned to cybersecurity, web-connected devices like cameras may have fewer built-in protections and not be consistently monitored, patched or updated, said Xu Zou, senior vice president for cloud security services at cybersecurity company Palo Alto Networks.\n\nInformation-security specialists inside companies and governments often aren't aware of connected cameras, which are frequently installed and operated by facilities managers or other non-IT teams. “Those teams have very little cybersecurity awareness and knowledge. Their job is to mount that camera, power it on, and walk away,” said Zou.\n\nInformation warfare\n\nPalo Alto found last year that the vast majority of weakly secured internet-connected devices are webcams and digital video recorders. The company also found that video-streaming and video-conferencing equipment—ubiquitous in office buildings around the globe—are particularly vulnerable. That has made them useful for information warfare, not just military operations, said Ari Ben Am, an adjunct fellow at the Center on Cyber and Technology Innovation at the Foundation for Defense of Democracies, a Washington think tank.\n\nAnti-Israeli groups, he said, use imagery from hacked cameras in propaganda videos and content. “For them it is about playing up their intelligence superiority,” he said.\n\nIranian-linked group Handala taunts Israelis by boasting that it can see and hear them close to home. “Have a message? Find the nearest traffic camera and talk,” Handala wrote last year on one of its websites, which has since been taken down.\n\nIn Russia, officials from the Ministry of Industry and Trade were grousing on a videoconference call earlier this year about their reliance on Chinese drone components when an unexpected voice joined in. “Colleagues, can you hear me?” the voice asked. Yes, replied the meeting leader. The intruder then asked a seemingly technical question that quickly turned obscene. “Turn it off,” the meeting leader told his colleagues, in a video later posted online by Ukrainian hackers.\n\n‘Internet of things’\n\nVulnerable accessories linked into a network are among hackers' prime targets, expanding what technicians call the attack surface. Often, cameras aren't the target, but serve as the weak link in a larger hacking attempt. Ransomware group Akira tapped an unprotected webcam to get around one organization's network-security systems and extort it, S-RM reported last year. Once hackers are inside a local network, they can usually probe stored files, email accounts and other devices, including cameras.\n\n“The Internet of Things is such a wild area that everything—whether you realize it or not—is an extension of your attack surface,” said Paul Caron, S-RM head of cyber for the Americas.",
  "priority": "P1",
