  "title_cn": "预测市场投注蔓延至陪审团裁决",
  "dek_cn": null,
  "body_cn": "在林赛·克兰西的夏季谋杀案审判期间，投注者对判决结果下了超过 10 万美元的赌注。由于陪审团僵持不下，案件于 9 月初被宣告审判无效，此后人们又下了超过 50 万美元的赌注，赌的是这位被指控杀害自己孩子的马萨诸塞州女性最终是否会被判有罪。\n\n在犹他州，泰勒·罗宾逊的审判日期尚未确定，他被指控去年在一次大学活动中杀害了保守派评论员查理·柯克。但在罗宾逊上个月正式提出无罪辩护之前，人们就已经下了超过 30 万美元的赌注，赌的是案件的结果。\n\n在这两种情况下，这些赌注都可以在 Polymarket 运营的一个境外平台上进行。该网站还提供关于联邦政府起诉肖恩·“迪迪”·库姆斯以及政府目前对被罢免的委内瑞拉总统尼古拉斯·马杜罗的案件的赌注。\n\n预测市场引发了新的伦理问题和内幕交易风险，涉及从公司审计到美国军事行动的各个方面。这些问题现在已经摆到了法院门口，给司法系统带来了特别不寻常的挑战。\n\n“当公众将法庭视为赌场时，正当程序很容易受到腐败的影响，”全国刑事辩护律师协会主席 C·梅丽莎·欧文说道。\n\n最直接和最明显的问题是，陪审员可能会对自己的审议过程进行赌博，从而产生经济激励，促使他们达成特定的结果，无论案件的事实如何。\n\n一些法院工作人员也可能掌握关于审议进展情况的内幕信息。\n\n[请见 A6 页：]\n\n芝加哥大学法学教授 M·托德·亨德森表示，赌博平台的普及性和广泛性使得腐败陪审员的可能性成为一种真实的风险。\n\n他说：“但还有一个更广泛的问题，即大量赌注的预测市场可能会影响陪审员对案件的思考方式。如果人们下了数百万美元的赌注，认为被告将被判无罪，这是否会使陪审团更不愿判有罪？”\n\n“对于坐在家中的普通陪审员“Joe Q”（泛指）而言，”亨德森说，“问题是：如果他看到 Polymarket 上的赔率，如果他看到报纸或电视上有人谈论泰勒·罗宾逊是否有罪的可能性，他是否有可能产生偏见？”\n\n罗宾逊的律师向审理法官指出了 Polymarket 上的投注，并认为这种投注是“在道德上站不住脚的勾当”，把死刑诉讼变成了商品。\n\n辩方表示，在 7 月份的一次初步法庭听证会上，交易量非常活跃，并认为继续直播庭审程序会制造对罗宾逊不利的审前舆论。\n\n托尼·格拉夫法官在上个月的一份裁决中写道：“法庭认可被告关于陪审团受污染及预测市场活动的担忧是真诚的。”但他允许继续直播庭审程序，并建议可以通过陪审团的选择来解决任何担忧，包括询问潜在的陪审员是否接触过预测市场。\n\n通常，关于刑事案件的投注市场都是在离岸平台上进行的，因为预测市场公司希望避免违反美国的相关规定，这些规定禁止对敏感话题进行赌博，包括战争、恐怖主义和暗杀。\n\n尽管如此，市场观察人士表示，访问这些离岸平台并不困难。罗格斯大学的统计学教授哈里·克兰表示，大约有 20% 到 40% 的投注资金来自美国国内，用于在离岸 Polymarket 市场进行投注。克兰同时也是商品期货交易委员会的顾问委员会成员，该委员会负责监管预测市场。这项研究并未显示，这些资金是来自许多人进行小额投注，还是来自少数超级用户。\n\nPolymarket 的发言人表示，人们“对现实世界事件的结果进行交易，包括法律、政治和经济发展。这些市场不会影响结果——它们反映了市场参与者集体认为会发生什么。”\n\nPolymarket 与《华尔街日报》的出版商道琼斯公司建立了数据合作关系。\n\n至少有几家法院开始关注这个问题。位于芝加哥的美国地区法院正在更新其内幕交易政策，以禁止员工利用工作中的信息进行投注。法院发言人肖恩·刘易斯表示，该地区的法官已经注意到了可能在陪审团审判中出现类似担忧的情况。“随着技术和社会的发展，法院将继续调整其政策，以确保公正和公平地进行司法审判。”\n\n位于曼哈顿的联邦地方法院吸引了至少两个值得关注的投注市场。在去年 5 月至 7 月期间，Polymarket 的用户对肖恩·“迪迪”·库姆斯性交易案件的判决结果进行了近 79.8 万美元的投注。库姆斯被判犯有敲诈勒索罪和性交易罪不成立，但被判犯有较轻的罪行。\n\n预计明年，马杜罗将在同一法庭接受审判，他面临的指控包括与毒品恐怖主义有关的罪名。Polymarket平台上关于马杜罗是否会被判有罪的预测，目前已吸引了约13.3万美元的交易量。另一个关于他是否会被判处监禁的预测，交易量已超过90万美元。",
  "context_cn": null,
  "context_attr": null
 },
 {
  "id": "E2-010",
  "page": "A1",
  "section": "Personal Journal",
  "title": "The Sunday Scaries Are Real—And They're Getting Worse",
  "dek": "As workloads mount, people struggle to tame the pre-Monday dread spiral",
  "byline": "Ray A. Smith",
  "body_en": "Jill Davis's official work week starts Monday morning. Unofficially, it kicks off every Sunday afternoon when, like clockwork, anxious thoughts begin seeping into her brain.\n\n\"Am I organized and ready enough? Am I prepared for this meeting?\" says Davis, the chief development officer at a nonprofit. To bat the angst away, she'll often launch into work, writing emails and saving them as drafts so she doesn't disturb her colleagues. Chances are they're already fretting about work, too.\n\n\"Sunday scaries\" have become such a fixture of Americans' weekends that Merriam-Webster added the term to its online dictionary last month. Its definition: a feeling of anxiety or sadness one may feel when the weekend is almost over, either because the work/school week is about to begin, there is no more time for fun or relaxing weekend activities, or both.\n\nThat pre-Monday dread isn't a new phenomenon, but there's reason to believe it's intensifying as workloads mount and job security ebbs. More than half of U.S. workers say they experience\n\n[Continued on page A12.]\n\nthe Sunday scaries, according to an October survey of 2,100 people by job platform FlexJobs. Having a list of unfinished tasks hanging over them was the most commonly cited cause.\n\nIt's only a matter of time before the scaries ruin Saturday too, turning it into a day to dread the dread we'll feel on Sunday.\n\nFor Davis, the jitters so consumed her Sundays that she bought a $30 hourglass to rein them in. It takes an hour for the sand to run through it—the maximum time she now lets herself work, or even think about work. On a recent Sunday, “when it ran out, I got up and I left and went in the other room,” she said.\n\nEffectively treating the Sunday scaries without causing negative side effects is a delicate balancing act. It requires being busy enough to ease the stress but not the kind of busy that drags everyone down. It appears we're getting worse at that.\n\nThe share of Sundays that employees devoted to work messaging and chats was about 12% last year, compared with 6.6% in 2023, according to data compiled for The Wall Street Journal by workforce analytics firm ActivTrak.\n\n“People feel like they have to toil away harder in order to make sure they don't get fired,” said Angie Callen, an executive coach and author of “Scary Good: Discovering Life Beyond the Sunday Scaries.” Her advice? Ask yourself honestly why you are engaging with work on a Sunday. Will you get a positive return on your time? “You don't have to settle for the Sunday scaries,” said Callen. “We let Mondays ruin our Sundays. Stop. Be present in your life instead of accepting that default.”\n\nJoanna Johnston, a longtime tech executive, used to combat her Sunday scaries by getting up as early as 6 a.m. to squeeze in an hour of Monday prep work, then putting in more time in the evenings. When she sent Slack and other messages, it was usually to a peer or in response to a manager. But she didn't always adhere to the unwritten rules of weekend communication with subordinates. “I'm sure before I became more sensitive to the intrusion this caused, I did it to my direct reports and broader team as well,” said Johnston, who retired from corporate roles in June and now coaches executives. Despite the career shift, she's still working on Sundays. “I think I just have a problem,” she laughed.\n\nUntil a few years ago, Chris Keller ran his own marketing agency and often worked seven days a week. Now in a corporate role, he limits himself to two hours on early Sunday mornings to prepare for the week. Otherwise, “I know it could get away from me really quickly,” said Keller, marketing chief at a credit union. Keller said he will typically use the time to dictate notes to a transcription app to prepare for meetings. “If I don't get ideas and thoughts out, I'll not be able to enjoy the other things that I'm supposed to be doing on the weekends,” he said.\n\nDo psychiatrists approve? If doing some work on Sunday will relieve you of feeling behind or overwhelmed on Monday, it's OK, just set guardrails, said Kia-Rai Prewitt, a psychiatrist with Cleveland Clinic's Behavioral Health. “If I can just log in really quick and respond to this email, that's all I'm going to do,” she said. “You have to set the boundary and say, ‘I'm not going to respond to other emails.’”\n\nOthers recommend exhausting all options to avoid work altogether. Consider spending a little more time at work on Friday “so you can officially be done” or getting to work a little earlier on Monday “so that you're not going to be behind the eight ball,” said Amy Cooper Hakim, an industrial organizational psychologist. “There's always a way to shift those same extra minutes.”\n\nIf all else fails, try a rebrand. Davis, the Sunday afternoon worrier, worked with an executive coach who helped her reimagine the “Sunday scaries” as “Monday maybe.” The idea is to approach Monday with curiosity rather than thinking everything has to be buttoned-up and perfect by then. “I'm trying to release the pressure of what's so special about everything getting done before Monday,” she said. “I sleep better on Sunday nights.”",
  "priority": "P1",
  "type": "article",
  "terms": [
   {
