  "final_id": "008",
  "title_cn": "“周日恐慌”真实存在——而且愈演愈烈",
  "dek_cn": "随着工作量增加、职业保障减弱，人们越来越难遏制周一前的恐惧螺旋",
  "body_cn": "吉尔·戴维斯的工作周正式从周一早上开始。但实际上，从每个周日下午开始，她就会感到焦虑，各种不安的想法开始涌上心头，就像一个定时的闹钟。\n\n“我是否已经做好充分的准备，并且有条不紊？我是否已经为这次会议做好了准备？”戴维斯说道。她是一家非营利组织的首席发展官。为了驱散这种焦虑，她经常开始工作，写邮件并将其保存为草稿，这样就不会打扰她的同事。很可能，她的同事们也已经开始为工作感到焦虑。\n\n“周日恐慌”已经成为美国人周末生活中不可或缺的一部分，以至于《韦氏词典》上个月将其添加到在线词典中。它的定义是：一种焦虑或悲伤的感觉，当周末即将结束时，人们可能会感到这种感觉，原因可能是工作/学习周即将开始，或者没有更多的时间进行有趣的或放松的周末活动，或者两者兼而有之。\n\n这种对周一的恐惧并非一种新的现象，但有理由相信，随着工作量的增加和工作保障的减少，这种恐惧正在加剧。根据招聘平台FlexJobs对2100人进行的10月份调查，超过一半的美国工人表示他们会经历“周日恐慌”，而最常见的诱因是未完成的任务清单。\n\n很快，这种焦虑感就会蔓延到周六，使周六也变成了一个令人感到焦虑的日子，因为人们会开始为周日将要感受到的焦虑而感到不安。\n\n对于戴维斯来说，这种焦虑感已经严重影响了她的周日，以至于她买了一个30美元的沙漏来控制这种情绪。沙子流完需要一个小时——这是她现在允许自己工作或甚至思考工作的时间上限。在一个最近的周日，“当沙子流完时，我站起来，离开了房间，去了另一个房间，”她说。\n\n在不产生负面副作用的情况下，有效地缓解“周日恐慌”是一项微妙的平衡艺术。它需要人们保持一定的忙碌程度，以减轻压力，但不能是那种会拖累所有人的忙碌。看来我们在这方面做得越来越差。\n\n根据劳动力分析公司ActivTrak为《华尔街日报》提供的数据，去年员工用于工作消息和聊天的时间占周日的约12%，而2023年为6.6%。\n\n“人们觉得他们必须更加努力地工作，以确保自己不会被解雇，”《Scary Good：发现摆脱周日恐慌的生活》一书的作者、一位高管教练安吉·卡伦说道。她的建议是什么？诚实地问自己，为什么要在周日处理工作。这样做是否能带来积极的回报？“你不必屈服于周日恐慌，”卡伦说。“我们让星期一毁掉了我们的星期天。停止。与其接受这种默认状态，不如活在当下。”\n\n一位在科技行业工作多年的高管乔安娜·约翰斯顿过去会通过早上 6 点起床，挤出一个小时的时间来为星期一做准备，然后在晚上再投入更多时间，以此来对抗她的周日恐慌。当她发送 Slack 和其他消息时，通常是发给同事或回复经理。但她并不总是遵守与下属进行周末沟通的不成文规定。“我确信，在我变得更加敏感，意识到这种行为会造成干扰之前，我也曾对我的直接下属和更广泛的团队这样做，”约翰斯顿说。她于 6 月从公司职位退休，现在担任高管教练。尽管职业生涯发生了转变，她仍然在周日工作。“我想我只是有问题，”她笑着说。\n\n几年前，克里斯·凯勒经营自己的营销公司，经常一周工作七天。现在，他担任公司职位，他将自己限制在周日清晨的两个小时内，为新的一周做准备。否则，“我知道情况会迅速失控，”凯勒说，他是信用合作社的营销主管。凯勒说，他通常会利用这段时间，将笔记口述给转录应用程序，为会议做准备。“如果我不把想法和思路表达出来，我就无法享受周末的其他活动，”他说。\n\n精神科医生是否认可这种做法？如果周日做一些工作可以减轻你在周一的焦虑或不知所措的感觉，那么这样做是可以的，但要设定一些限制，克利夫兰诊所行为健康部门的精神科医生基娅-拉伊·普雷维特说。“如果我能快速登录并回复这封电子邮件，那就是我所要做的，”她说。“你必须设定界限，并说：‘我不会回复其他电子邮件。’”\n\n其他人建议尝试所有方法，以避免完全进行工作。考虑在周五多花一些时间在工作上，“这样你就可以正式完成工作”，或者周一早点到公司，“以免一开局就处于被动”，工业组织心理学家艾米·库珀·哈基姆说。“总有一种方法可以调整这些额外的时间。”\n\n如果所有方法都失败了，可以尝试重新定义。吉尔·戴维斯，这位经常在周日下午感到焦虑的人，与一位高管教练合作，帮助她将“周日恐慌”重新定义为“也许是周一”。这个想法是，要以好奇的态度来迎接周一，而不是认为一切都必须在那时完成并达到完美。“我试图减轻那种压力，即一切都必须在周一之前完成，”她说。“我周日晚上睡得更好。”",
  "context_cn": "招聘平台 FlexJobs 10 月对 2100 人的调查显示，超过半数美国员工会经历“周日恐慌”，最常见的原因是未完成的工作清单。劳动力分析公司 ActivTrak 的数据显示，员工周日用于工作消息和聊天的时间占比从 2023 年的 6.6% 升至去年的约 12%。精神科医生建议设定界限（如只回一封邮件），组织心理学家则建议把额外工作挪到周五或周一清晨。",
  "context_attr": "原文有"
 },
 {
  "id": "E2-001",
  "page": "A1",
  "section": "News",
  "title": "Americans' Savings Help Fuel Private Credit",
  "dek": "and helped power the rise of their private lending to risky companies",
  "byline": "Heather Gillers",
  "body_en": "When Sabrina Elliott inherited some money, her son-in-law helped her invest $40,000 in a product that dangled the prospect of stock-market gains with no risk of loss.\n\nIn exchange for locking up her money for 12 years, it promised returns tied to the market's performance. One catch: The company behind the product, Aviva, could periodically lower the portion of market gains Elliott received.\n\nThat happened twice after Aviva was purchased by Athene, an insurer backed by private-asset giant Apollo Global Management. By the time the policy ended in late 2023, her annualized return had come to 3.7%.\n\n\"That's one of the downfalls of the indexed annuities,\" said Carlos Dias Jr., Elliott's son-in-law and a financial adviser outside Orlando, Fla. \"Insurance companies can change the amount that's received from the particular index every year, every two years.\"\n\nWall Street behemoths like Apollo and Brookfield are just now starting to find ways to tap in to the vast sums of money in 401(k) accounts, pitching their alternative investments as another way for people to grow their nest eggs.\n\nBut private-asset managers have already been vacuuming up the savings of American households through indexed annuities, which have surged in popularity over the past decade\n\n[Continued on page A10.]\n\nThe annuity industry recently got a black mark after Delaware Life, an insurer controlled by Dodgers owner Mark Walter, disclosed it was under federal investigation for failing to report loans to other entities he owned.\n\nIndexed annuities are meant to appeal to older people who may be on fixed incomes and have little appetite for risk, but still want some exposure to stock-market gains. People turn over $50,000 or $100,000 worth of savings for a decade or so. Insurers guarantee that their investment return will never fall below zero, with the potential for better returns than a CD or savings account.\n\nExactly how much a person's money grows is based on terms the insurer can change during the life of policy, such as the share of the deposit that can earn market returns, any cap on returns and any spread subtracted from those returns.\n\nIn the first year of Elliott's policy, 60% of her money earned returns tied to the S&P 500, minus a 2.9-percentage-point spread. In the policy's seventh year, Athene lowered that share to 55%, then to 45% in the 11th year. The rest of the money grew at a fixed rate of 1%.\n\nElliott, a 69-year-old professor of nutrition in the Florida university system, had paid for add-ons that let her choose to parcel out payments over her lifetime or to her heirs. That reduced her annual return by about 2 percentage points.\n\nThe 3.7% annualized rate she earned over the 12-year life of her annuity beat the 1.9% she would have gotten from Treasurys. But it paled next to the annualized return of 13% on the S&P 500.\n\nAthene, which Apollo fully took over in 2022, said in a statement that the annuity “performed exceptionally well, earning returns well in excess of similar safe yield alternatives, guaranteeing 100% principal protection, and providing optional enhanced death benefits and guaranteed lifetime income.”\n\nThe company said “it is highly misleading to measure the performance of fixed indexed annuities using a scenario that pairs historically low rates with a strong bull equity market.”\n\nProducts tied to the stock market are typically overseen by the Securities and Exchange Commission, and selling them often requires a securities license. But indexed annuities can also be sold by state insurance agents, who aren't subject to the same rules on disclosing conflicts of interest or acting in a client's best interest. Indexed annuities are often among the most lucrative products these agents can sell.\n\nAmerican Equity, for instance, pays agents a commission of 5% to 7% of a policyholder's deposit on some 10-year indexed annuities. That is compared with around 3% for simpler annuities with fixed rates sold by the Brookfield-owned insurer.\n\nInsurers say the higher commissions reflect the complexity of the products, which require greater explanation. Still, consumer advocates say indexed annuities can be a magnet for misleading sales pitches.\n\nEducational materials for an indexed annuity sold by Delaware Life, for instance, show a premium of $100,000 growing to $2.7 million over the next 21 years. But the “Momentum Growth” annuity is tied to a stock-and-crypto index established just last year. The returns advertised draw on hypothetical data based on the past 15 years—a period that included 11 years with double-digit stock-market gains.\n\nAfter the financial crisis, private-asset managers began buying up insurers to access their huge pools of policyholder premiums. Indexed annuities were particularly attractive. Payouts follow a relatively predictable timetable, and the policies typically last about a decade, compared with about three to five years for fixed-rate annuities. That gives asset managers flexibility to put the money into riskier, less-liquid private-credit investments like collateralized loan obligations or asset-backed securities.\n\nIf their investments do poorly, the insurer can cushion against loss by lowering the caps on policyholder gains, and if their investments do well they can fatten their profits the same way, said Larry Rybka, chief executive of Valmark Financial Group, which sells life insurance, annuities and investment products. “That's why private equity likes this, because they can change the deal on the consumer,” Rybka said.",
  "priority": "P1",
  "type": "article",
