  "final_id": "009",
  "title_cn": "美国人的储蓄助燃私人信贷",
  "dek_cn": "并推动了私募机构向风险企业放贷业务的增长",
  "body_cn": "当萨布丽娜·埃利奥特继承了一些钱时，她的女婿帮助她投资了 4 万美元，购买了一种产品，该产品承诺在没有损失风险的情况下，让她的投资与股市收益挂钩。\n\n作为回报，她需要将资金锁定 12 年，该产品承诺收益与市场表现挂钩。但有一个限制：提供该产品的公司英杰华保险，可能会定期降低埃利奥特获得的股市收益比例。\n\n在英杰华保险被阿波罗全球管理公司支持的保险公司 Athene 收购后，这种情况发生了两次。到 2023 年底该保单到期时，她的年化收益率为 3.7%。\n\n“这是指数型年金的一个缺点，”埃利奥特的女婿、位于佛罗里达州奥兰多市的理财顾问小卡洛斯·迪亚斯说道，“保险公司可以每年或每两年更改从特定指数中获得的收益金额。”\n\n华尔街巨头，如阿波罗和博枫，现在才开始寻找方法，以利用 401(k) 退休储蓄计划中庞大的资金，并将他们的另类投资作为人们增加财富的另一种方式进行推广。\n\n但私人资产管理公司已经通过指数型年金，吸走了美国家庭的储蓄，而指数型年金在过去十年中越来越受欢迎。\n\n[请参见 A10 页]\n\n特拉华人寿最近因受到负面评价，该公司是一家由道奇队老板马克·沃尔特控制的保险公司，该公司披露，由于未能报告其向其拥有的其他实体提供的贷款，该公司正在接受联邦调查。\n\n指数型年金旨在吸引那些可能拥有固定收入，且风险承受能力较低，但仍然希望获得一定程度的股市收益的老年人。人们将价值 5 万美元或 10 万美元的储蓄投入其中，期限约为十年。保险公司保证他们的投资收益永远不会低于零，并且有可能获得比定期存款或储蓄账户更高的收益。\n\n具体而言，个人的资金增长多少，取决于保险公司在保险期间可以更改的条款，例如可以获得市场收益的存款比例、收益上限以及从收益中扣除的任何差额。\n\n在埃利奥特购买保险的第一年，她的资金有60%的收益与标准普尔500指数挂钩，扣除2.9个百分点的差额。在保险的第七年，Athene将这一比例降低到55%，然后在第11年降低到45%。剩余的资金以1%的固定利率增长。\n\n69岁的埃利奥特是佛罗里达州立大学系统的一名营养学教授。她购买了附加险，可以选择在她的整个生命周期内或在她去世后将资金分配给她的继承人。这使她的年收益减少了约2个百分点。\n\n她在12年保险期内获得的年化收益率为3.7%，高于她若投资美国国债本可获得的1.9%。但与标准普尔500指数的年化收益率13%相比，这个收益率显得微不足道。\n\nAthene在2022年被阿波罗完全收购。该公司在一份声明中表示，该保险“表现出色，收益远高于类似的安全收益替代品，保证100%的本金保护，并提供可选的额外死亡赔偿和保证终身收入。”\n\n该公司表示，“使用一种将历史低利率与强劲的牛市相结合的情景来衡量固定指数型年金的表现，具有高度误导性。”\n\n与股市挂钩的产品通常由美国证券交易委员会监管，销售这些产品通常需要证券许可证。但指数型年金也可以由州保险代理人销售，这些代理人不受关于披露利益冲突或以客户的最佳利益行事的相同规则的约束。指数型年金通常是这些代理人可以销售的最有利可图的产品之一。\n\n例如，美国公平人寿公司为某些10年期指数型年金的保单持有人支付的佣金为保单存款的5%至7%。相比之下，博枫旗下的保险公司销售的简单固定利率年金的佣金约为3%。\n\n保险公司表示，较高的佣金反映了这些产品的复杂性，需要更详细的解释。尽管如此，消费者权益倡导者表示，指数型年金可能成为具有误导性的销售宣传的诱饵。\n\n例如，特拉华人寿公司销售的一种指数型年金的宣传材料显示，10万美元的保费在未来21年内增长到270万美元。但这种名为“动量增长”的年金与一个去年才成立的股票和加密货币指数挂钩。宣传的收益基于过去15年的假设数据——这一时期包括11年股市实现两位数增长。\n\n在金融危机之后，私人资产管理公司开始收购保险公司，以获取其庞大的保单持有人保费。指数型年金尤其具有吸引力。支付时间表相对可预测，而且这些保单通常持续约十年，而固定利率年金的期限约为三到五年。这使资产管理公司能够灵活地将资金投入到风险更高、流动性较差的私人信贷投资中，例如担保贷款凭证或资产支持证券。\n\nValmark金融集团首席执行官拉里·里布卡表示，该公司销售人寿保险、年金和投资产品。他说，如果投资表现不佳，保险公司可以通过降低保单持有者收益上限来减少损失；如果投资表现良好，它们也可以通过同样的方式增加利润。“这就是私募股权公司喜欢这种方式的原因，因为它们可以改变与消费者的协议。”",
  "context_cn": "指数型年金锁定资金约 12 年，承诺收益与股市挂钩、本金不亏，但保险公司可定期下调客户能拿到的收益分成、上限和利差。销售佣金高达保单存款的 5%–7%，远高于普通固定年金的约 3%。私募巨头收购保险公司后，可将保费池投向担保贷款凭证等流动性差的私人信贷资产；投资不佳时就下调保户收益上限来缓冲。",
  "context_attr": "原文有"
 },
 {
  "id": "E1-010",
  "page": "A2",
  "section": "U.S. News",
  "title": "Why Higher Bond Yields Aren’t Going Away",
  "dek": null,
  "byline": "THE OUTLOOK | By Matt Grossman and Anthony DeBarros",
  "body_en": "Some economic shocks look like bumps in the road—tariffs, to which businesses adjust, or a war with Iran that raises oil prices temporarily. But economists increasingly believe that higher U.S. interest rates will define the rest of the decade.\n\nSo concludes The Wall Street Journal's latest survey of forecasters. The surveyed analysts don't see the recent surge in long-term yields to quarter-century highs as a temporary blip that will fade as inflation cools. Instead, they expect higher long-term yields to be sustained.\n\nTreasurys set the tempo for borrowing costs across the economy. Higher yields mean higher mortgage rates and more expensive borrowing for companies. They also increase the burden on the federal budget, where interest on the national debt now exceeds military spending.\n\nThe 71 analysts who submitted projections in the survey from Oct. 2-7 now expect the benchmark 10-year yield to end 2026 at 5.1%, up from 4.4% the last time we asked, in July. Not all respondents answered every question.\n\nHigher yields are expected to persist. Forecasters see a 10-year yield of 4.96% halfway through 2027 and 4.78% at the end of 2027, up from 4.3% and 4.26% in July projections.\n\nA mix of improving growth, inflation and concerns about the U.S. fiscal trajectory have pushed bond yields higher since the start of the summer. Many economists said the cocktail has forced them to re-evaluate assumptions about demand for Treasurys, whose yields rise when their prices fall.\n\n\"It has been a sea change in the fundamentals underlying the fixed-income market and the economy,\" Allen Sinai, president of consulting firm Decision Economics, said in an interview.\n\nHe said even after the Federal Reserve's interest-rate increase last month, the central bank has fallen behind in the battle to tame inflation. Sinai said it would take more than one hike to smother inflation, which the Fed appeared to acknowledge with its September projections. Those showed the Fed's short-term interest-rate target, the federal-funds rate, topping 4% this year and staying there through 2027.\n\nSinai forecasts the 10-year yield could rise to nearly 6% next year and remain above 5% through the 2020s. Yields on Treasury securities are heavily influenced by investors' expectations for how the Fed will set interest rates in coming years.\n\nMuch of the recent increase in inflation is because of higher oil prices, which economists expect to recede: They forecast the U.S. oil-price benchmark will end the year at $88 a barrel, compared with $91.85 on Friday, and slip below $80 by July.\n\nAs a result, they expect inflation as measured by the consumer-price index of 3.5% in December, 2.6% next June, and 2.3% at the end of 2027. That has changed little from three months ago. The inflation gauge that the Fed tracks, the personal-consumption expenditures price index, is forecast to cool to 2.4% by the end of 2027, from 3.5% at the end of this year.\n\nCarl Tannenbaum, chief economist at Chicago-based Northern Trust, said factors beyond the inflation outlook have pushed up expected yields. Inflationary pressure from the Iran war could abate if the conflict winds down, which would help ease yields, all else equal, he said. But investors will remain nervous about the large and growing debt burden, while the AI infrastructure build-out could lift growth expectations and demand for capital, Tannenbaum said. He sees the 10-year yield around 5.2% through 2028, before falling to 5% by the end of 2029.\n\nYields at that level would strain the federal budget. In February, the Congressional Budget Office projected the 10-year Treasury yield won't exceed 4.3% for the rest of the decade. The White House Office of Management and Budget is even more optimistic, projecting yields falling into the mid-3% range later this decade.\n\nEconomists penciled in higher expected growth this year. They expect GDP to increase by 2.4% in 2026, up from their 2.1% July projection. That reflects upward revisions to second-quarter growth and interim data suggesting the economy might have grown by an annualized rate of more than 3% in the third quarter. Respondents see growth slipping back thereafter, to 2.1% or 2.2% for the rest of the decade.\n\nLikewise, forecasters don't anticipate much drama in the labor market. On average, they think unemployment will hang around 4.2% through 2029, little changed from the 4.2% unemployment rate in September.\n\nThey put the probability of recession in the coming 12 months at 24%, barely budged from 25% in July.\n\nNot all forecasters agree yields will stay high. Rajeev Dhawan, an economist at Georgia State University, thinks the labor market is more fragile than it appears.\n\nOver the past six months, the economy has added a solid 66,000 jobs a month on average, but few have been in the most lucrative fields, he said.\n\nIn Dhawan's view, a weaker labor market could force the Fed to bring rates lower again, which could pull down yields. \"The labor market's got a white-collar job malaise,\" Dhawan said. \"Once the Fed realizes it isn't as strong, they will pivot to rate cuts.\"\n\n[Graphics with the article: (1) \"Probability the U.S. is in a recession in next 12 months, including today,\" 2015-2026; (2) \"GDP (annualized growth)\" with October '26 forecast vs July '26 forecast, Q3 2026-Q3 2027; (3) \"Closing yield, 10-year Treasury notes\" with October '26, July '26 and April '26 forecasts, Dec. 31 2026-Dec. 31 2029. Note: Recession probability is an average of economists' answers. GDP forecasts are for inflation-adjusted GDP. Source: Wall Street Journal surveys of economists.]",
  "priority": "P2",
  "type": "article",
