   }
  ],
  "final_id": "018",
  "title_cn": "“伊萨亚斯”飓风过后",
  "dek_cn": null,
  "body_cn": "周六，在佛罗里达州米尔顿，伊斯兰·马哈福兹在“伊萨亚斯”飓风过后，从他被毁的小型便利店中抢救出一些物品。这场风暴是大西洋飓风季的第一场飓风。（照片：杰拉尔德·赫伯特/美联社）",
  "context_cn": null,
  "context_attr": null
 },
 {
  "id": "E1-019",
  "page": "A3",
  "section": "U.S. News",
  "title": "Newsom Backs Bid To Counter Billionaire Tax Effort",
  "dek": null,
  "byline": "BY PAUL KIERNAN",
  "body_en": "California Gov. Gavin Newsom has opposed a high-profile ballot measure that would tax billionaires' wealth. Now he is endorsing a different measure aimed at prohibiting wealth taxes.\n\nNewsom announced his endorsement Friday via the campaign backing the antitax measure, known as Proposition 42. \"Californians deserve to have their retirement and life savings protected from new state taxes,\" Newsom said.\n\nProp. 42 has raised tens of millions of dollars from a political committee funded in part by billionaires, including Google co-founder Sergey Brin. This effort took off after a healthcare-workers' union proposed a one-time 5% tax on billionaires' wealth. Voters are set to decide on both ballot measures on Nov. 3.\n\nThe proposed billionaire tax, aimed at offsetting steep cuts to Medicaid funding signed into law last year by President Trump, is novel in the U.S. Unlike the income taxes that most Americans pay, it would apply to the net worth of California residents with net assets of $1 billion or more.\n\nThe wealth tax has exposed bitter divisions in the Democratic Party and in California's labor movement.\n\nNewsom came out against the tax last year, weeks after it was proposed by a healthcare-workers union, and has argued the levy could push the state's largest taxpayers to flee. The state Democratic Party, meanwhile, voted in August to endorse the measure.\n\nProp. 42 could invalidate a billionaire tax by changing California's constitution to prohibit new taxes on financial assets or personal property, other than real estate.\n\nThe California Democratic Party and several large unions oppose it.\n\nCalifornia billionaires have poured money into campaigns claiming the tax could weaken the state's fiscal base or be expanded by the legislature to apply to a broader swath of residents.\n\nA likely legal battle looms if voters approve this measure and the wealth tax.\n\nThe union backing the billionaire tax effort—the Service Employees International Union-United Healthcare Workers West—said Newsom was siding with California's wealthiest residents over healthcare for children and families. \"Governor Newsom is in lock step with his billionaire-backers,\" Debru Carthan, the union's vice president, said.",
  "priority": "P2",
  "type": "article",
  "turn_to": null,
  "terms": [
   {
    "en": "Paul Kiernan",
    "zh_suggested": "保罗·基尔南",
    "note": "待查"
   },
   {
    "en": "Proposition 42",
    "zh_suggested": "42号提案",
    "note": "加州反财富税修宪提案"
   },
   {
    "en": "Sergey Brin",
    "zh_suggested": "谢尔盖·布林",
    "note": "谷歌联合创始人"
   },
   {
    "en": "Service Employees International Union-United Healthcare Workers West",
    "zh_suggested": "服务业雇员国际工会-联合医护工作者西部",
    "note": "待查"
   },
   {
    "en": "Debru Carthan",
    "zh_suggested": "德布鲁·卡森",
    "note": "待查"
   }
  ],
  "final_id": "019",
  "title_cn": "纽森支持一项旨在对抗富豪税收计划的举措",
  "dek_cn": null,
  "body_cn": "加利福尼亚州州长加文·纽森反对一项备受关注的提案，该提案旨在对亿万富翁的财富征税。现在，他支持另一项提案，旨在禁止征收财富税。\n\n纽森周五通过支持反对征税提案的竞选团队宣布了他的支持，该提案被称为“42号提案”。纽森表示：“加利福尼亚人有权保护他们的退休金和生活储蓄，使其免受新的州税影响。”\n\n“42号提案”已筹集了数千万美元，资金来自一个政治委员会，该委员会的部分资金来自亿万富翁，包括谷歌联合创始人谢尔盖·布林。在一家医护工作者工会提议对亿万富翁的财富一次性征收 5% 的税后，这项活动获得了推动。选民将于 11 月 3 日就这两项提案进行表决。\n\n拟议的亿万富翁税旨在抵消特朗普总统去年签署生效的对医疗补助资金的大幅削减，在美国是前所未有的。与大多数美国人缴纳的所得税不同，它将适用于净资产为 10 亿美元或以上的加利福尼亚居民的净资产。\n\n这项财富税暴露了民主党和加利福尼亚州劳工运动内部的严重分歧。\n\n纽森去年反对这项税收，时间是在一家医护工作者工会提出该提案的几周后，他认为这项税收可能会导致该州最大的纳税人逃离。与此同时，该州民主党于 8 月投票支持该提案。\n\n“42号提案”可以通过修改加利福尼亚州的宪法，禁止对金融资产或个人财产（房地产除外）征收新的税收，从而使亿万富翁税失效。\n\n加利福尼亚州民主党和几个大型工会反对该提案。\n\n加利福尼亚州的亿万富翁向竞选活动投入了大量资金，声称这项税收可能会削弱该州的财政基础，或者由立法机构扩大适用范围，覆盖更广泛的居民。\n\n如果选民同时批准这项提案和财富税，一场法律战很可能随之而来。\n\n支持这项针对富豪征税计划的工会——服务业雇员国际工会-联合医护工作者西部——表示，纽森州长站在加州最富有的居民一边，而不是优先考虑儿童和家庭的医疗保健需求。“纽森州长与支持他的富豪们步调一致，”该工会的副主席德布鲁·卡森说道。",
  "context_cn": null,
  "context_attr": null
 },
 {
  "id": "E1-020",
  "page": "A3",
  "section": "U.S. News",
  "title": "Local Politics Stymie Silicon Valley Standoff",
  "dek": "between county leaders and developers frustrates $3 billion project",
  "byline": "BY ZUSHA ELINSON",
  "body_en": "SOLANO COUNTY, Calif.—In the summer of 2025, lobbyists for Silicon Valley-backed entrepreneur Jan Sramek were in overdrive. With Gov. Gavin Newsom's office in his corner, Sramek was chasing a prize: a $3 billion shipyard beside the city he wants to build from scratch here.\n\n\"WE HAVE SOME GREAT NEWS!!!!!!,\" a Newsom staffer emailed after the site made the shortlist. \"We have not had the chance to land a WHALE of a project like this since Tesla more than a decade ago.\"\n\nThe excitement didn't last. Within an hour, Sramek warned of an obstacle: county government red tape. Ten months later, the shipmaker, Saronic Technologies, chose Brownsville, Texas, taking with it an estimated 10,000 jobs.\n\nSramek, 39 years old, is no stranger to big deals. By 22, he was handling multimillion-dollar trades at Goldman Sachs in London. Now, he is trying to build a kind of walkable suburban utopia, with housing, a high-tech manufacturing hub and a shipyard, a 90-minute drive from San Francisco. His company California Forever has raised about $1 billion from investors, including venture firm Andreessen Horowitz and LinkedIn co-founder Reid Hoffman, assembled top urban planners and won over state leaders including Newsom.\n\nThe group wants to address a regional housing shortage around San Francisco, as well as a lack of jobs in Solano County, which this year lost a Budweiser brewery and a Valero Energy refinery.\n\nBut its biggest hurdle is the county itself.\n\nThe face of the opposition is Mitch Mashburn, 59, a cowboy-hat-wearing Republican county supervisor and descendant of local farmers who has complained the push for the shipyard was being \"crammed down our throat.\"\n\nSramek casts the standoff as a fight against forces that have priced people out of housing and stalled local economies. \"The battle for the future of California is being fought in Solano County,\" he said.\n\nOpponents view it as a tech-billionaire land grab. \"I call it broligarch city because it's really tech bros behind this,\" said Jim DeKloe, a community-college professor and local Sierra Club leader.\n\nEmails seen by The Wall Street Journal show relations have soured to the point where county officials appear more inclined to block the project than negotiate.\n\nFormer County Supervisor Jim Spering faults both sides. County leaders like Mashburn aren't forward-looking and don't know how to cut deals that would help the region, he said. And Sramek is one of those \"dreamer developers that don't understand that you've got to build a relationship with the community\" before \"telling them what you want to do,\" Spering said.\n\nSramek said he thinks his project would have faced stiff opposition no matter the approach. From the start, his campaign to buy up 60,000 acres for a master-planned community for 400,000 people enraged locals, especially after California Forever sued multigenerational farming families, alleging they conspired to inflate land prices. He called opponents the \"70-year-old Sierra Club type.\"\n\nAfterward, Sramek and Mashburn pledged to slow down and work together. The detente faced its first test in Saronic's planned large shipyard along the Sacramento River on California Forever's land. As Saronic, which makes autonomous sea vessels, weighed sites in 2025, Sramek's deputies courted local leaders with riverside tours alongside retired military brass.\n\nBut by April, county officials were feeling sidelined. When a Sramek deputy reminded then-County Administrator Bill Emlen of six months of meetings with him, Mashburn and others, receipts attached, Emlen shot back. \"Sending invites to public briefings does not substitute for a seat at the table,\" he wrote.\n\nSramek found an ally in Bret Prebula, a former government and restless dealmaker who became city manager of Suisun City, one of Solano County's poorest communities. Prebula, 43, saw an opportunity for his 4-square-mile city, which has a large Black population and has long felt overlooked in Solano County. In early 2025, he proposed expanding Suisun City's borders by 35 square miles to annex a significant portion of California Forever's land. For Prebula, it meant city revenue. For Sramek, it was a way around the county's strict rules on development outside city limits.\n\nIn June 2025, Sramek offered Suisun City a $55,000 grant for the local July 4 fireworks celebration and other events threatened by a budget shortfall. He offered to buy $1.5 million in downtown properties to help close the gap, emails show.\n\nSoon after, they struck a deal: California Forever would cover the city's project-related costs and pay it $10 million if the annexation is finalized.\n\nOpponents cried foul. DeKloe, the Sierra Club leader, called it a \"weasel tactic\" intended to bypass a county-wide vote.\n\nWhen Solano County made the shortlist last August, Sramek pushed for special legislation to fast-track the project. State lawmakers worked up a bill but wanted the county to sign off. At a board meeting, Mashburn chastised California Forever for the rush but voted to endorse a compromise bill.\n\nThe bill fell short for Saronic, according to Sramek. Despite months of talks between Sramek's team, Mashburn and lawmakers, trying to hammer out a better one, Saronic said in June it had picked Texas.\n\n\"We kept raising our hand and saying, if we don't pass the legislation, they are going to go to Brownsville,\" Sramek said, \"but there wasn't the political will to get it done.\" A Saronic spokeswoman didn't respond to requests to comment.\n\nPulling into California Forever's unmarked office, Sramek called the vote a speed bump. But the emails show California Forever boosters fear Mashburn has another bullet in the chamber. The Suisun City annexation requires a tax-sharing agreement with Solano County, one developers [say] Mashburn has said publicly the county can't be forced to make.\n\n\"What's happening here is not just local friction,\" Prebula complained in an email to state officials. \"it is a clear example of how counties can weaponize process and inertia.\"",
  "priority": "P2",
  "type": "article",
  "turn_to": null,
