 {
  "id": "E1-029",
  "page": "A5",
  "section": "U.S. News",
  "title": "Hegseth Gets Heat Over Livestream Firing Squad",
  "dek": null,
  "byline": "BY LARA SELIGMAN AND YOKO KUBOTA",
  "body_en": "The planned Dec. 3 execution of Nidal Hasan, a former Army officer who killed 13 people in a 2009 attack, has sparked a national debate after the Pentagon announced that his death by firing squad would be livestreamed.\n\nWhen asked about the livestream, President Trump told reporters Friday afternoon that his administration \"is making a determination about that, but he will be killed.\"\n\nNidal Hasan was a U.S.-born Muslim and Army psychiatrist working at Fort Hood in Texas who said he opened fire because he was trying to defend fellow Muslims. According to testimony, Hasan walked into a crowded building at Fort Hood on Nov. 5, 2009, with 420 rounds of ammunition stuffed in his pockets and paper towels to muffle the sound of the bullets. He yelled \"Allahu akbar\"—Arabic for \"God is great\"—and methodically shot at soldiers, repeatedly shooting wounded soldiers as they tried to escape. Hasan was eventually shot and paralyzed from the waist down in the attack. He killed 13 people and wounded 32 more.\n\nHasan admitted during his court-martial that he carried out the shooting because he believed the military was at war with Islam. During the trial, he acted as his own lawyer and offered no defense for the attack. He was sentenced to death in 2013 by a jury of fellow officers.\n\nHasan has since been held on death row at the military prison in Fort Leavenworth, Kan. The Army, which is required to provide him legal counsel, declined to comment on his behalf.\n\nIt would be the first execution of a servicemember in 65 years. Only a president can approve the execution of U.S. servicemembers, who are subject to the military's own legal system. Trump approved Hasan's firing squad execution on Oct. 2 upon Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth's recommendation, the Pentagon said.\n\nArmy regulation says the execution must take place no sooner than 60 days and no later than 150 days after the president's approval. In the case of Hasan, his Dec. 3 execution would be almost immediately after the 60-day mark.\n\nIf carried out as planned, it would be the first U.S. military execution since 1961, when U.S. Army Pvt. John Bennett was hanged at Leavenworth after a court-martial convicted him of rape and attempted murder. It also would be the first military death by firing squad since World War II, according to the Death Penalty Information Center.\n\n\"It hadn't been since World War II since we've done that. Well, I kind of like the mentality we had in World War II, which was: 'win,' \" Hegseth said.\n\nHegseth said the execution was planned to take place at Fort Hood on the same day of the week and time of day that Hasan opened fire there.\n\nDemocrats and some Republicans have recoiled at Hegseth's plan to livestream the execution, with one Republican saying it turns the event into entertainment and others suggesting it puts America on par with \"jihadists.\" Hasan had told military officials he hoped to become a martyr.\n\nVice President JD Vance on Friday questioned whether the livestream would happen. If it did, he said, he wouldn't be watching. \"I don't know that's actually going to happen,\" he told reporters. He later added: \"Look, if it's all through livestream, I'm not going to be watching it.\"\n\nBishops called on the Pentagon to reconsider the plan. \"This decision is a barbaric promotion of a culture of death,\" Archbishop Paul S. Coakley said. The president of the United States Conference of Catholic Bishops called on the Pentagon to reconsider the plan.\n\nRepublicans expressing opposition include Rep. Rob Bresnahan of Pennsylvania, who wrote on X that the focus shouldn't be \"turning an execution into entertainment.\" Rep. Austin Scott of Georgia, who noted he supports the death sentence, said on X: \"Livestreaming executions is what jihadists do. America is better than that.\" Sen. John Curtis of Utah urged Hegseth in a post on X to reconsider, writing that, \"Broadcasting a death diminishes us and is not an effective deterrent.\"\n\nThe Pentagon and Army have declined to say where the video will be livestreamed and whether it would be put on the internet or a closed circuit for select members of the public to view. Under Army regulations, no one under the age of 18 is allowed to witness an execution of a U.S. servicemember. That rule would be impossible to follow if the military livestreams the execution on the internet.",
  "priority": "P1",
  "type": "article",
  "turn_to": null,
