   }
  ],
  "final_id": "030",
  "title_cn": "行刑失败后派克出院",
  "dek_cn": null,
  "body_cn": "克丽斯塔·派克是田纳西州的一名女囚，她在州政府试图以注射方式对她执行死刑的行动中幸存下来。她的律师表示，她已出院，并返回监狱。\n\n50岁的派克于周六返回位于纳什维尔的黛布拉·K·约翰逊康复中心，她已经在那里服刑了30多年。“她将继续在监狱接受医疗护理，我们将继续关注她的康复情况，”派克的律师说。\n\n派克于1996年因谋杀科琳·斯莱默而被判处死刑。9月30日，在处决行动失败后，她被紧急送往当地医院。派克接受了两剂戊巴比妥，这是一种镇静剂，至少有17个州将其用于死刑，但处决行动在两次注射后被取消，因为她仍然活着。\n\n田纳西州惩教部门表示，他们遵循了既定的程序，并且该药物是有效的。田纳西州州长比尔·李（共和党人）此后暂停了该州的处决。\n\n在医院，派克起初被插管并用上呼吸机，医护人员设法清除她体内的戊巴比妥。最近几天，派克的律师表示，她已经恢复意识，并且可以说话。\n\n她的法律团队呼吁州长将她的死刑减为终身监禁，让她在狱中度过余生。",
  "context_cn": null,
  "context_attr": null
 },
 {
  "id": "E1-031",
  "page": "A6",
  "section": "U.S. News",
  "title": "Probe of Trump Foes Falters",
  "dek": "Departures, infighting, inexperience beset DOJ inquiry into alleged conspiracy",
  "byline": "By Sadie Gurman, Dave Michaels and Josh Dawsey",
  "body_en": "Last year, Justice Department officials said an investigation would unravel a grand conspiracy by a cabal of national security officials who sought to undermine President Trump.\n\nTrump allies hoped it would confirm what they have been alleging for years: Officials who investigated the president over the past decade engaged in what was essentially a coordinated effort to violate his civil rights.\n\nInstead, the probe has been beset by departures, infighting and inexperience. The 81-year-old Reagan-era prosecutor coaxed out of retirement to lead the effort, Joe diGenova, quit last month, saying top department officials were pressuring the team to bring criminal cases before they were ready. He hadn't charged anyone. Officials denied pressuring him.\n\nDiGenova was one of several overlapping supervisors of the probe, with different interests and marching orders, adding to the chaotic nature of the effort. Last month, another senior counsel, Kurt Olsen, who had joined the probe from the White House, resigned from the inquiry, people familiar with the matter said. He had recently lost a security clearance after failing government polygraph tests about his foreign contacts. Olsen said the security clearance issue wasn't a factor in his departure but declined to elaborate.\n\nTrump is frustrated by the lack of progress and has complained about it to allies, a person familiar with the conversations said.\n\nA number of the Justice Department officials said the case is proceeding apace, with roughly a dozen prosecutors continuing to pursue a set of related investigations that could result in charges in coming weeks. But they privately acknowledge the idea of tying all those loose threads together in one big conspiracy case in South Florida, as they had initially attempted, isn't a practical approach.\n\nOne of the officials said leadership is still confident in the people who continue to work on the inquiry, and recently brought on two former prosecutors. A White House spokeswoman said Trump has full confidence in Attorney General Todd Blanche to hold people accountable.\n\nIn addition to the departures of Olsen and diGenova, Justice Department officials came to believe a third supervisor of the case, Miami U.S. attorney Jason Reding Quiñones, wasn't appropriately managing the investigation and his office more broadly.\n\nA number of the prosecutors on the probe complained to senior officials in Washington that Quiñones had given them no guidance, some of the people said.\n\nLawyers in the deputy attorney general's office are now directly overseeing the Florida-based investigation from Washington, officials said.\n\nA spokeswoman for Quiñones defended his record on the job and said he was proud to serve in the Trump administration but declined to comment on the conspiracy investigation.\n\nThe putative targets of the probe include a raft of people Trump has long disdained, including former CIA Director John Brennan, former FBI Director James Comey and officials involved in the August 2022 search of his Mar-a-Lago home. (Comey was separately charged in connection with a photo he posted of seashells that Trump alleged was threatening, and a judge dismissed a second case targeting him. He has denied the charges and said the cases were an attempt at political retribution.)\n\nThe investigation, which officials informally referred to as the grand-conspiracy probe, sought to tie together a series of past episodes, including the 2016 intelligence community assessment that Russia interfered in that year's election to boost Trump; the FBI's subsequent investigation into alleged Trump campaign ties to that Russian meddling; and the more recent criminal prosecutions by special counsel Jack Smith in 2023 and 2024 that stemmed from Trump's 2020 election loss.\n\nThe probes went to two other U.S. attorneys before Quiñones raised his hand to oversee it, people familiar with the assignment said.\n\nOne of the investigation's first targets: congressional testimony Brennan had given in 2017 and 2023 about the 2016 assessment. A veteran national-security prosecutor working on that line of inquiry, Maria Medetis Long, soon told senior officials she hadn't found a basis to indict Brennan, some of the people said. In April, she was removed from the probe.\n\nThe Justice Department had struggled to find and retain other prosecutors to work on it and brought on temporary hires; some of them had never worked as federal prosecutors. The office had to give them a crash course on presenting evidence to grand juries, some people familiar with the probe said.\n\nJustice Department officials in Washington at the time wanted prosecutors to speed up their process, including by subpoenaing witnesses to testify directly before a grand jury, without any FBI interview first, they said.\n\nProsecutors usually avoid doing that to make sure they know what to expect a witness to say and avoid surprises before the grand jury. Prosecutors had to later withdraw the subpoenas after realizing information the witnesses would need to discuss with grand jurors was classified.\n\nProsecutors had been trying to make the case that the FBI's court-authorized August 2022 search of Trump's Mar-a-Lago home, which led to charges that he illegally retained classified information, was unlawful. But an FBI agent told diGenova that tying the search to a broader conspiracy spanning from Florida to Washington would be challenging; diGenova disagreed and called FBI Director Kash Patel, who moved the agent off the investigation.",
  "priority": "P1",
  "type": "article",
  "turn_to": null,
