 {
  "id": "E1-043",
  "page": "A9",
  "section": "World News",
  "title": "Kenyan Club Rejects Bid To Allow Women Into Bar",
  "dek": null,
  "byline": "BY CAROLINE KIMEU",
  "body_en": "One of Kenya's most prestigious social clubs has voted to keep women out of its men-only bar for the foreseeable future.\n\nThe policy, reflected in a fictional scene where Meryl Streep's character is coldly turned away in the 1985 film \"Out of Africa,\" has spurred rancorous debates at the exclusive club for months and was the subject of an article in The Wall Street Journal earlier this year.\n\nMuthaiga Country Club in Nairobi, opened in 1913 for the British elite, has evolved into an exclusive club for top government officials, wealthy businesspeople and successful professionals. Membership is by invitation only, with admission fees upward of $4,000.\n\nYet its century-old Member's Bar has remained strictly reserved for men, and women can only go in once or twice a year during major club events.\n\nFor some members, the bar policy is a source of frustration that has been simmering for decades. For others, it is \"hallowed ground in terms of the genesis story of the club.\"\n\nMore than 700 club members voted on Thursday to keep the men-only bar and set up a separate space for women, against 200 who supported the idea of opening up the men's bar to everyone. \"It was a slaughter,\" said one male member, who had to follow events from outside because the ballroom, where the vote happened, was packed. Over 1,000 members followed the meeting online.\n\nThe club didn't respond to a request for comment.\n\nOne female member opened the debate by defending the men's bar as one of the few remaining spaces men could be men, and insisted that the club had bigger fish to fry than the bar, members recounted. Another woman member who had been campaigning against the men's bar suddenly decamped to the other side.\n\nOther members, including many women, argued that they shouldn't have to pay for the running of a bar they couldn't use.\n\nSome members worry the vote could jeopardize reciprocal privileges with over 100 affiliated international clubs. London's Reform Club already ended its agreement over the policy, and an American club recently withdrew its interest in having a reciprocal arrangement, members familiar with the matter said.\n\nChloé Spo, the French businesswoman who resubmitted the proposal to open up the bar to all members, concluded that Muthaiga is \"still a man's club.\"",
  "priority": "P2",
  "type": "article",
  "turn_to": null,
  "terms": [
   {
    "en": "Caroline Kimeu",
    "zh_suggested": "卡罗琳·基梅乌",
