  ],
  "final_id": "048",
  "title_cn": "尼古丁品牌形象焕然一新。",
  "dek_cn": "以下内容最初发表在莎拉·阿什利·奥布莱恩的每周《Wellness newsletter》中。",
  "body_cn": "尼古丁的品牌重塑正在如火如荼地进行。网红和企业家们正在传播一种观点，即这种具有高度成瘾性的物质，在少量使用时实际上对人体有益。他们声称，它可以改善注意力、改善情绪和增强记忆力。推广无烟尼古丁产品的公司也在积极利用健康、保健和健身的概念。\n\nLucy公司销售“智能尼古丁”产品，包括调味口香糖和尼古丁袋，在其网站上表示：“无论您使用尼古丁来提高注意力、增强精力还是放松身心，都有适合您的Lucy产品。”Athletic Nicotine的网站上展示了一位年轻女性在划船机上运动，配文为：“为我们每个人体内的赢家提供更高水平的尼古丁。”另一家品牌Nic Nac，宣传其产品含有“优质成分”，并告诉消费者，可以在任何需要提高表现的场合使用其尼古丁含片，例如在健身房、办公室或飞机上。\n\n这些公司都告诉我，他们的产品是为现有的成年尼古丁使用者设计的。Athletic Nicotine表示，他们不会将自己定位为“健康产品、保健补充剂或性能增强剂”。Lucy表示，其网站上的文案旨在吸引那些已经将尼古丁用于这些目的的人。\n\n引言：“网红们正在传播一种观点，即尼古丁在少量使用时实际上是有益的。研究驳斥了这些说法。”\n\n多年来，网红们一直在谈论尼古丁作为一种保健产品。他们推广了无烟尼古丁配方，包括尼古丁含片、尼古丁袋和口香糖。这在DIY健康时代是一种常见的现象：网红们利用轶事证据和有限的研究来推广下一款“神奇”产品。他们的宣传可能会对其追随者产生强大的影响。虽然尼古丁袋这类产品被认为比香烟危害小——香烟几乎损害人体的每一个器官——但正是尼古丁让烟草产品如此容易上瘾。\n\n肺科医生哈斯米娜·卡图里亚最近在《华盛顿邮报》撰文，驳斥了尼古丁的养生说法，并强调了其风险。她写道，尼古丁会激活人体的“战斗或逃跑”反应，增加心率和血压，并可能扰乱睡眠。\n\n弗吉尼亚大学（我的母校）公共卫生科学副教授梅丽莎·利特尔告诉我，研究人员尚未发现认知能力与尼古丁使用之间存在关联。她同时也是该校尼古丁与烟草研究中心的负责人。她说：“关于尼古丁对记忆、情绪和新陈代谢的益处，现有证据并不充分。”她补充说，从尼古丁使用中获得的情绪改善，主要来自“使用尼古丁替代疗法来缓解戒断症状的吸烟者。大多数不吸烟的人发现尼古丁会使他们的情绪恶化，甚至会增加恶心感。”此外，她表示，尼古丁袋这类产品的长期健康影响仍然未知。\n\n与此同时，从2023年1月到2026年5月，尼古丁袋的销量增加了319%。",
  "context_cn": null,
  "context_attr": "无"
 },
 {
  "id": "E2-007",
  "page": "A11",
  "section": "Personal Journal",
  "title": "Facial fat grafting grows",
  "dek": "Item from Sara Ashley O'Brien's weekly Wellness newsletter",
  "byline": "Sara Ashley O'Brien",
  "body_en": "My colleague Chavie Lieber and I published an article recently about how facial filler has fallen out of favor. The filler look—characterized by overly plump lips and pillowy cheeks—is losing appeal. It's partly because people are shrinking due to GLP-1s, so their features, too, must scale to size.\n\nThe American Society of Plastic Surgeons recently released its annual report with statistics showing cosmetic-procedure trends in 2025. Notably, 82% of the organization's member surgeons reported receiving consultation requests related to GLP-1 use in 2025. Of all cosmetic procedures tracked, one saw the most growth year-over-year: facial fat grafting.\n\nFacial fat grafting, up 39% in 2025 compared with the year prior, involves removing fat from elsewhere in the body, such as the stomach, via liposuction and then having it injected into the face. The idea is to rejuvenate and restore lost volume, which might come as a result of weight loss. “Ozempic Face” isn't what most people are going for when they start taking these medications. The desired outcome is a more naturally filled out look.\n\n“People want to look as good as they feel,” ASPS president Dr. Bob Basu, a plastic surgeon in Houston, told me. “People want to look refreshed, but they want to look like an authentic version of themselves, and I think that's where fat comes into play.”\n\nASPS said it no longer reports the costs of procedures because they're variable, fluctuating by many factors including geographic location or a surgeon's expertise. In 2024, the organization said the price for the surgeon's fee for facial fat grafting was between $3,000 and $5,500, which doesn't include additional costs for things like anesthesia, operating room facility fees and other fees.\n\nDr. Kelly Killeen, an ASPS member and plastic surgeon at the Practice Healthcare in Beverly Hills, told me facial fat grafting can even sometimes be done in the office, depending on how much fat is being placed and any other procedures involved. She said it could run from as little as $4,000 up to more than $20,000, with add-ons like skin-tightening procedures.\n\nTo subscribe to the Friday newsletter, go to wsj.com/newsletters.",
  "priority": "P2",
  "type": "article",
  "turn_to": null,
  "terms": [
   {
    "en": "GLP-1",
    "zh_suggested": "GLP-1",
    "note": "glucagon-like peptide-1, weight-loss drugs"
   },
   {
    "en": "Ozempic Face",
    "zh_suggested": "Ozempic脸",
    "note": "facial volume loss from weight-loss drugs"
   },
   {
    "en": "facial fat grafting",
    "zh_suggested": "面部脂肪移植"
   },
   {
    "en": "American Society of Plastic Surgeons",
    "zh_suggested": "美国整形外科医师协会",
    "note": "ASPS"
   },
   {
    "en": "Dr. Bob Basu",
    "zh_suggested": "鲍勃·巴苏医生",
    "note": "待查, ASPS president"
   },
   {
    "en": "Dr. Kelly Killeen",
    "zh_suggested": "凯利·基林医生",
    "note": "待查"
   },
   {
    "en": "Chavie Lieber",
    "zh_suggested": "查维·利伯",
    "note": "待查, WSJ colleague"
   },
   {
    "en": "liposuction",
    "zh_suggested": "抽脂术"
   }
  ],
  "final_id": "049",
  "title_cn": "面部脂肪移植术的需求正在增长。",
  "dek_cn": "摘自莎拉·阿什利·奥布莱恩每周健康通讯。",
  "body_cn": "我的同事查维·利伯和我最近发表了一篇文章，讨论了面部填充剂为何不再受欢迎。这种填充剂带来的效果——过度丰满的嘴唇和饱满的脸颊——正在失去吸引力。这部分原因是人们因为使用GLP-1而体重减轻，因此他们的面部特征也需要相应地调整。\n\n美国整形外科医师协会最近发布了年度报告，其中包含2025年美容手术趋势的统计数据。值得注意的是，该协会82%的会员外科医生表示，在2025年收到了与GLP-1使用相关的咨询请求。在所有追踪的美容手术中，同比增长最显著的是面部脂肪移植。\n\n2025年面部脂肪移植同比增长39%，这种手术包括通过抽脂术从身体其他部位（如腹部）提取脂肪，然后将其注射到面部。其目的是为了恢复面部年轻状态，并补充因体重减轻而流失的体积。人们开始服用这些药物时，通常并不希望出现“Ozempic脸”的效果。他们希望达到的效果是更自然、更饱满的面部轮廓。\n\n“人们希望看起来和感觉一样好，”美国整形外科医师协会主席、休斯顿整形外科医生鲍勃·巴苏医生告诉我。“人们希望看起来焕发活力，但他们希望看起来像真实的自己，我认为脂肪在其中发挥了作用。”\n\n美国整形外科医师协会表示，他们不再报告手术的费用，因为这些费用会因多种因素而异，包括地理位置和外科医生的专业水平。2024年，该协会表示，面部脂肪移植手术的医生费用在3000美元到5500美元之间，不包括麻醉、手术室费用和其他费用。\n\n凯利·基林医生是美国整形外科医师协会的会员，也是比弗利山庄Practice Healthcare的整形外科医生。她告诉我，面部脂肪移植有时甚至可以在诊所进行，具体取决于需要填充的脂肪量以及是否需要进行其他手术。她说，手术费用可能从4000美元到20000美元以上不等，具体取决于是否需要进行其他项目，例如皮肤紧致手术。\n\n如需订阅每周五的新闻简报，请访问wsj.com/newsletters。",
  "context_cn": null,
  "context_attr": "无"
 },
 {
  "id": "E2-008",
  "page": "A12",
  "section": "Personal Journal",
  "title": "Videos of People Paying Down Their Debt Are Generating Interest",
  "dek": "Viewers return to cheer, judge and see whether balances went up or down",
  "byline": "Dalvin Brown",
  "body_en": "Facing your debt can be painful. Watching someone else deal with theirs can be gripping. That became clear to Rodney Morgan in July, when he posted a TikTok of himself sitting on a blowup mattress with his head in one hand, and his debt tally of $40,058.44 imposed on the screen.\n\n“I'm rebuilding from scratch as a flight attendant and being honest about where I'm starting,” wrote Morgan, a 33-year-old Los Angeles resident. It was a changeup from his usual posts about life as a flight attendant, and racked up 1.5 million views—far more than the roughly 100,000 views he said his previous best-performing videos drew.\n\nTikTok is filled with influencers dispensing all manner of advice on how to get ahead financially, whether it is saving to retire early, earning a passive income or using credit-card rewards to fund a globe-trotting lifestyle. But users are also finding big audiences showing the private money struggles many would be too embarrassed to share.\n\nThe format is simple: Users share the exact amount they owe, along with updates on how they are chipping away at the figure—or suffering setbacks. Viewers tune in to see the numbers move up and down and root for people, often weighing in with advice.\n\nMore than 28,000 debt-related videos were uploaded to TikTok in the 12 months through September, according to Tubular Labs, which tracks social-video viewing.\n\nPart of #DebtTok's draw is that it addresses a taboo topic that people feel they can't discuss even with friends and family. Like gossip, the videos let viewers size up other people's financial choices and measure their own against them, said Brooke Erin Duffy, a Cornell University professor who studies social-media culture.\n\nPersonal-finance gurus like Suze Orman and Dave Ramsey gained huge followings by dispensing advice to callers who shared their money problems. TikTok videos cut out the guru—the host is the person with the problem, not the one giving advice.\n\nViewers tune in for the drama of a hero's journey, like a movie where someone takes on a big obstacle, said Brittney Castro, a certified financial planner at Chime, a mobile banking app. “It's so human to want to see that person win and succeed,” she said.\n\nFor those posting about their debt, racking up views can help pay off bills. TikTok pays creators for some popular videos, and viewers can send virtual gifts during livestreams. Creators also earn income when brands sponsor their posts, or a cut when followers buy through links they share.\n\nAfter Morgan's video took off, for instance, a personal-finance app offered him his biggest social-media deal yet. He expects the sponsored post to pay off a piece of his debt: the roughly $800 left on the Home Depot and Lowe's cards he opened to remodel his house.\n\nEvery night before bed, Tianna Carter records an update on her debt, sometimes at 10 p.m. in her kitchen. “I look forward to it every day,” she said. Carter, a 31-year-old human-resources director for a public school district, didn't say at first the amount she owed: $54,057.93. Then she decided to share the figure. “People understand more and can relate more to my number,” said Carter, who lives in Harrisburg, Pa. Her followers don't always agree with her choices, such as her spending on her personal upkeep. “How am I supposed to keep you accountable if you're getting your hair done?” one commenter wrote. Carter said she treats salon visits as fixed costs and cuts elsewhere, like bringing lunch to work. She puts the money she makes from her posts toward daycare costs and paying down her debt.\n\nWhen Kayla Rose was in college, her father gave her a credit card for groceries and gas. She ended up using it to buy clothes, workout gear and home décor as well, promoting some of what she bought in her videos. Rose, now a 29-year-old full-time influencer in Palm Beach, Fla., told followers in March that she owed more than $25,000 on credit cards and planned to pay it off with money she made from her videos. The posts about her debt brought new followers, and she paid off the cards in August. “What paid off my credit-card debt was these silly little videos you guys are currently watching,” she told followers.",
  "priority": "P2",
  "type": "article",
  "turn_to": null,
