 {
  "id": "E2-014",
  "page": "A14",
  "section": "Arts in Review",
  "title": "Lights, Camera, Costumes!",
  "dek": "As silent movies emerged as an art form, fashion played an essential, energizing role on screen",
  "byline": "Laura Jacobs (Exhibition Review)",
  "body_en": "New York. The story for Paramount's 1927 silent movie “It” was provided by the British writer Elinor Glyn, who used the word to mean a “magnetic force” that draws others. The 21-year-old actress Clara Bow, who starred in the film, was henceforth known as the first “It Girl.” Charisma? Glamour? Not quite. Her creamy cheeks, tousled bob and bright, beguiling eyes brought a hint of id, jolts of energy, into the picture. And energy was what moving pictures were all about. Where photographs were still, and theater could talk, silent movies were action and “It” was everything.\n\nThat the mute infant art form soon relied on fashions that excited the eye should come as no surprise. Glyn's older sister, Lady Duff-Gordon, otherwise known as the couturier Lucile, immediately forged relationships with actresses—Billie Burke, for instance. As early as 1913 she was designing heart-stopping gowns for their movies, which in turn brought renown to her fashion house. What is surprising is that “Goddesses in the Machine: Fashion in American Silent Film,” at New York's Bard Graduate Center, is the first major exhibition to explore the subject.\n\nLaunched in the U.S. in 1896, with the New York premiere of Thomas Edison's Vitascope film projector, silent movies astonished audiences. And yet the medium, entirely visual, posed definite challenges. “Seeking ways to craft narratives without audible dialogue,” reads the show's introductory wall text, “filmmakers used costume to convey character, drive plot, and heighten emotion.”\n\nThe first costumes were made by dressmakers and couturiers, but a network of costume professionals would emerge, designers who realized that clothing in film had to be fascinating, energized—stories within the story.\n\nThe show's co-organizers—Michelle Finamore, a fashion and design historian, and Emma Cormack, the associate curator for exhibitions and study collection curator at Bard Graduate Center—remind us that during those early decades, when the movie industry began on the East Coast, and relocated to California in the mid-1910s, costumes were not preserved. Even after the advent of the talkies, and well into the sound era, they were reused and revised with no thought to historical value. Leslie Caron once told me that the unforgettable white gown she wore in 1958's “Gigi,” designed by Cecil Beaton and ravishing with swallow's wings at the shoulders, was used on and on, feathers plucked, and that it eventually had sleeves. Garments that have lasted, usually aided by conservation, are rare—and rarely exhibited.\n\nSo it's quite the bounty that the exhibition presents 16 surviving silent-screen treasures along with stills and film clips that show the pieces in action. Among them are two ensembles worn by Lillian Gish in D.W. Griffith's “Way Down East” (1920); a risqué Travis Banton evening dress used in both Dorothy Arzner's “Fashions for Women” (1927) and A. Edward Sutherland's “Pointed Heels” (1929); and a fringed mauve gown from which Gloria Swanson unwound herself in Cecil B. DeMille's “Male and Female” (1919). These gems are surrounded by a wealth of period garments, accessories, artworks, posters, sketches, photographs and clips from over 50 silent movies.\n\nThe exhibition of more than 180 objects unfolds over three floors, with the first bringing us into the early moviegoing experience. A 1920s usher's uniform in sky blue with black trim, likely from the Plaza Theatre in London, stands at the entrance. Videos show people at the new “picture palaces,” where genders and classes mingle and comic clashes ensue. Displays of brightly colored glass preview slides, fanzines such as Photoplay, and Beautebox tins lithographed with the faces of stars (Pola Negri! Bebe Daniels!) illustrate the various forms of advertising and collectibles aimed at filmgoers.\n\nOn the second floor, the curators look at the actresses who went to Callot Soeurs, Madame Frances and Henri Bendel for their costumes, and then move on to the new studio system and its designers, who pulled a sense of color from the black-and-white spectrum, and exploited reflective materials—lamé, panné velvet, satin, sequins—for onscreen shimmer and flash. Silent movies may be the home of slapstick, but they are also akin to ballet. Light reinforces gesture and shadows contour expression.\n\nOn the third floor, the focus is movement, “woven into the fabric of cinema” through dance-hall and jazz-club settings, as well as in scenes of driving, swimming, riding and piloting. A surprisingly immaculate canary-yellow dress, glitteringly embellished for Mae Murray in “Circe the Enchantress” (1924), is flanked by the film that has her wearing it in a nightclub, dancing up a storm. Look at those Watteau straps in the back, dripping with rhinestones and looping, swinging, amplifying the dynamics. Onscreen the dress is the color of limelight.\n\nWith precise plotting and symbolic pieces, the curators have neatly fit the era into the center's jewel-box spaces. Nothing is belabored. And who would have guessed that a grail of It-ness would be part of the show, nestled next to the matador jacket worn by Rudolph Valentino in 1922's “Blood and Sand.” On loan from La Cinémathèque Française, Paris, laid flat in a vitrine, is a flesh-colored silk-chiffon slip dress, its hem beaded with silver lozenges. A relic from “Red Hair,” a 1928 silent film now lost, it was made for Clara Bow.\n\nGoddesses in the Machine: Fashion in American Silent Film. Bard Graduate Center, through Jan. 3, 2027.\n\nMs. Jacobs is the Arts Intel Report editor for the newsletter Air Mail.",
  "priority": "P2",
  "type": "article",
  "turn_to": null,
