  "title_cn": "人工智能热潮将如何终结？",
  "dek_cn": null,
  "body_cn": "我可以告诉你人工智能数据中心热潮大概会如何收场，但说不出具体时间。部署大型语言模型一直以来都是一种蛮力方法：英伟达的大型、快速且昂贵的GPU，利用海量内存，在液冷、能耗巨大的数据中心中处理一万亿个参数，所有这些都采用蛮力定价。这股味儿，像极了当年的IBM大型机。\n\n请谨慎对待对当前定价的任何推断。与所有新技术一样，人工智能也进入了改进阶段——就像大型机被更便宜的迷你计算机、微处理器、Web服务器以及现在的AI架构所取代一样。AI token价格一直在跌，但跌速会比多数人预想的更快。技术增长绝非直线。\n\n硅谷几十年来一直秉持着“更小、更便宜、更快、更好”的理念。我请风险投资家史蒂夫·尤维特森将这一理念转化为数字。“我们经常看到技术变革，当新方法好10倍时，就会发生。”它可以是性能提高10倍，或者成本降低10倍，有时两者兼而有之。他称之为“10倍法则”。\n\n而且这种情况正在发生。上周，根据OpenRouter的数据，OpenAI和Anthropic对其部分模型的定价较高，每百万输入token的价格为1到2美元。可与之媲美的中国开源权重模型，如智谱Z.ai的GLM和阿里巴巴的通义千问，定价为6到12美分，便宜约90%——这就是10倍法则的转折点。美国AI实验室的市场份额从去年的70%下降到今天的30%。所有实验室仍在增长。但价格很重要。\n\nAI架构正在演变。与其用一万亿个参数，不如用更高效的小型语言模型，比如700亿参数，并在苹果Mac上运行。这些本地模型没有token成本，除了硬件和电力的成本。但它们的能力肯定不如大型语言模型，对吧？并非如此。斯坦福大学的研究人员表明，本地运行的小型语言模型“可以成功回答88.7%的单轮对话和推理问题”。\n\n毫无疑问，我们始终需要高性能模型在数据中心中运行。IBM仍然销售大型机。但增长将发生在其他地方。在哪里？这是另一个硅谷的格言，我一直秉持：“智能正在向网络的边缘迁移。”真正的突破将发生在强大的AI模型在智能手机上运行时。这已经开始发生了。9月，加州PrismML发布了流行的开源通义千问3.8 27B AI模型的压缩版本。该公司将其压缩到小于6GB。这意味着它不仅可以运行在PC上，还可以运行在高端智能手机上。内存大小减少了大约9到10倍。这就是10倍法则。\n\n技术不会停滞不前。高通最近宣布推出适用于智能手机的新骁龙NPU。该公司称，通过使用所谓的“混合专家模型”，它可以在3GB内存中运行一个拥有300亿参数的AI模型。根据Deep View的数据，目前手机上最大的AI模型拥有40亿个参数，运行在苹果A20 Pro芯片上。高通提供了7.5倍的性能提升。\n\n需求不会很快放缓，但价格将开始以10倍的幅度下降。\n\n像高通新的骁龙和谷歌Pixel手机中的Tensor芯片这样的NPU正在不断涌现。同样，最新的苹果M系列和A系列芯片也是如此，这些芯片可以在iPhone上本地运行AI模型——也许这就是苹果没有加入AI数据中心支出狂潮的原因。\n\n一家由英伟达支持的初创公司Reflection AI声称，其AI模型与领先的中国开源AI模型相比，“推理计算量减少了3-4倍”。就这些吗？才3、4倍？等到10倍那天再叫醒我。以AI发展的速度，这可能在今年年底就能实现。\n\n如今，各种智能代理备受追捧，从Meta的Muse到xAI的Grokbot。它们运行在数据中心的虚拟机上，但也引发了各种隐私问题。初创公司Perplexity最近与英伟达合作，将智能代理部署在个人电脑上。无需支付token费用，而且您可以拥有自己的数据。\n\n我经常听到这样的说法：如果token费用降低，需求就会增加。这就是杰文斯悖论。当然，需求弹性是存在的。但如果价格下降90%，需求必须增加900%，才能保持不变。还记得互联网需求增长，而负债累累的Global Crossing公司却因价格下降过快而轰然倒闭吗？从历史上看，半导体每年的价格都会下降30%。当价格下降超过50%时，芯片制造厂关闭、公司倒闭等灾难接踵而至。\n\n2027年会是数据中心的顶峰吗？时间依然难测。目前需求火爆，但在多个10倍级改进落地、价格崩盘之前，支出就是一场“胆小鬼博弈”。这可能会引发数据中心停摆和债务违约的连锁反应，即使token需求增加。\n\n是的，模型训练、药物研发以及赢得像最近被解出的纳维-斯托克斯方程这类奖项，都需要数据中心里的高性能芯片。但聊天机器人、智能代理和其他任务可能会越来越多地在个人电脑、手机、智能眼镜以及未来十年硅谷所发明的各种新型设备上运行。\n\n我听说有人将此称为“大迁移”。尤维特森的“10倍法则”如今清晰可见，一如既往，却被人们忽视。变革不会一蹴而就，它会逐渐发生，然后突然爆发。\n\n请发送邮件至kessler@wsj.com。",
  "context_cn": "这是 Andy Kessler 在本报的固定专栏 Inside View。文章的核心论点是他所说的\"10 倍因子\"：技术变革往往在新方案便宜 10 倍时发生。作者引用 OpenRouter 的数据称，OpenAI 与 Anthropic 的收费为每百万输入 token 1 至 2 美元，而中国开源权重模型（如智谱的 GLM、阿里的 Qwen）仅 6 至 12 美分，便宜约 90%；美国 AI 实验室的市场份额已从去年的 70% 降至 30%。与此同时，小模型正在走向终端：加州公司 PrismML 把 Qwen3.8 27B 压缩到 6GB 以下，可在高端智能手机上运行。Kessler 警告，如果价格再跌 90%，需求必须增长 900% 才能持平，否则可能出现数据中心停建与债务违约潮——他把智能从数据中心向终端迁移的趋势称为\"大迁徙\"（Great Migration）。",
  "context_attr": "原文有"
 },
 {
  "id": "E2-018",
  "page": "A15",
  "section": "Opinion",
  "title": "Fusionism",
  "dek": "An Alliance Of Principles. Book: “Fusionism: Liberty, Virtue, and the Future of the American Right” by Stephanie Slade (Notre Dame, 304 pages, $30)",
  "byline": "Aurelian Craiutu (Bookshelf)",
  "body_en": "The name of Frank Meyer is rarely heard on the political right today. Younger conservatives in the 2020s won't know much about fusionism, the political outlook Meyer formulated—briefly, the idea that free markets, virtue and tradition can cohere with one another. Since his death in 1972, fusionism's heyday, it has gone out of fashion, replaced by a form of right-wing bellicosity with little patience for compromise.\n\nStephanie Slade's “Fusionism: Liberty, Virtue, and the Future of the American Right” explores the intellectual roots of fusionism and, by extension, American conservatism. The starting point is the great debate between traditionalists as defenders of order, morality, religion and virtue, and libertarians and classical liberals as advocates of the free market and individual rights. Over time, neither side achieved dominance within American conservatism; what emerged instead was a “fusion” of the two. Ms. Slade, an editor at Reason magazine, has given us an excellent account of a once-influential political outlook that deserves renewed attention.\n\nFusionism was given its first full expression in the Sharon Statement of 1960, drafted by young conservatives in the home of William F. Buckley Jr. in Sharon, Conn. They believed conservatives needed a solid philosophical and metaphysical foundation anchored in the principles of limited government, free markets and individual rights. For them, liberty and virtue were the two pillars on which any good society rests. We need freedom to be virtuous, the fusionists argued, and we require virtue to live happily and freely. “The task of the conservative movement in America,” Ms. Slade explains, “was to ‘harmonize’ social conservatism with free-market economics . . . because human flourishing is impossible without freedom as well as virtue.”\n\nFusionists were liberals, but their liberalism differed from that of the progressives. This is evident in their conception of the role of the state. In the fusionist view, the state can't be trusted with authority over economic, moral, religious or lifestyle questions. Its job is to keep individuals free so that they can pursue a life of virtue on their own. Fusionists were conservatives, but their traditionalism had little in common with the vision of ultraconservatives who opposed the values of the modern world.\n\nFusionism, Ms. Slade maintains, was not merely an alliance between factions for short-term political gain. It offered a coherent set of shared commitments with a pedigree that runs through the American founding. Russell Kirk and Friedrich Hayek held views that seem irreconcilable, but they both adhered more closely to fusionist principles than is commonly understood. The same goes for Milton Friedman, who remained in the fusionist orbit for a long time.\n\nAnother arguably fusionist figure discussed in the book is Ronald Reagan, who combined libertarian leanings with genuine appreciation for freedom anchored in virtue. Sadly, Reagan's worldview has fallen into disrepute among what Ms. Slade calls the Dissident Right. She devotes half of the book to an analysis of the current political landscape on the right and explains why Reagan's vision is deeply at odds with today's scorched-earth politics.\n\nPull quote: “A half-century ago, conservatives championed free markets and moral traditionalism. Much of today's right has abandoned that synthesis.”\n\nHow did the Republican Party go from Reagan to Donald Trump? Today's right-wing populism, Ms. Slade asserts, has roots in the post-Reagan years when a split emerged between neoconservatives, committed to free markets and global cooperation, and paleoconservatives, who embraced economic nationalism, protectionism and anti-immigrant policies. The latter hearkened back to an earlier America First isolationism and introduced coarseness into political debates, vilifying adversaries and dissidents within their own camp. In Ms. Slade's assessment, the face of the paleocon movement was “Pitchfork Pat” Buchanan, whose militant and isolationist worldview reached its maturity in the MAGA movement. “The paleocons were concerned less with keeping the government small,” she writes, “than with ensuring it be used to promote the right causes and help the right people.”\n\nSix decades ago, Meyer worried that a heresy had crept into Republican thinking, one that demanded election victories at all costs and that placed no importance on principle or policy. If he were alive today, he would not be pleased with his friends. The Dissident Right exploits grievance and status resentments triggered by immigration and economic hardship. Its leaders declare opponents traitors and denounce compromise, gradualism and moderation as signs of weakness. The Dissident Right, Ms. Slade persuasively shows, believes that our system of checks and balances and limited government is rotten and must give way to a “Caesarist” approach to executive power. Self-described conservatives jettison free-market policies and embrace big deficits and government expenditures for the benefit of Republican voting constituencies; their aim isn't to shrink the state or limit its purview but to control and expand it for nakedly partisan purposes.\n\nCould an updated form of fusionism ameliorate some of these pathologies? Nobody knows for sure. Breathing life into the liberty-virtue synthesis might require relinquishing the vocabulary of fusionism. Perhaps a new type of conservatism will emerge that reflects and responds to the complexity and tensions of modern life.\n\nMs. Slade believes that there is a kernel of fusionism in almost all of us. She may be right. It is impossible, in the 21st century, to identify a single overriding interest or value that ought alone to govern social life, whether freedom, virtue, equality or efficiency. During the Cold War, as collectivism made advances in all areas of life, individualism made sense as a lodestar. But we live in a multiplicity of realms—social, economic, cultural, political, religious—that follow their own logic and cannot be reduced to a single principle. What's needed is a balance between rival values and principles that is flexible, moderate and resistant to doctrinal purity. Ms. Slade's history of fusionism might help conservatives practice this art of balance in dark times.\n\nMr. Craiutu is a professor of political science at Indiana University, Bloomington.",
  "priority": "P2",
  "type": "opinion",
