  "title": "What Are U.S. Soldiers Doing in Ecuador?",
  "dek": null,
  "byline": "Mary Anastasia O'Grady (Americas)",
  "body_en": "News that U.S. soldiers were deployed to Ecuador last month to fight drug-trafficking cartels deserves more attention than it has received. A trademark pledge of President Trump is to stay out of “endless wars.” Yet if ever there were a conflict destined to go on forever, it's the armed effort to defeat the suppliers of mind-altering substances in the face of robust consumer demand.\n\nThis isn't to dismiss out of hand the Shield of the Americas, the new U.S. regional security initiative under which the Ecuador mission was launched. The State Department says the goal of the hemispheric project, unveiled in March, is to pull together “likeminded allies” to “work together to advance strategies that stop foreign interference in our hemisphere, criminal and narco-terrorist gangs and cartels, and illegal and mass immigration.”\n\nEnhanced cooperation on the security interests of pro-Western countries in the hemisphere is a welcome development. To the extent it's possible for the U.S. to assist neighbors in recovering democratic institutions that have been lost to organized crime, the initiative has promise. But if the Americas Counter Cartel Coalition, operating under the “shield,” requires sending American soldiers to fight in the jungles of far-flung lands, it will tread on dangerous ground.\n\nThe U.S. successfully supported Colombia and President Álvaro Uribe in that country's war against narco-trafficking rebels in the 2000s by helping with intelligence, equipment and training and by sending in military advisers. It never committed troops.\n\nA growing number of countries now welcome security help from the U.S. because armed groups have overwhelmed national police and military. The problems go well beyond narcotics production and trafficking. Criminal networks rule swaths of weak countries. They run extortion and kidnapping rackets, collect taxes, interfere with elections, traffic people, intimidate the political class with threats of violence, and run illegal gold-mining operations that trample the rights of mining companies and rural communities.\n\nQuito is one of 19 governments that have joined the shield. Argentina, Bolivia, Chile, Colombia, Costa Rica, Guatemala, Honduras, Peru and Panama are members. Four Caribbean countries, including the Dominican Republic, are in the group. Most member governments are center-right. Guatemala is one notable exception. All have reason to worry about threats to democracy from transnational criminal networks.\n\nOver the past decade drug traffickers searching for the path of least resistance from South America to the north have gravitated to Ecuador. Once a small-bore banana republic marked by political instability, its weak institutions, rampant corruption and attractive location made it a target for gangsters looking to create a transit hub. It is now an organized-crime hot spot.\n\nQuito wants help in curbing cartel violence, but demand for drugs persists. As cartel violence has intensified and security has deteriorated, Ecuadorians have desperately sought a way back to the old tranquility they remember. President Daniel Noboa won a special election in 2023 and a regular election in 2025 by promising to crack down on crime. But progress has been slow. The arrival of U.S. forces raised hopes that the tide will soon turn. In raids last month, Ecuadorian forces, fighting alongside Americans, killed three alleged narcos and captured dozens more.\n\nYet the broader strategy is unclear. Mr. Uribe's triumph in Colombia over the guerrillas and the paramilitary didn't end the flow of cocaine to the U.S. One reason is that American demand for narcotics is inelastic: Even when the white hats reduce supply and prices go up, consumers keep buying. The net effect is that cartel income increases.\n\nIllicit groups aren't about to abandon their profitable businesses. But they can be cut down to size. The difficulty is finding a way to minimize their capacity to control territory without trampling republican values. Mr. Uribe managed that. His government re-established its authority across the country and restored it to its proper function of defending life and property without destroying democracy. Contrast that with El Salvador, which has gone the strongman route, employing high-powered weapons and sweeping lockups with no due process and at the expense of pluralism. Freedom has been exchanged for peace. Some political opponents of President Nayib Bukele have fled into exile. Sadly, the Trump administration celebrates Mr. Bukele.\n\nEcuador could come out a winner if it can copy Colombia and avoid the error of El Salvador. If the costs of operating in Ecuador go high enough, gangsters might even leave, establishing routes through different countries. It's what regional analysts call the “balloon effect.” The trouble is it doesn't do much for the Americas. To reach a lasting peace, the region will do better to emphasize open markets, deregulation, transparency, independent institutions that check the concentration of power, and efforts to professionalize the military. That's a hemispheric cause worth fighting for.\n\nWrite to O'Grady@wsj.com.",
  "priority": "P2",
  "type": "opinion",
  "turn_to": null,
  "terms": [
   {
    "en": "Shield of the Americas",
    "zh_suggested": "美洲之盾",
    "note": "U.S. regional security initiative"
   },
