  "final_id": "060",
  "title_cn": "美国士兵在厄瓜多尔做什么？",
  "dek_cn": null,
  "body_cn": "上个月，美国向厄瓜多尔部署士兵，以打击贩毒集团，这一消息值得更多关注，但目前关注度不足。特朗普总统一直强调的一项重要承诺是，避免卷入“无休止的战争”。然而，如果说存在一场注定会持续下去的冲突，那就是通过武装力量，在面对强劲的消费需求时，打击那些提供令人上瘾物质的供应者。\n\n这并不是要完全否定“美洲之盾”，即美国发起的一项新的区域安全倡议，厄瓜多尔的任务就是在该倡议框架下启动的。美国国务院表示，这项于三月份公布的半球项目旨在团结“志同道合的盟友”，共同“努力推进战略，以阻止外国干涉我们半球，打击犯罪和贩毒恐怖主义团伙以及贩毒集团，并遏制非法的大规模移民”。\n\n加强在半球内，对亲西方国家安全利益的合作，是一项值得欢迎的进展。在一定程度上，如果美国能够帮助邻国恢复因有组织犯罪而丧失的民主制度，这项倡议将具有积极意义。但是，如果“美洲反卡特尔联盟”在“美洲之盾”的框架下运作，需要派遣美国士兵前往偏远地区的丛林中作战，那么它将触及危险的领域。\n\n2000年代，美国成功地支持哥伦比亚和阿尔瓦罗·乌里韦总统，通过提供情报、设备和培训，并派遣军事顾问，帮助哥伦比亚打击贩毒叛乱分子。但美国从未派遣军队。\n\n现在，越来越多的国家欢迎美国提供安全援助，因为武装团伙已经压倒了国家警察和军队。问题远不止于毒品生产和贩运。犯罪网络控制着许多薄弱国家的广大地区。它们从事敲诈勒索和绑架活动，收取税款，干涉选举，贩卖人口，以暴力威胁恐吓政治阶层，并进行非法采矿活动，侵犯了矿业公司和农村社区的权利。\n\n基多是加入“美洲之盾”的19个政府之一。阿根廷、玻利维亚、智利、哥伦比亚、哥斯达黎加、危地马拉、洪都拉斯、秘鲁和巴拿马都是成员国。四个加勒比国家，包括多米尼加共和国，也加入了该组织。大多数成员政府都是中右翼。危地马拉是一个值得注意的例外。所有这些国家都有理由担心来自跨国犯罪网络的对民主的威胁。\n\n在过去十年中，为了寻找从南美到北美的最容易的路径，贩毒分子逐渐将目光投向了厄瓜多尔。厄瓜多尔曾经是个政治动荡、不起眼的香蕉共和国，其薄弱的机构、猖獗的腐败和优越的地理位置使其成为犯罪分子建立中转枢纽的目标。现在，它已经成为一个有组织犯罪的热点地区。\n\n基多希望获得援助，以遏制贩毒集团的暴力活动，但对毒品的需求依然存在。随着贩毒集团的暴力活动加剧，安全局势恶化，厄瓜多尔人民迫切希望恢复他们记忆中的平静。丹尼尔·诺沃亚总统在2023年赢得特别选举，并在2025年赢得普通选举，承诺打击犯罪。但进展缓慢。美国军队的到来，让人们对局势即将好转抱有希望。上个月，在一次突击行动中，厄瓜多尔军队与美国军队并肩作战，击毙了三名涉嫌贩毒分子，并逮捕了数十人。\n\n然而，更广泛的战略尚不明确。阿尔瓦罗·乌里韦在哥伦比亚对付游击队和准军事组织时取得的胜利，并没有阻止可卡因流入美国。其中一个原因是，美国对毒品的需求缺乏弹性：即使正义之师减少供应，价格上涨，消费者仍然会继续购买。最终结果是，贩毒集团的收入增加。\n\n这些非法组织不会轻易放弃其有利可图的业务。但可以削弱它们的力量。难点在于，如何在不损害共和价值观的前提下，最大限度地减少它们控制领土的能力。阿尔瓦罗·乌里韦成功地做到了这一点。他的政府在全国范围内重建了权威，并使其恢复到保护生命和财产的应有功能，同时没有破坏民主。与此形成对比的是萨尔瓦多，它选择了强人路线，使用高杀伤力武器，进行大规模逮捕，没有经过正当程序，并以牺牲多元主义为代价。自由被和平所取代。一些纳伊布·布克莱总统的政治对手已经流亡海外。令人遗憾的是，特朗普政府赞扬了纳伊布·布克莱。\n\n如果厄瓜多尔能够效仿哥伦比亚，并避免重蹈萨尔瓦多的覆辙，它可能会从中获益。如果厄瓜多尔的运营成本足够高，犯罪分子甚至可能会离开，并建立通过其他国家的路线。区域分析师称之为“气球效应”。问题在于，这对整个美洲并没有带来太大的好处。为了实现持久的和平，该地区最好强调开放市场、放松管制、透明度、独立的机构，以制衡权力的集中，以及努力实现军队的专业化。这是一项值得为之奋斗的半球性事业。\n\n请致信 O'Grady@wsj.com。",
  "context_cn": null,
  "context_attr": null
 },
 {
  "id": "E2-020",
  "page": "A15",
  "section": "Opinion",
  "title": "Will the FTC Protect You From Discounts?",
  "dek": null,
  "byline": "Mary Sullivan",
  "body_en": "The Federal Trade Commission seems poised to join the backlash against personalized pricing. That would be a mistake. The practice remains poorly understood.\n\nPersonalized pricing, or its more sinister name, “surveillance pricing,” is a method of setting prices whereby companies use personal data to make individualized offers. According to a new FTC proposal, personalized pricing likely constitutes an “unfair or deceptive” practice unless the seller discloses “the fact that the price is personalized, the basis of that personalization, and the type of data used.”\n\nThe agency's conclusion is premature and relies on incomplete information. Don't take my word for it. The FTC concedes as much, stating in its August enforcement bulletin that “the extent to which businesses currently use personalized pricing is not well understood, and the effects of personalized pricing on consumers are unclear.”\n\nNor do we have a grasp of its prevalence. Remarkably, the FTC's proposal doesn't cite a single instance of a company's using personalized pricing.\n\nThe commission rushes to regulate personalized pricing.\n\nRather, it relies on hypotheticals designed to make the practice appear harmful to consumers. Consider a grocery chain charging more for milk to a family with children. A hotel could charge a higher price for a person traveling for a funeral. These scenarios strain credulity. The FTC should develop its enforcement policy around real-world behavior, not imagined harms.\n\nAccording to the theory of perfect price discrimination, a monopolist charges each consumer the maximum price that he is willing to pay for a good or service. This would make consumers worse off because all the gains from trade go to the seller.\n\nIn reality, even a monopolist wouldn't be able to extract all consumer surplus, because of the presence of alternatives and the inability to fully know consumers' willingness to pay. With personalized pricing, some buyers would see higher prices and others lower ones. The overall effect on consumer welfare is an empirical question.\n\nSo far, only one study, from 2022, has directly examined it with a pricing field experiment. Researchers at the University of Chicago found personalized pricing could benefit many consumers. While the practice raised prices for some, 60% were better off. They received individualized offers that were lower than the previous uniform price.\n\nThese results should give the FTC reason to pause any proposed enforcement and first develop a better understanding of personalized pricing. Instead of caricaturing how firms set prices, the commission should grapple with how the practice actually works. Rather than assume personalized pricing is always bad for consumers, it should account for the possible benefits suggested by scholars. Our limited knowledge provides little justification for rushing forward with policies that could effectively ban personalized pricing—a practice whose potential upside we have yet to fully understand.\n\nMs. Sullivan, a former FTC economist, is a visiting scholar at the George Washington University Regulatory Studies Center.",
  "priority": "P2",
  "type": "opinion",
  "turn_to": null,
  "terms": [
   {
    "en": "Federal Trade Commission",
    "zh_suggested": "美国联邦贸易委员会",
    "note": "FTC"
   },
   {
    "en": "personalized pricing",
    "zh_suggested": "个性化定价"
   },
   {
    "en": "surveillance pricing",
    "zh_suggested": "监控定价"
   },
   {
    "en": "perfect price discrimination",
    "zh_suggested": "完全价格歧视"
   },
   {
    "en": "George Washington University Regulatory Studies Center",
    "zh_suggested": "乔治·华盛顿大学监管研究中心"
   }
  ],
  "final_id": "061",
  "title_cn": "联邦贸易委员会会“保护”你不受折扣之害吗？",
  "dek_cn": null,
  "body_cn": "美国联邦贸易委员会似乎准备加入反对“个性化定价”的行列。这将是一个错误。目前，人们对这种做法的理解还很有限。\n\n“个性化定价”，或者说它更具负面色彩的称谓“监控定价”，是一种定价方法，即公司利用个人数据来制定个性化的报价。根据美国联邦贸易委员会的一项新提案，“个性化定价”很可能构成一种“不公平或具有欺骗性”的做法，除非销售商披露“价格是根据个人情况定制的这一事实，以及定制价格的依据和所使用的数据类型”。\n\n该机构的结论过于仓促，并且基于不完整的信息。请不要只听我的，美国联邦贸易委员会也承认了这一点，在其 8 月发布的执法公告中指出，“目前企业使用‘个性化定价’的程度尚不清楚，‘个性化定价’对消费者的影响也尚不明确”。\n\n我们对这种做法的普遍程度也缺乏了解。值得注意的是，美国联邦贸易委员会的提案中没有引用任何一家公司使用“个性化定价”的实例。\n\n该委员会急于对“个性化定价”进行监管。\n\n相反，它依赖于一些假设，旨在使这种做法看起来对消费者有害。例如，一家连锁超市向有孩子的家庭收取更高的牛奶价格；一家酒店可以向因参加葬礼而旅行的人收取更高的价格。这些情景都难以令人信服。美国联邦贸易委员会应该根据现实世界的行为来制定其执法政策，而不是基于想象中的危害。\n\n根据“完全价格歧视”的理论，一家垄断企业会对每位消费者收取他们愿意为某种商品或服务支付的最高价格。这将使消费者的情况变得更糟，因为所有贸易收益都归销售商所有。\n\n实际上，即使是一家垄断企业也无法榨取所有消费者的剩余价值，因为存在替代品，并且无法完全了解消费者的支付意愿。通过“个性化定价”，一些买家的价格会更高，而另一些买家的价格会更低。这种做法对消费者整体福利的影响是一个需要通过实证研究来回答的问题。\n\n到目前为止，只有一项研究，即2022年的一项研究，直接通过定价领域实验对其进行了考察。芝加哥大学的研究人员发现，个性化定价可以使许多消费者受益。虽然这种做法提高了部分消费者的价格，但有60%的消费者认为情况有所改善。他们收到了个性化的优惠，这些优惠低于之前的统一价格。\n\n这些结果应该促使美国联邦贸易委员会暂停任何拟议的执法行动，并首先深入了解个性化定价。委员会不应简单地对企业如何制定价格进行片面描述，而应深入研究这种做法的实际运作方式。与其假设个性化定价总是对消费者不利，不如考虑学者们提出的这种做法可能带来的好处。我们有限的知识无法为匆忙推行可能实际上禁止个性化定价的政策提供充分的理由——我们尚未完全了解这种做法可能带来的潜在好处。\n\n苏利文女士是一位前美国联邦贸易委员会经济学家，目前是乔治·华盛顿大学监管研究中心的客座学者。",
  "context_cn": null,
  "context_attr": null
 },
 {
  "id": "E2-021",
  "page": "A16",
  "section": "Opinion",
  "title": "Trump’s Diesel Insult to Lindsey Graham",
  "dek": null,
  "byline": "Review & Outlook (lead editorial)",
  "body_en": "The world misses Lindsey Graham, and never more than now as President Trump sides more decidedly with Vladimir Putin in his war against Ukraine and Europe. The late Senator might have been able to persuade the President to avoid this moral and strategic mistake.\n\nMr. Trump announced late Friday that the U.S. will buy up to 4.8 million tons (nearly 36 million barrels) of Russian diesel fuel in stages. That includes an “immediate” supply of 300,000 tons, another 500,000 in November, and one million “immediately thereafter.”\n\nThe delivery of another three million tons is “based on the condition of their Diesel Refineries,” Mr. Trump wrote on Truth Social. The U.S. Treasury lifted some sanctions on Russia through April 7 so it could export the diesel.\n\n“Between our TOTAL CONTROL of the Strait of Hormuz, and this great announcement on Russian Energy, Diesel Prices for Americans and, indeed, the World, will be COMING DOWN, IN RECORD NUMBERS, AND FAST!” Mr. Trump wrote on Truth Social.\n\nThis is supposed to help Republicans in the November elections that are three weeks away. But 36 million barrels of Russian diesel won't move prices much. The world consumed 28 million barrels of diesel and other distillates daily in 2025, according to OPIS, which is part of Dow Jones Energy.\n\nWhat the deal will do is directly finance Mr. Putin's bloody war in Ukraine. And it may be worse. The Financial Times reported Saturday that the Trump Administration “threatened to cut off vital intelligence sharing” to Kyiv if it continues attacking Russian refineries.\n\nIn September Mr. Trump blamed Ukraine for causing diesel-fuel shortages with its long-range strikes on Russian refineries. Never mind Iranian and Houthi strikes on Middle Eastern energy production and transport. Russia helps Iran with intelligence and weapons.\n\nOn Sunday Mr. Trump announced what he called “an ENERGY CEASEFIRE in the War between Russia and Ukraine. Both parties have agreed.” Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky said Ukraine supports such a cease-fire if Russia does the same, but as we write this Russia hasn't commented.\n\nAn energy cease-fire would help both sides, as Ukraine faces another harsh winter and Russian refineries are under siege. But what about Mr. Putin's relentless attacks on Ukrainian cities?\n\nHis deal with Putin helps the Kremlin's war on Ukraine's cities.\n\nOn Wednesday 22 people, including five children, were killed in a Russian cruise missile strike on an apartment building in the city of Pryluky. The next day a glide bomb hit two buses in Kramatorsk, killing 33. After the diesel announcement, Russia launched glide bombs Saturday at a residential area of Zaporizhzhia, killing at least 20, including three children. Without a complete cease-fire, Russia will continue to torment Ukrainian civilians.\n\nThe strategic turn here is hard to understand. Only last month Mr. Trump signed Lindsey Graham's bill to allow new sanctions and tariffs on countries that buy Russian oil and gas. Now Mr. Trump has decided to buy Russian diesel and lift some current U.S. sanctions. The U.S. has been browbeating Europe, India and China not to buy Russian oil, and so much for all that diplomatic effort.\n\nThe Kremlin has been under financial pressure from Western sanctions and Ukraine's ability to hit targets in Russia. The Centre for Research on Energy and Clean Air estimates Russian oil companies will earn some $4.5 billion from the diesel deal.\n\nMr. Trump added to the pressure on Ukraine by saying it's “time for Ukraine to get a new president.” But he'll have to search far and wide for anyone in Ukraine who supports financing Vladimir Putin.\n\nMr. Trump complained again on the weekend that Mr. Zelensky could have settled the war many times. Yes, Kyiv could always surrender. Ukrainians prefer to fight for their independence and freedom. Eyeing a Nobel peace prize, Mr. Trump wants to broker a Russia-Ukraine peace, but the only pressure he ever applies is on Ukraine.\n\nHis multiple attempts at appeasing Mr. Putin with summitry, flattery, offers of new investment, and more haven't moved the Russian. On the weekend a Russian official said the diesel deal doesn't change Mr. Putin's determination to achieve his war aim, which is to turn Ukraine into a Kremlin satrapy. Until that changes, Mr. Trump's diesel deal helps Russia and not the cause of peace.",
  "priority": "P1",
  "type": "opinion",
  "turn_to": null,
  "terms": [
   {
    "en": "Lindsey Graham",
    "zh_suggested": "林赛·格雷厄姆",
