 {
  "id": "E2-033",
  "page": "A17",
  "section": "Opinion",
  "title": "The ‘Scientific’ Socialism of Abdul El-Sayed",
  "dek": null,
  "byline": "Allysia Finley (Life Science)",
  "body_en": "Apparently, “following the science” means embracing socialism. So argues Abdul El-Sayed, the left-wing Democrat running for Senate in Michigan.\n\n“Once you train in the sciences, you don't leave that scientific approach to thinking and that rigor of thinking. And I try to bring that to policy problems,” Mr. El-Sayed, who has a medical degree but has never practiced as a physician, told Scientific American. “You construct a hypothesis of the world and you test it . . . and then you'll study the outcome of the intervention to see if it works.”\n\nA drug maker would scrap an experimental medicine that fails to help patients and causes severe adverse effects. Mr. El-Sayed and his fellow Democrats want to rerun failed policy experiments at a higher dosage.\n\nConsider taxes. Most European countries that have imposed wealth taxes—including Austria, Denmark, Germany, Sweden and France—repealed them because they prompted high earners to flee. Before France's repeal in 2018, a net 580 households subject to the tax left each year on average. After repeal, many returned.\n\nProgressives claim the wealthy are impervious to higher taxes. So why has a stream of Cessnas vamoosed from California, New York and New Jersey to zero-income tax Florida, Texas and Nevada?\n\nA 2013 study of European soccer players found countries with lower tax rates attracted the most talented foreign players. Irony alert: One of the study's authors was left-wing economist Emmanuel Saez, an architect of a California wealth tax ballot measure, who advocates drowning the rich to pay for cradle-to-grave states. Democrats are on board.\n\nMr. Sayed has endorsed a wealth tax on billionaires and even those with smaller fortunes. He also wants to tax capital gains above $1 million at the same rate as ordinary income, remove the $184,500 income cap on the 12.4% Social Security payroll tax and boost the top marginal personal income-tax rate (now 37%), though he doesn't specify how high.\n\nSuch ideas are mainstream in the Democratic Party. New Jersey Sen. Cory Booker has proposed raising the top income-tax rate to 43%. Maryland Sen. Chris Van Hollen bids 49%. Uncapping the Social Security tax would boost the marginal tax rate for higher earners by another 12.4%, putting the total top federal rate in the neighborhood of 60%.\n\nThrow in state taxes, and the wealthy in some states would be paying north of 70% of their income to the government. The top rates in the U.K. (45%), Germany (47.5%) and France (55.4%) would be a relative bargain. Watch as professional basketball players jump to European leagues.\n\nSoaking the rich hasn't solved the fiscal problems of European welfare states. Instead, it has stunted economic growth and, in turn, tax revenue, resulting in a lower standard of living and shoddy public services. Witness the recent student-led protests in France over the rickety infrastructure in public schools. Its national healthcare system is similarly dilapidated. Seniors may not have the energy to take to the streets because their hips are failing and the government won't pay for a replacement. They're going to die soon anyway, so why spend the money? Better to spend it on subsidizing idle young people—France's youth unemployment rate tops 20%—who can't find jobs because of stultifying taxes, regulations and labor rules.\n\nSingle-payer healthcare has proved itself a failure, so he wants to rerun the experiment on America.\n\nMr. El-Sayed and his socialist compatriots present “Medicare for all”—a euphemism for a single-payer system—as a healthcare nirvana. The Democratic candidate's idealized system has no copays, deductibles, premiums or provider network. It promises free healthcare on demand from any doctor. Such a system doesn't exist anywhere in the world. Medicare requires premiums, copays and deductibles, and as Mr. El-Sayed acknowledged, “as it operates right now, there is some subcontracting to private companies.” About half of Medicare beneficiaries are enrolled in privately administered Advantage plans, which are less expensive than traditional fee-for-service because insurers do a better job than the government of controlling waste and fraud. Democrats want to eliminate the Advantage program and move entirely to a fee-for-service system. If you like your Medicare plan, don't expect to keep it.\n\nMost countries fund their government-run systems with payroll and value-added taxes, so that people pay for healthcare by having less disposable income. And by enduring delays. Government-run health systems are inefficient and stingy. Result: longer waits for appointments with procedures, if rationing bodies will even approve them.\n\nAbout 17% of patients in the U.S. wait more than two months for an appointment with a specialist, which is significantly less than in socialized-medicine utopias like Canada (48%), France (45%) and the U.K. (43%). Government-run healthcare means less access, not more.\n\nDemocrats are unlikely to ban private insurance in the short term, if only because this would be unpopular with unions that have Cadillac plans. The bigger danger is they muck up private insurance with more regulation as they try to fix the unintended effects of their disastrous ObamaCare experiment.",
  "priority": "P2",
  "type": "opinion",
  "turn_to": null,
  "terms": [
   {
    "en": "Abdul El-Sayed",
    "zh_suggested": "阿卜杜勒·埃尔-赛义德",
    "note": "Michigan Senate candidate"
   },
   {
    "en": "Scientific American",
    "zh_suggested": "《科学美国人》"
   },
   {
    "en": "Emmanuel Saez",
    "zh_suggested": "埃马纽埃尔·塞斯"
   },
   {
    "en": "Cory Booker",
    "zh_suggested": "科里·布克",
    "note": "Senator (D., N.J.)"
   },
   {
    "en": "Chris Van Hollen",
    "zh_suggested": "克里斯·范霍伦",
    "note": "Senator (D., Md.)"
   },
   {
    "en": "Medicare for all",
    "zh_suggested": "全民医保"
   },
   {
    "en": "Allysia Finley",
    "zh_suggested": "阿利西娅·芬利",
    "note": "待查, WSJ columnist"
   }
  ],
  "final_id": "074",
  "title_cn": "阿卜杜勒·埃尔-赛义德的“科学”社会主义",
  "dek_cn": null,
  "body_cn": "显然，“遵循科学”意味着拥抱社会主义。这是阿卜杜勒·埃尔-赛义德的观点，他是一位左翼民主党人，正在竞选密歇根州的参议员职位。\n\n阿卜杜勒·埃尔-赛义德对《科学美国人》表示：“一旦你接受了科学训练，你就不会放弃这种科学的思维方式和严谨的思考方法。我试图将这种方法应用于政策问题。”他拥有医学学位，但从未从事过医生职业。他说：“你构建一个关于世界的假设，然后进行验证……然后，你将研究干预措施的结果，以了解它是否有效。”\n\n一家制药公司会放弃一种未能帮助患者并导致严重不良反应的实验药物。阿卜杜勒·埃尔-赛义德和他的民主党同僚希望以更高的剂量重新进行失败的政策实验。\n\n以税收为例。大多数已经征收财富税的欧洲国家——包括奥地利、丹麦、德国、瑞典和法国——都取消了这种税收，因为这促使高收入者逃离。在法国于2018年取消财富税之前，平均每年有580户需要缴纳该税的家庭离开。取消后，许多人返回。\n\n进步人士声称，富人对更高的税收毫不在意。那么，为什么会有源源不断的塞斯纳飞机从加利福尼亚州、纽约州和新泽西州飞往没有收入税的佛罗里达州、德克萨斯州和内华达州呢？\n\n2013年的一项关于欧洲足球运动员的研究发现，税率较低的国家吸引了最多的外国优秀球员。值得注意的是，这项研究的作者之一是左翼经济学家埃马纽埃尔·塞斯，他是一位加利福尼亚州财富税提案的设计者，他主张狠狠地向富人征税，以资助从摇篮到坟墓的社会福利体系。民主党人对此表示支持。\n\n阿卜杜勒·埃尔-赛义德支持对亿万富翁甚至拥有较小财富的人征收财富税。他还希望对超过100万美元的资本收益按与普通收入相同的税率征税，取消12.4%社会保障工资税的184,500美元收入上限，并提高最高的个人所得税率（目前为37%），但他没有说明具体提高多少。\n\n这些想法在民主党内是主流观点。新泽西州参议员科里·布克提议将最高的所得税率提高到43%。马里兰州参议员克里斯·范霍伦提议提高到49%。取消社会保障税的上限将使高收入者的边际税率再提高12.4%，使最高的联邦税率达到60%左右。\n\n如果再加上州税，那么在一些州，富人将不得不向政府缴纳超过 70% 的收入。与英国（45%）、德国（47.5%）和法国（55.4%）的最高税率相比，这简直是小便宜。看看职业篮球运动员如何涌向欧洲联赛。\n\n对富人征收重税并没有解决欧洲福利国家的财政问题。相反，它阻碍了经济增长，进而影响了税收收入，导致生活水平下降和公共服务质量下降。看看最近法国学生发起的抗议活动，他们抗议公共学校基础设施的破败。该国的国家医疗保健系统也同样状况不佳。老年人可能没有精力走上街头，因为他们的髋关节功能衰退，而且政府不会为他们支付更换费用。他们反正很快就要去世了，所以为什么要花这笔钱呢？不如把钱花在补贴那些无所事事的年轻人身上——法国的青年失业率超过 20%——他们因为过高的税收、繁琐的法规和劳动规则而找不到工作。\n\n单一支付医疗保健模式已经被证明是失败的，所以他想在美国重试。\n\n阿卜杜勒·埃尔-赛义德和他的社会主义同伴将“全民医保”——一种单一支付制度的委婉说法——描述为一种理想的医疗保健模式。这位民主党候选人所设想的理想制度没有任何自付额、免赔额、保费或服务网络。它承诺可以随时从任何医生那里获得免费的医疗保健。这种制度在世界上任何地方都不存在。医疗保险需要支付保费、自付额和免赔额，而且正如阿卜杜勒·埃尔-赛义德所承认的那样，“目前，它确实存在一些外包给私营公司的情况。”大约一半的医疗保险受益人参加了由私营公司管理的“优势计划”，这些计划比传统的按服务收费模式更便宜，因为保险公司比政府更能有效地控制浪费和欺诈行为。民主党人希望取消“优势计划”，并完全转向按服务收费模式。如果你喜欢你的医疗保险计划，不要指望能够继续保留它。\n\n大多数国家通过工资税和增值税来资助其政府运营的系统，这样人们就可以通过减少可支配收入来支付医疗保健费用。而且还要忍受延误。政府运营的医疗保健系统效率低下且吝啬。结果是：预约和治疗的等待时间更长，如果配给机构批准的话。\n\n在美国，大约 17% 的患者需要等待两个多月才能预约专科医生，这远低于加拿大（48%）、法国（45%）和英国（43%）等实行社会化医疗的理想国。政府运营的医疗保健意味着更少的医疗服务，而不是更多的医疗服务。\n\n民主党人不太可能在短期内禁止私人保险，原因之一是这样做会不受工会的欢迎，因为工会拥有高福利的保险计划。更大的危险是，他们试图解决其灾难性的奥巴马医改实验所产生的意外后果，从而对私人保险进行更多的监管，从而破坏了私人保险。",
  "context_cn": null,
  "context_attr": null
 },
 {
  "id": "E2-034",
  "page": "A17",
  "section": "Opinion",
  "title": "Republican Senate Losses Will Hurt Long After Trump",
  "dek": null,
  "byline": "Matthew Continetti",
  "body_en": "Republicans are bracing for an ugly November. The GOP House majority looks lost, and Democrats are increasingly confident of capturing the Senate. Divided government lies ahead, along with obstruction and investigations. “I'm going to be impeached,” President Trump told South Carolina voters in August. “They're going to impeach me. They have no idea why.”\n\nImpeachment may be the least of Mr. Trump's worries. After all, he's been through it twice. What he hasn't experienced is full Democratic control of Congress. It would make all his problems worse.\n\nThe president might believe he would be able to dismiss a Speaker Hakeem Jeffries as a nuisance, as he did Speaker Nancy Pelosi in his first term. He won't have that luxury if Chuck Schumer is also Senate majority leader. A Democratic Senate would be impossible to ignore.\n\nNor would the pain end for Republicans when Mr. Trump leaves office. Senate elections have a long tail. Six-year Senate terms are spread across presidential administrations, giving senators many chances to serve in the majority, influence policy and cast defining votes.\n\nThe senators who win this year won't be up for re-election until 2032, the end of the next presidential term. They will become the base of support for, or a bulwark against, the legislation and appointments of Mr. Trump's successor. Some may try to become that successor.\n\nThis has happened before. Democrats netted six seats in 2006 and gained Senate control. Sherrod Brown, Bob Casey Jr., Claire McCaskill, Jon Tester, Jim Webb and Sheldon Whitehouse opposed George W. Bush during his last years in office, for sure. But their true impact wasn't felt until 2009, when they voted for President Obama's stimulus bill and ObamaCare.\n\nConversely, Republicans gained nine seats and a Senate majority in 2014. The class of '14 included conservative stars Ben Sasse, Tom Cotton and Joni Ernst, who helped sustain Majority Leader Mitch McConnell's 2016 hold on Merrick Garland's nomination to replace Justice Antonin Scalia. And when Donald Trump became president, they all voted for Mr. Trump's Supreme Court nominees and the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act.\n\nEven small gains in the Senate can have major consequences. Democrats won two Senate seats in 2018 (while losing four), yet Kyrsten Sinema and Jacky Rosen went on to vote for President Biden's spending deluge. Democrats won both Georgia Senate seats amid the 2020 election debacle—aiding Mr. Biden and providing a presidential springboard for Jon Ossoff.\n\nThough Republicans lost a Senate seat in 2022, one GOP senator elected that year found himself as Mr. Trump's vice president in 2025, casting the tie-breaking vote for the One Big Beautiful Bill Act. Americans who cast votes this year in protest might wind up launching the career of the Democrats' JD Vance. Also recall 2004, when an open Senate seat in Illinois served as the launch vehicle for Mr. Obama.\n\nNew members of the upper chamber will remain there through the first term of the president's successor. Here's what a Democratic Senate might do: Not only would Democrats stop Mr. Trump's legislative agenda. They would amplify the progressive tax, spending and regulatory bills that come from a Democratic House. President Trump's veto pen would be busy.\n\nAt the same time, Senate Democrats would block presidential appointments. The Supreme Court wouldn't be exempt. Democrats have been thirsting for revenge since Sen. McConnell blocked Mr. Garland's nomination. Winning the Senate in November would give them that chance in the event of a vacancy.\n\nThwarting Mr. Trump would be only the start. Democrats in Congress will use policy and messaging to soften the ground for the 2028 presidential campaign. And if a Democrat wins in 2028, he could, like every first-term president since Bill Clinton, take office with a unified government. Then the conservative nightmare begins.\n\nBy 2029, potential Democratic members of the class of 2026—Roy Cooper, Mr. Brown, Mary Peltola, Josh Turek, Abdul El-Sayed and company—would all be ensconced in the Dirksen, Russell and Hart office buildings. They'd be eager to cast votes for the next Sonia Sotomayor or Ketanji Brown Jackson. They'd be champing at the bit to end the legislative filibuster. That would open the door for bills to override state-based election laws, including voter ID, to impose term limits on or add seats to the Supreme Court, and grant statehood to the District of Columbia and Puerto Rico. Senators elected under the banner of “affordability” would be positioned to raise taxes, limit oil and gas production, expand government and muck with health insurance—a lefty free-for-all.\n\nA Senate election has lasting consequences. That is even more reason for Republicans to make a priority of spending in races that could tip the balance of power. Mr. Trump may agree with Democrats that the election is about him, but it is also about setting the stage for what comes after him. Mr. Trump might not be on the ballot this year. The future of American politics certainly is.\n\nMr. Continetti is a columnist for WSJ Opinion's Free Expression.",
  "priority": "P2",
  "type": "opinion",
  "turn_to": null,
