  "final_id": "075",
  "title_cn": "共和党在参议院失利，影响将远超特朗普任期",
  "dek_cn": null,
  "body_cn": "共和党正在为一场艰难的十一月做准备。共和党在众议院的多数席位似乎岌岌可危，而民主党则越来越有信心赢得参议院的控制权。未来将是分治政府，外加阻挠和调查。特朗普总统在八月对南卡罗来纳州的选民说：“我将被弹劾。”“他们要弹劾我。他们根本不知道为什么。”\n\n弹劾可能只是特朗普总统面临的众多问题中最小的一个。毕竟，他已经经历过两次弹劾。他没有经历过的是民主党完全控制国会。这将使他面临的所有问题更加严重。\n\n特朗普总统可能认为，他可以像在第一任期对待南希·佩洛西那样，把哈基姆·杰弗里斯当作讨厌鬼打发掉。如果查克·舒默同时也是参议院多数党领袖，他将无法再这样做。一个由民主党控制的参议院将无法被忽视。\n\n即使特朗普总统卸任，共和党所面临的困境也不会结束。参议院选举的影响是长期的。参议员的任期为六年，横跨多个总统任期，这使得参议员有许多机会在多数党中任职，影响政策，并投出关键的决定性票。\n\n今年赢得席位的参议员，要到2032年，即下一个总统任期结束时，才能再次参加连任选举。他们将成为支持或反对特朗普总统继任者提出的立法和人事任命的基础。其中一些人可能会试图成为那位继任者。\n\n这种情况已经发生过。2006年，民主党增加了六个席位，并赢得了参议院的控制权。谢罗德·布朗、鲍勃·凯西二世、克莱尔·麦卡斯基尔、乔恩·泰斯特、吉姆·韦伯和谢尔登·怀特豪斯在乔治·W·布什总统的最后几年里，肯定都反对他的政策。但他们真正的影响力，直到2009年，他们投票支持奥巴马总统的经济刺激计划和《奥巴马医改法案》时，才得以体现。\n\n相反，2014年，共和党增加了九个席位，并赢得了参议院的多数席位。2014届的参议员包括保守派明星本·萨斯、汤姆·科顿和乔妮·恩斯特，他们帮助米奇·麦康奈尔多数党领袖在2016年维持了对梅里克·加兰大法官提名人选的反对，以填补安东尼·斯卡利亚大法官去世后的空缺。当唐纳德·特朗普成为总统时，他们都投票支持特朗普总统提出的最高法院大法官提名人选，并支持《减税与就业法案》。\n\n即使在参议院取得微小的进展，也可能产生重大的影响。2018年，民主党赢得了两个参议院席位（同时失去了四个席位），但克尔斯滕·西内马和杰基·罗森随后投票支持了拜登总统提出的巨额支出计划。在2020年大选中，民主党赢得了佐治亚州的两个参议院席位，这有助于拜登总统，并为乔恩·奥索夫提供了一个竞选总统的跳板。\n\n尽管共和党在2022年失去了一个参议院席位，但当年当选的一位共和党参议员在2025年成为了特朗普的副总统，并在《大而美法案》的投票中投出了决定性的一票。今年那些为了抗议而投票的美国人，最终可能会为民主党的J·D·万斯铺平政治道路。回想一下2004年，伊利诺伊州的一个空缺的参议院席位成为了奥巴马的政治跳板。\n\n新当选的参议员将会在总统继任者的第一任期内继续在参议院任职。以下是民主党控制参议院后可能采取的行动：民主党不仅会阻止特朗普的立法议程，还会推动来自民主党控制的众议院的累进税收、支出和监管法案。特朗普总统的否决权将会频繁使用。\n\n与此同时，参议院民主党人会阻止总统提名。最高法院也不会例外。自从参议员麦康奈尔阻止了梅里克·加兰的提名后，民主党人就一直渴望复仇。如果在11月赢得参议院，他们将有机会在出现空缺时实现这一目标。\n\n阻止特朗普只是一个开始。国会中的民主党人将利用政策和宣传来为2028年的总统竞选做好准备。如果一位民主党人在2028年获胜，他可能会像自比尔·克林顿以来每一位第一任期总统一样，以统一的政府形式上任。那时，保守派的噩梦将会开始。\n\n到2029年，2026届潜在的民主党成员——罗伊·库珀、布朗先生、玛丽·佩尔托拉、乔什·图雷克、阿卜杜勒·埃尔-赛义德等人——都将安顿在迪克森、罗素和哈特办公大楼中。他们将渴望为下一位索尼娅·索托马约尔或凯坦吉·布朗·杰克逊投票。他们将迫不及待地想要结束立法阻挠。这将为通过法案来推翻以各州为基础的选举法律（包括选民身份证明），对最高法院设置任期限制或增加席位，以及授予哥伦比亚特区和波多黎各州地位打开大门。那些以“可负担性”为旗帜当选的参议员，将有机会提高税收，限制石油和天然气产量，扩大政府规模，并干预医疗保险——左翼大混战。\n\n参议院选举具有持久的影响。这更充分地说明了共和党应该优先考虑在可能改变权力平衡的选举中投入资金。特朗普可能同意民主党人的观点，认为这次选举是关于他的，但它也是关于为他之后的事情做好准备。特朗普可能不会出现在今年的选票上，但美国政治的未来肯定会受到这次选举的影响。\n\n马修·康蒂内蒂是《华尔街日报》观点版专栏作家，为“Free Expression”撰稿。",
  "context_cn": null,
  "context_attr": null
 },
 {
  "id": "E2-035",
  "page": "A18",
  "section": "Sports",
  "title": "Late Interceptions Doom Texas",
  "dek": "The No. 1 Longhorns lost to Oklahoma after a late wobble by quarterback Arch Manning",
  "byline": "Robert O'Connell",
  "body_en": "Barely a month ago, Arch Manning finally looked like the golden boy quarterback the football world had been waiting to see. With a furious fourth-quarter rally, Manning had led Texas to a 20-point comeback win over Ohio State to launch the Longhorns to the No. 1 spot in the Top 25. He entrenched himself as the favorite to win the Heisman Trophy, guide Texas to a national title and cement himself as the top pick in the NFL draft.\n\nBut in a stunning collapse against Oklahoma on Saturday, Manning showed that years after being anointed as football's next star he remains far from the finished product.\n\nWith 20 seconds left in a tie game, Manning was trying to drive Texas into field-goal range when he slung a desperate pass off his back foot—straight into the hands of a Sooners defender for a critical interception.\n\nMoments later, Oklahoma kicked a field goal to win 27-24.\n\n“I think we beat ourselves,” Manning said after the game. “I can't be loose with the ball.”\n\nThat looseness doomed Texas all afternoon long, as a 10-point lead crumbled into their first defeat of the season. In addition to the last-minute interception, he threw a pick earlier in the fourth quarter that was returned for a touchdown, giving Oklahoma its first lead of the day. In all, Manning's turnovers led directly to 10 Sooner points.\n\n“There's a level of anger and frustration,” Texas coach Steve Sarkisian said.\n\nThe puzzling thing about Saturday's meltdown wasn't simply that the No. 1 team in the nation lost to a .500 team that had dropped out of the Top 25 rankings. It was that Texas spent all offseason dedicated to building the perfect football machine around the perfect football prospect—a roster so refined they wouldn't be in this kind of do-or-die situation to begin with.\n\nAfter Manning's debut as a full-time starter last season ended without a playoff appearance, the Longhorns shelled out more than $40 million in NIL spending on this year's roster. Texas splurged on high-profile transfers such as receiver Cam Coleman and running back Hollywood Smothers. They had one job: Make Arch Manning live up to his last name and join football royalty.\n\n“When we're at our best, we're a very explosive offensive football team,” Sarkisian said of the additions. “We were not that a year ago.”\n\nThe dynamite hasn't quite materialized. Manning has thrown for just 224.4 yards per game, the 53st-highest average in the country, according to Stats Perform. His two touchdowns per game are 32nd, and his passer rating is 64th.\n\nMost worrisome of all is Manning's inability to throw the football to his own team. After throwing seven interceptions all of last season, he has racked up five in just five games in 2026. In all, 3.4% of his throws have ended up in the hands of his opponents—a higher rate than 106 other quarterbacks nationwide.\n\nThe concerns around the Manning-led attack became so severe on Saturday that Sarkisian elected to try a 53-yard field goal rather than go for it on 4th-and-2. (The kick missed.) But despite a crushing loss to their biggest rivals, the Longhorns don't believe the sky is falling. In the age of an expanded playoff, one defeat doesn't spell doom for championship hopes.\n\n“We're not dead,” Sarkisian said. “We're going to be OK. We'll get back up off the mat.”\n\nHowever Texas' season ends up, fans awaiting the next great Manning may have to face a somewhat duller reality. The Heisman Trophy has all but slipped from his grasp, and his hold on the No. 1 spot in the NFL draft is facing threats from, among others, upstart Oklahoma State quarterback Drew Mestemaker.\n\n“Arch competed his tail off,” Sarkisian said, despite the throws that cost Texas its unbeaten record. “The competitor in him, he'd love to have that back.”",
  "priority": "P2",
  "type": "article",
  "turn_to": null,
