  "title_cn": "亚马逊正在洽谈收购一家人工智能初创公司",
  "dek_cn": null,
  "body_cn": "据知情人士透露，亚马逊正在与人工智能初创公司Decart（AI初创公司）洽谈收购事宜，交易估值可能达到约70亿美元。\n\n总部位于旧金山的Decart的软件可以帮助开发者更轻松地在不同的AI芯片之间切换，这在各公司竞相获取更多计算能力的情况下变得越来越重要。\n\n知情人士表示，如果谈判不破裂，或者没有出现其他竞购方，这笔交易可能会很快达成。\n\n据PitchBook（创投数据商）的数据，Decart已从红杉资本（Sequoia Capital）、Benchmark（风投）和Radical Ventures（风投）等投资者处筹集了超过4亿美元的风险投资。该公司在5月份的估值接近40亿美元。该公司由三位以色列工程师于2023年创立，他们是兄弟迪恩·莱特斯多夫（Dean Leitersdorf）和奥里安·莱特斯多夫（Orian Leitersdorf）以及莫舍·沙列夫（Moshe Shalev）。Decart正在开发名为Lucy和Oasis的世界模型。世界模型可以生成和模拟现实世界的3D环境。\n\n为了与英伟达竞争，亚马逊开发了自己的定制AI芯片Trainium3（亚马逊自研AI芯片），英伟达目前仍是图形处理器市场的领导者，而图形处理器是驱动人工智能发展的专用芯片。据《华尔街日报》报道，亚马逊网络服务（AWS）在与Decart会面并对其创始人印象深刻后，于2025年向该公司提供了对该芯片的早期访问权限。\n\n近年来，亚马逊的其他重大收购包括2022年以65亿美元收购米高梅电影和电视工作室，以及今年早些时候宣布的，以约110亿美元收购星链竞争对手Globalstar（卫星通信公司，星链竞争对手）。\n\n以色列新闻媒体Calcalist（以色列财经媒体）本月早些时候报道称，Decart正在与一家大型科技公司进行高级别的收购谈判。",
  "context_cn": "Decart 是一家总部位于旧金山的人工智能初创公司，2023年由三位以色列工程师（莱特斯多夫兄弟迪恩与奥里安、莫舍·沙列夫）创立，其软件帮助开发者在不同AI芯片之间切换——在各公司竞相获取算力的背景下愈发重要。公司正在开发名为 Lucy 和 Oasis 的“世界模型”（world models），可生成并模拟现实世界的3D环境。知情人士称亚马逊的收购估值约为70亿美元，远高于 Decart 今年5月近40亿美元的估值；此前 Decart 已从红杉资本、Benchmark 等投资者处融资超过4亿美元，亚马逊网络服务也在2025年向其开放了自研AI芯片 Trainium3 的早期使用权限。",
  "context_attr": "原文有"
 },
 {
  "id": "E3-002",
  "page": "B1",
  "section": "Business & Finance",
  "title": "The Upside to EV Slump: Charging Is Getting Better",
  "dek": "Public chargers continue to be installed at a steady clip in the U.S.",
  "byline": "By Patrick George",
  "body_en": "Electric-vehicle sales in the U.S. are down by half compared with last year. The EV charging industry evidently didn't get the memo.\n\nCharging companies in the U.S. are on track to install about as many public EV plugs this year as in 2025, the industry's record high. By adding thousands of new plugs this year, charging executives say they aim to serve longtime EV owners looking for easier road trips as well as buyers flocking to used EVs—while prepping infrastructure for a day when EV demand might bounce back.\n\n\"I still think people are breathing into paper bags,\" Rick Wilmer, chief executive of the charging company ChargePoint, said of the industry's mood amid the downturn. \"But the growth is real. The EV-driving community wants more chargers in the world.\"\n\nOptimism has been in short supply for the EV industry since President Trump axed tax credits and fuel-economy regulations aimed at moving Americans away from gasoline-powered cars. Automakers backed away from their most ambitious pro-EV goals, canceling models and planned ones, while listing multibillion-dollar write-downs as they retool their investments and pivot back to gas.\n\nEven so, the U.S. by early October added nearly 2,000 charging stations with more than 12,000 plugs, according to charging-industry data firm Paren. That is roughly on pace with last year, which ended with 3,885 new charging stations added with 19,839 plugs. The U.S. now has more than 80,000 public fast-charging plugs, data shows.\n\nMore crucially, charging providers are learning from the mistakes of the past that often frustrated EV owners, said Bill Ferro, co-founder and chief technical officer of Paren.\n\nNew stations have more plugs on average to cut down on wait time. Chargers are becoming more powerful to \"refuel\" EVs more quickly. And the industry is working on its real-estate game: adding more stations near restaurants and busy areas, not in distant parking lots far from restrooms or other amenities.\n\n\"I've started calling it the Great American Build-Out,\" Ferro said. \"You don't realize you're in it until you're in the middle of it.\"\n\nThat build-out attempts to solve a chicken-and-egg problem that beguiled EVs since their earliest days: Without adequate charging infrastructure, [text as printed] U.S. car sales to be electric, hydrogen or plug-in hybrid models by 2030.\n\nInstead, demand for the cars never reached levels projected by the auto industry. EV sales peaked around 12% of the new-car market in the third quarter of 2025 before the tax credits went away, and have hovered around 6% this year.\n\nAuto-industry executives frequently say they expect EV demand to eventually rise again in the U.S., especially as less expensive electric models come to market.\n\nTo be sure, charging providers also have a long way to go to reach consistent profitability as they contend with high capital costs.\n\nChargePoint's Wilmer noted that losses are narrowing and that the company has seen growth across all its business lines, including public stations and fleet customers.\n\nAutonomous vehicles, which are overwhelmingly electric, are expected to drive charging demand.\n\nWilmer and other charging-industry executives say there aren't enough plugs to properly serve America's current fleet of EV drivers—let alone the ones who might join the fray later on.\n\nThe charging space's glow-up isn't happening everywhere. Growth is primarily concentrated in coastal states like California and New York, although Ferro said Texas and Florida are scaling up quickly.\n\nStill, the industry has more than a few recent success stories. Ionna, the charging network backed by Hyundai, Toyota and other automakers, [text fragmented in print] a restroom. More are coming from the retail giant.\n\nThe leader by a country mile remains Tesla. According to Paren, the Elon Musk-helmed company added more than 3,500 fast-charging plugs this year, good for about 40,000 total across the U.S. While Musk insists Tesla's future lies in robotics and AI, it continues to scale its charging network. The company doesn't break out charging revenue in its financial reports but lists it as part of a services business that brought in $4.6 billion in the second quarter. (Tesla didn't respond to a request for comment.)\n\nSome industry executives are keeping a close eye on how gas prices might affect consumer behavior. Tiya Gordon, co-founder of It's Electric, a startup that recently secured an exclusive contract to build curbside, public charging stations across New York City, thinks sustained higher fuel costs could mean a \"pendulum swing\" is coming for EVs—so much so that she recently got a tattoo of the Strait of Hormuz.\n\n[Chart: U.S. public EV fast-charging ports by month since January 2021. Note: Data through September 2026. Source: Energy Department.]\n\n[Photo caption: New stations have more plugs and powerful chargers to cut down on wait time. (Angus Mordant/Bloomberg News)]\n\n[Photo caption: Elon Musk's Tesla added more than 3,500 fast-charging plugs this year.]",
  "priority": "P2",
  "type": "article",
  "turn_to": "B2",
  "terms": [
   {
    "en": "ChargePoint",
    "zh_suggested": "ChargePoint（充电桩公司）",
    "note": "CEO Rick Wilmer"
   },
   {
    "en": "Paren",
    "zh_suggested": "Paren（充电行业数据公司）",
    "note": "联合创始人兼CTO Bill Ferro"
   },
   {
    "en": "Ionna",
    "zh_suggested": "Ionna（充电网络）",
    "note": "现代、丰田等车企支持"
   },
   {
    "en": "It's Electric",
    "zh_suggested": "It's Electric（初创公司）",
    "note": "联合创始人Tiya Gordon；纽约路边充电桩"
   }
  ],
  "final_id": "081",
  "title_cn": "电动汽车销量下滑带来的积极影响：充电基础设施正在不断完善",
  "dek_cn": "在美国，公共充电桩的安装速度依然保持稳定。",
  "body_cn": "美国电动汽车的销量与去年相比下降了一半。显然，电动汽车充电行业并没有意识到这一情况。\n\n美国充电公司今年的公共电动汽车充电桩安装数量，预计将与2025年持平，达到行业历史最高水平。充电公司高管表示，他们计划今年增加数千个新的充电桩，旨在为那些希望更轻松地进行长途旅行的现有电动汽车车主以及涌向二手电动汽车市场的买家提供服务——同时为电动汽车需求可能反弹的那一天做好基础设施准备。\n\nChargePoint（充电桩公司）首席执行官里克·威尔默表示，在谈及行业低迷期的情绪时说：“我仍觉得大家都还在惊慌失措（往纸袋里喘气）。但增长是真实的。电动汽车用户希望世界上有更多的充电桩。”\n\n自特朗普总统取消旨在鼓励美国人放弃燃油汽车的税收优惠和燃油经济性法规以来，电动汽车行业的乐观情绪一直不足。汽车制造商退出了他们最初雄心勃勃的电动汽车发展目标，取消了现有车型和计划中的车型，并列出了数十亿美元的资产减值，以重新调整其投资并转向燃油汽车。\n\n尽管如此，根据充电行业数据公司Paren的数据，到10月初，美国新增了近2000个充电站，拥有超过12000个充电桩。这一数字与去年大致持平，去年新增了3885个充电站，拥有19839个充电桩。数据显示，美国目前拥有超过8万个公共快速充电桩。\n\nParen的联合创始人兼首席技术官比尔·费罗表示，充电服务提供商正在从过去的一些错误中吸取教训，这些错误常常让电动汽车车主感到沮丧。\n\n新的充电站平均拥有更多的充电桩，以缩短等待时间。充电桩的功率正在提高，以便更快地“补充”电动汽车的电量。此外，该行业还在努力改善其选址策略：在餐馆和繁忙区域附近增加更多的充电站，而不是设置在远离洗手间或其他便利设施的偏远停车场。\n\n费罗说：“我开始称之为‘伟大的美国充电站建设’。你不会意识到自己已经身处其中，直到你真正参与其中。”\n\n这项建设试图解决电动汽车自诞生之初就面临的“先有鸡还是先有蛋”难题：如果没有足够的充电基础设施，[此处印刷文本如此]2030年美国汽车销量中电动、氢能或插电混动车型的占比……\n\n然而，实际情况是，对这些汽车的需求并未达到汽车行业预测的水平。在税收优惠取消之前，电动汽车的销量在 2025 年第三季度达到峰值，约占新车市场的 12%，此后销量一直徘徊在 6% 左右。\n\n汽车行业高管们经常表示，他们预计美国对电动汽车的需求最终会再次增长，尤其是在价格更实惠的电动车型上市后。\n\n当然，充电服务提供商要想实现持续盈利，还有很长的路要走，因为他们面临着高昂的资本成本。\n\nChargePoint（充电桩公司）的威尔默指出，公司的亏损正在减少，并且该公司在所有业务领域都实现了增长，包括公共充电站和面向车队的客户。\n\n自动驾驶汽车，其中绝大多数是电动汽车，预计将推动充电需求。\n\n威尔默和其他充电行业高管表示，目前美国的充电桩数量不足以满足现有电动汽车用户的需求，更不用说未来可能加入的更多用户。\n\n充电行业的蓬勃发展并非在所有地区都同步进行。增长主要集中在加利福尼亚州和纽约州等沿海州，尽管费罗表示，德克萨斯州和佛罗里达州的增长速度也在迅速提升。\n\n尽管如此，该行业近期仍有一些成功的案例。Ionna（充电网络）是一家由现代汽车、丰田和其他汽车制造商支持的充电网络，[原文此处有缺失]。更多充电站将由这家零售巨头建设。\n\n特斯拉仍然是该行业的领头羊。根据Paren（充电行业数据公司）的数据，埃隆·马斯克领导的特斯拉公司今年增加了超过3500个快速充电桩，使美国境内的总数达到约4万个。尽管马斯克坚称特斯拉的未来在于机器人技术和人工智能，但该公司仍在不断扩大其充电网络。该公司在其财务报告中并未单独列出充电业务的收入，而是将其列为服务业务的一部分，该业务在第二季度带来了46亿美元的收入。（特斯拉未对置评请求做出回应。）\n\n一些行业高管正在密切关注汽油价格可能如何影响消费者的行为。Tiya Gordon是It's Electric（初创公司）的联合创始人，该公司最近获得了在纽约市建设路边公共充电站的独家合同。她认为，持续上涨的燃料成本可能会导致电动汽车市场出现“摆动”，甚至最近还在身上纹了霍尔木兹海峡的图案。\n\n[图表：自2021年1月以来，美国公共电动汽车快速充电桩数量按月统计。注：数据截至2026年9月。来源：能源部。]\n\n[照片说明：新的充电站拥有更多的充电桩和更强大的充电器，以缩短等待时间。（Angus Mordant/彭博新闻）]\n\n[照片说明：埃隆·马斯克的特斯拉公司今年增加了超过3500个快速充电桩。]",
  "context_cn": null,
  "context_attr": null
 },
 {
  "id": "E3-003",
  "page": "B1",
  "section": "Business & Finance",
  "title": "Delta CEO Says It's Prepared for Swings",
  "dek": null,
  "byline": "By Alison Sider",
  "body_en": "Fuel is taking a chunk out of Delta Air Lines' profits. The carrier on Friday sharply lowered its outlook for the year, reflecting a $6 billion increase in fuel costs.\n\nBut even with fuel prices on the rise, Delta Chief Executive Ed Bastian said his airline—the most profitable in the U.S.—is best-positioned to ride out the wild swings. Airlines cut routes and managed to charge higher fares and fees for checked bags.\n\nSo far, travel demand has been resilient. But some carriers are on track to lose money this year—unlike Delta.\n\nThe Wall Street Journal spoke with Bastian about how the industry is navigating the turbulence, the never-ending appetite for new premium-travel amenities, and why Delta is sticking to its decision to skip Starlink Wi-Fi.\n\nWSJ: Last time we spoke, there seemed to be a lot more optimism that fuel prices were coming down. How do you see things now?\n\nBASTIAN: Well, obviously that didn't come to pass. It's real hard to predict what's going to happen. So what we're doing is focusing on things we can control. If you consider fuel prices were up 60%, and the company earned a level of profitability comparable to where we were a year ago, it's pretty stunning, and certainly very different than anything that you would have seen within the industry going back over a number of cycles of prior fuel spikes.\n\nWSJ: Are you seeing any resistance to higher fares? Are there people who are kind of throwing in the towel?\n\nBASTIAN: I wouldn't say throwing in the towel, but those at the lower end of the economic curve are going to be the most sensitive, and that's why we reduced our supply offering in that market. Holiday bookings look strong. We're expecting our fourth-quarter revenues to be up 20%.\n\nWSJ: Is there an upper limit to premium demand?\n\nBASTIAN: I don't think it's getting crowded. But just because someone says they're going premium, that does not make them a premium airline. We've been at it for 15 years. Premium is the overall experience, not just bringing in new seats or adding Starlink. I think we're probably still in the early-to-mid innings in terms of the overall premium supply versus demand.\n\nWSJ: You mentioned Starlink. Does it get to you when Elon Musk is criticizing you?\n\nBASTIAN: I don't read it. All kidding aside, we know what we're doing in the space, and we've been the pioneer in Wi-Fi. Starlink's a great product, and we know it well. We were involved in the early years of bringing it to aviation, and they had a different business model than we had. We're happy with where we sit.\n\n[Photo: Ed Bastian. (Anindito Mukherjee/Bloomberg News)]",
  "priority": "P2",
  "type": "article",
  "turn_to": "B2",
  "terms": [
   {
    "en": "Ed Bastian",
    "zh_suggested": "埃德·巴斯蒂安（达美航空CEO）",
    "note": null
   },
   {
    "en": "Starlink",
    "zh_suggested": "星链（SpaceX卫星互联网）",
    "note": "达美决定不采用其机上Wi-Fi"
   }
  ],
  "final_id": "082",
  "title_cn": "达美航空首席执行官表示，公司已做好应对市场波动的准备",
  "dek_cn": null,
  "body_cn": "燃油成本正在蚕食达美航空的利润。该公司周五大幅下调了对全年的预期，反映了燃油成本增加了 60 亿美元。\n\n即便燃油价格上涨，达美航空首席执行官埃德·巴斯蒂安（达美航空CEO）表示，他的航空公司——美国盈利能力最强的航空公司——最能应对这种剧烈波动。航空公司减少了航线，并设法提高了机票价格和行李托运费。\n\n到目前为止，旅行需求依然强劲。但一些航空公司今年可能会亏损——与达美航空不同。\n\n《华尔街日报》与巴斯蒂安进行了对话，讨论了行业如何应对当前的挑战，人们对新型高端旅行服务的需求是否会持续增长，以及为什么达美航空坚持不采用星链（SpaceX卫星互联网）Wi-Fi。\n\n《华尔街日报》：上次我们谈话时，似乎大家对燃油价格下降的前景更加乐观。您现在如何看待这个问题？\n\n巴斯蒂安：显然，情况并非如此。很难预测未来会发生什么。因此，我们现在专注于我们可以控制的事情。如果考虑到燃油价格上涨了 60%，但公司的盈利水平与一年前大致相同，这确实令人惊讶，而且与过去燃油价格大幅上涨时的情况截然不同。\n\n《华尔街日报》：您是否注意到人们对更高票价的抵触情绪？是否有人开始放弃旅行？\n\n巴斯蒂安：我不会说人们放弃了旅行，但经济状况较差的人群对价格上涨更为敏感，这就是为什么我们减少了在该市场的航班数量。假日预订情况良好。我们预计第四季度的收入将增长 20%。\n\n《华尔街日报》：高端服务的需求是否存在上限？\n\n埃德·巴斯蒂安：我认为现在还谈不上需求饱和。但仅仅因为有人表示要提供高端服务，并不意味着他们就成了一家高端航空公司。我们已经在这方面努力了 15 年。高端服务指的是整体体验，而不仅仅是更换新的座椅或增加星链服务。我认为，就整体高端服务供给与需求之间的关系而言，我们可能还处于发展的早期或中期阶段。\n\n《华尔街日报》：您提到了星链。当埃隆·马斯克批评你们时，您会感到在意吗？\n\n埃德·巴斯蒂安：我不会去看那些评论。开玩笑归开玩笑，我们清楚自己在做什么，而且我们一直是机上 Wi-Fi 的先驱。星链是一个很棒的产品，我们对它非常了解。我们参与了星链早期在航空领域的应用，但他们的商业模式与我们不同。我们对目前的情况感到满意。\n\n[照片：埃德·巴斯蒂安。（Anindito Mukherjee/彭博社）]",
  "context_cn": null,
  "context_attr": null
 },
 {
  "id": "E3-004",
  "page": "B1",
  "section": "Technology",
  "title": "Artificial Intelligence Has a God Problem",
  "dek": null,
  "byline": "By Tim Higgins",
  "body_en": "Pastor Joseph Hutchinson began a recent Sunday sermon by pointing to the Book of Revelation, the final part of the New Testament best known for apocalyptic prophecy. And he quickly argued for how artificial intelligence will fit into end times.\n\n\"AI wants to be God,\" the pastor warned those at Gospel Light Baptist Church in Orangeburg, S.C.\n\nThe sermon, the 74-year-old later told me, was inspired by the questions he was getting from the congregation, which numbers around 150. In his roughly 50 years preaching, Hutchinson says the anxiety he is hearing about AI outranks everything—wars, economy.\n\n\"The more you see people that are projecting doom and despair coming out, then the more people say, 'Well, the preacher must have been right, and the Bible must be right,'\" Hutchinson said.\n\nAlready a polarizing political issue, the AI industry's latest challenge is emerging from the pulpit. Unlike recent generations of technology, the Christian community appears poised to put its stamp on AI early in an attempt to ensure it benefits all of humanity rather than a few tech billionaires.\n\nThe risk for the likes of OpenAI and Anthropic is getting further sideways with a swath of the American populace, some of whom are unhappy with AI data centers and Flock cameras in their communities.\n\n\"A lot of pastors are realizing when people get freaked out about institutions they depend on, they try to find their voice to advocate for themselves and their communities,\" said Meghan Sullivan, a philosophy professor at the University of Notre Dame. \"And churches are really good at giving people a vehicle for advocating for their vision of the common good in broader society, especially in parts of the country like\" the Midwest.\n\nThis month, the National Association of Evangelicals issued a policy statement on AI. The Presbyterian Church (U.S.A.) held a summit on the topic in August. And Pope Leo XIV published a rare encyclical in May on \"safeguarding the human person in the time of artificial intelligence.\"\n\nHistory is replete with moments when new technology—so powerful, so complex as to be almost magical—stirs primal fears. After the U.S. dropped atomic bombs to end World War II, the New York Times invoked Old Testament language when writing that the technology had \"sowed the whirlwind.\"\n\nToday, AI carries its own whirlwind of change. With potential good and bad so intertwined, it is hard to know if the technology will bring a brighter future or darker days: cures for cancers, untold scientific discoveries, sustainable abundance. Yet also rogue AI hacks, job losses, and questions about the very meaning of work.\n\nOne of the self-inflicted challenges for AI creators is that the tech community has framed much of the public conversation, intentionally or not, in language that resonates with the Christian community. That raises concerns about whether they can be trusted, especially with the baggage tech-at-large carries from the ways, some religious leaders say, it failed to ensure social media didn't become harmful.\n\nThe theme of a higher power creating AI in its own image only for it to rebel against its creator is a familiar one to those who study the Bible. The idea that people in AI labs think they have a power akin to God to create a new kind of consciousness is unsettling, if not blasphemous, to some. And, for others, there is a certain hubris seen in tech leaders chasing a technology that on one hand promises to make them incredibly wealthy while suggesting it is only safe within their control.\n\nIn his May encyclical, the pope drew comparisons to the Tower of Babel, a cautionary tale that appears in the first Book of Genesis around an effort to build a tower that reached to the heavens in a bid to guarantee stability and power that displeased God. \"The risk of dehumanization—of building a future that excludes God and reduces the other to a means—is an ancient and ever-new temptation that today takes on a technical guise,\" the pope wrote.\n\nSince then, the pope hasn't let up, including on social-media platform X where a robust debate is playing out among AI's creators.\n\nMaybe that's why in recent days, OpenAI CEO Sam Altman tried to distance his company from a report by the New York Times that rival Anthropic unsuccessfully lobbied the Vatican to change its views on whether AI could have a consciousness.\n\n\"I am very uncomfortable about people trying to ascribe religious force or a surrender of human judgment to AI models, and think it is a real safety issue,\" Altman posted on X a weekend ago.\n\nThose raising concerns can't be dismissed as simply Luddites. Many faith leaders say they are hoping to steer the technology to ensure it is beneficial to humanity—not harmful. [Print edition is fragmented here; web edition of the same article attributes the following to Walter Kim, president of the National Association of Evangelicals:] \"There's a reason why the faith community is having a moment because they're beginning to see that this is a technology that is actually addressing…not only the consequential horizontal societal issues that are at stake, but it's actually raising the larger question of what does it mean to be human?\"\n\nDuring that recent Sunday sermon in South Carolina, Hutchinson's Siri assistant squawked from his iPhone as he connected how all of the surveillance aspects of AI would be powerful in Revelation's apocalypse.\n\nWhen it comes to AI, the pastor is hopeful it will find amazing cures for illnesses and he agrees with President Trump that America shouldn't slow down development at the risk of letting China get ahead.\n\nIt's just that he also sees a power being created, which he cautioned his flock to remember, could be misused.\n\n\"I see what it's leading to in the future and that doesn't scare me, but it bothers me for the other people that aren't ready,\" he said.\n\n[Photo caption: Pope Leo XIV drew comparisons between AI and the Tower of Babel in his May encyclical. (Vatican Media/AFP/Getty Images)]",
  "priority": "P2",
  "type": "article",
