  "final_id": "083",
  "title_cn": "人工智能有个上帝问题",
  "dek_cn": null,
  "body_cn": "约瑟夫·哈钦森牧师在最近的一次周日布道中，首先引用了《启示录》，这是《新约》的最后一部分，以其末世预言而闻名。他随即阐述了人工智能将如何融入末世。\n\n这位牧师在南卡罗来纳州奥兰治堡的福音之光浸信会中警告说：“人工智能想要成为上帝。”\n\n这位74岁的牧师后来告诉我，这次布道受到了会众提出的问题的启发。他的会众大约有150人。哈钦森说，在长达50年的布道生涯中，他发现人们对人工智能的焦虑程度超过了对战争和经济的担忧。\n\n哈钦森说：“人们越是看到有人预言灾难和绝望，就越多人会说，‘牧师说得没错，圣经也一定是正确的。’”\n\n人工智能行业面临的最新挑战，已经从一个极具争议的政治议题，演变成一个从讲坛上提出的问题。与以往几代技术不同，基督教社群似乎准备在人工智能发展的早期阶段就对其进行引导，以确保它能造福全人类，而不是少数科技亿万富翁。\n\n对于OpenAI和Anthropic等公司来说，风险在于它们可能会与越来越多的美国民众产生隔阂，其中一些人对人工智能数据中心和Flock（车牌识别摄像头公司）的摄像头在社区中出现的情况感到不满。\n\n圣母大学哲学教授梅根·沙利文说：“许多牧师意识到，当人们对他们所依赖的机构感到不安时，他们会试图找到自己的声音，为自己和社区发声。”“教会非常善于为人们提供一个平台，让他们能够为自己对更广泛社会共同利益的愿景进行倡导，尤其是在像美国中西部这样的地区。”\n\n本月，美国福音派协会发布了一份关于人工智能的政策声明。长老会（美国）于8月举行了一次关于该主题的峰会。教宗良十四世于5月发布了一份罕见的通谕，主题是“在人工智能时代保护人类”。\n\n历史上充满了许多时刻，当时，一种新的技术——强大而复杂，几乎具有魔力——引发了人们内心深处的恐惧。二战结束后，美国向日本投下了原子弹，纽约时报在报道中使用了《旧约》的语言，称这项技术“播下了风暴的种子”。\n\n如今，人工智能也带来了它自身的变革之风。它既有潜在的积极作用，也有潜在的消极影响，两者交织在一起，很难判断这项技术会带来更美好的未来，还是更黑暗的时代：它可以治愈癌症，带来前所未有的科学发现，实现可持续的繁荣。但它也可能导致恶意的人工智能攻击、失业，并引发人们对工作的意义本身的质疑。\n\n人工智能开发者面临的一个自我设定的挑战是，科技界有意或无意地，用与基督教社群产生共鸣的语言，主导了公众对话。这引发了人们对其可信度的担忧，尤其是在考虑到科技界未能确保社交媒体不会变得有害，从而给一些宗教领袖带来了负面印象。\n\n在《圣经》中，有一个熟悉的主题，即更高的力量按自己的形象创造了AI，后者却反抗了它的创造者。一些人认为，人工智能实验室中的人们认为自己拥有类似于上帝的力量，可以创造一种新的意识，这令人不安，甚至是对上帝的亵渎。此外，还有一些人认为，科技领袖们追求一项技术，一方面承诺让他们变得非常富有，另一方面又暗示只有在他们的控制之下才能保证安全，这种做法带有某种狂妄自大。\n\n在五月份的通谕中，教宗将人工智能与“巴别塔”进行了比较，这是一个警示故事，出现在《创世纪》的第一卷中，讲述了一个试图建造一座通往天堂的塔的故事，目的是为了确保稳定和权力，但却让上帝感到不满。教宗写道：“非人化——建立一个排斥上帝，并将他人视为工具的未来——是一种古老而又不断出现的诱惑，如今它以一种技术的形式出现。”\n\n此后，教宗一直对此保持关注，包括在社交媒体平台X上，人工智能的开发者们就此展开了激烈的辩论。\n\n也许这就是为什么最近，OpenAI的首席执行官萨姆·奥特曼试图将他的公司与纽约时报的一份报告区分开来，该报告称，竞争对手Anthropic曾试图游说梵蒂冈改变其对人工智能是否可能具有意识的看法，但最终未能成功。\n\n奥特曼几天前在X上发帖说：“我非常不赞成有人试图将宗教力量或放弃人类判断力赋予人工智能模型，我认为这是一个真正的安全问题。”\n\n那些提出担忧的人不能简单地被视为反对技术进步的人。许多宗教领袖表示，他们希望引导这项技术，确保它对人类有益，而不是造成危害。[印刷版在此处内容不完整；同一文章的网络版将以下引文归于美国福音派协会主席沃尔特·金：]“宗教界之所以开始关注此事，是因为他们逐渐意识到，这项技术实际上正在解决……不仅是当前社会面临的重大问题，而且还在引发一个更宏大的问题，即‘作为人类意味着什么’？”\n\n在南卡罗来纳州最近的一次周日布道中，哈钦森的Siri语音助手从他的iPhone中发出声音，他将人工智能的所有监控方面与《启示录》中的末日景象联系起来。\n\n在人工智能方面，这位牧师希望它能找到治疗疾病的惊人方法，并且他同意特朗普总统的观点，即美国不应放慢发展速度，以免让中国领先。\n\n他只是认为，这项技术正在创造一种力量，他告诫他的信徒要记住，这种力量可能会被滥用。\n\n他说：“我看到了它在未来可能带来的影响，这并没有让我感到害怕，但它让我为那些尚未做好准备的人感到担忧。”\n\n[照片说明：教宗良十四世在五月份的通谕中将人工智能与巴别塔进行了比较。（梵蒂冈媒体/法新社/盖蒂图片社）]",
  "context_cn": null,
  "context_attr": null
 },
 {
  "id": "E3-005",
  "page": "B1",
  "section": "Business & Finance",
  "title": "Reckitt Thrives on China Social Media",
  "dek": null,
  "byline": "By Natasha Khan",
  "body_en": "Several years ago, China-based executives of Lysol maker Reckitt leveled with their boss. More consumers were skipping supermarket runs and buying household staples while they scrolled on phones, the executives said.\n\nCould they have some funds to get ahead of the trend?\n\nChief Executive Kris Licht figured they were talking about China's equivalents of Amazon.com and Walmart+. \"But no,\" Licht recalled, \"I was told that e-commerce was a yesterday technology.\"\n\nThe result: a pivot in Reckitt's China sales tactics, one that propelled 12 consecutive quarters of double-digit sales growth in the country and helped the British firm weather a sales slowdown in established markets.\n\nWhat the CEO's colleagues said Reckitt needed to jump on was the rapid rise of a different venue for shopping in China. In what's known as social commerce, people scrolling through social-media apps for entertainment end up buying after seeing an ad.\n\nReckitt's success capitalizing on Chinese consumers' shift points to how Western firms that once counted on China for growth can thrive there though local competitors have made it a tougher place to do business. It's also an example of how social media and shopping are converging.\n\nAfter its China team's tête-à-tête with Licht, Reckitt pulled out the stops to go to the social-media apps where many Chinese shop.\n\nThe company built 18 studios inside a building on the outskirts of Shanghai. On videos broadcast from there, employees sell Veet hair-removal cream, Lysol's international equivalent Dettol and other Reckitt goods on China's popular social-media platforms.\n\nIn some of the studios, company staffers join guest actors and influencers making quick comedies and mini-dramas about domestic life featuring a Reckitt brand.\n\n\"This is the way forward,\" Licht said. \"Those that don't figure this out will be left behind.\"\n\nSome 800 million of China's 1.4 billion people now each day use social media like Douyin, the Chinese version of TikTok, and apps like Kuaishou. The users spend an average of about 90 minutes a day scrolling through short videos, Reckitt estimated. Key for consumer-good companies: Half of them have bought items on the platforms.\n\nSocial commerce is starting to catch on in the U.S., where shoppers are now buying products by clicking links in TikTok and Instagram videos, and watching live-shopping streams on apps like Whatnot.\n\n\"We all have the experience of finishing the day, relaxing on the sofa, watching videos and clicking 'buy' without thinking too much about it,\" said Vivien Huang, Reckitt's director of marketing in China.\n\nIt was around 2022 that Reckitt's team in China sought Licht's support getting Reckitt to invest in social commerce.\n\nThe team initially struggled to hire influencers or agencies with the right experience, so staff did it themselves. Out of a conference room converted into a makeshift studio, customer-service staff answered questions on video about the chemical composition of disinfectants.\n\nSales shot up, boosting the company while it grappled with stagnating sales in the U.S. and other developed markets. Reckitt's emerging-market sales grew 8.5% to 2.23 billion pounds in the first half of this year.\n\nFrom the company's Shanghai studios, crews broadcast live on the apps for 12 hours a day, while shoppers bombard them with comments and questions in a chat that dominates part of the screen.\n\nThe Reckitt hosts respond rapidly, a sort of interactive infomercial. A team monitoring the livestreams guides the hosts and adjusts the featured products they talk about in real-time.\n\nIn other studios, short dramas and skits are filmed. The videos, which are often under a minute, might depict a scene from a living room or highlight a domestic dispute. In one, a parent argues with their teenager about smelly shoes—and Dettol comes to the rescue.\n\nThe short-form video team drove 40 million new customers to Reckitt last year, drawn by videos they produced that the company said were viewed 15 billion times.\n\nReckitt predicts social commerce will catch on in some form in the more than 100 other countries where it sells its products, and has begun replicating its China playbook in markets including Brazil, Mexico and Malaysia.\n\n[Photo caption: From the company's Shanghai studios, crews broadcast live on apps for 12 hours a day.]",
  "priority": "P2",
  "type": "article",
  "turn_to": "B4",
