  "title_cn": "利洁时（英国消费品巨头）在中国社交媒体平台上蓬勃发展",
  "dek_cn": null,
  "body_cn": "几年前，总部位于中国的利洁时（英国消费品巨头）高管们向他们的老板坦诚地表达了自己的看法。这些高管表示，越来越多的消费者不再去超市购物，而是在浏览手机时购买日常家居用品。\n\n他们能否获得一些资金，以便抢先抓住这一趋势？\n\n首席执行官傅乐宏认为，他们所说的可能是中国版的亚马逊和沃尔玛会员计划。但傅乐宏回忆说：“后来我被告知，电子商务已经是一种过时的技术。”\n\n结果是，利洁时调整了其在中国市场的销售策略，从而实现了连续12个季度两位数的销售增长，并帮助这家英国公司应对了成熟市场销售额的放缓。\n\n首席执行官的同事们认为，利洁时需要抓住的是中国市场购物方式的快速转变。在所谓的社交电商模式中，人们在浏览社交媒体应用程序时，看到广告后会顺便购买商品。\n\n利洁时成功地利用了中国消费者购物习惯的转变，这表明，曾经依赖中国市场实现增长的西方公司，即使面临本土竞争对手的挑战，仍然可以在中国市场取得成功。这同时也是社交媒体和购物融合的一个例子。\n\n在利洁时中国团队与傅乐宏进行了一次深入交流后，利洁时全力以赴，进驻了许多中国消费者经常使用的社交媒体应用程序。\n\n该公司在上海郊区的一栋建筑内建立了18个工作室。通过从这些工作室直播的视频，员工在中国流行的社交媒体平台上销售薇婷（脱毛膏品牌）、滴露（Lysol的国际版）以及其他利洁时产品。\n\n在一些工作室中，公司的员工会与客座演员和网红一起，制作关于家庭生活的短喜剧和迷你剧，其中会融入利洁时的品牌。\n\n傅乐宏（利洁时CEO）表示：“这是未来的发展方向，如果不能抓住这一趋势，就会被远远甩在后面。”\n\n中国有14亿人口，其中约有8亿人每天使用抖音等社交媒体平台，抖音是中国版的TikTok，还有快手等应用。利洁时估计，用户平均每天花费约90分钟浏览短视频。这对消费品公司来说至关重要：其中一半的用户已经在这些平台上购买了商品。\n\n社交电商在美国开始流行，购物者现在可以通过点击TikTok和Instagram视频中的链接来购买产品，并通过Whatnot等应用观看直播购物。\n\n利洁时中国市场营销总监黄薇薇表示：“我们都有这样的经历，一天工作结束后，放松地坐在沙发上，观看视频，然后毫不犹豫地点击‘购买’。”\n\n大约在2022年，利洁时中国团队向傅乐宏寻求支持，希望利洁时能够投资社交电商。\n\n最初，团队在招聘具有相关经验的网红或代理机构方面遇到困难，因此员工们决定自己动手。他们将一个会议室改造成一个临时工作室，客户服务人员在视频中回答有关消毒剂化学成分的问题。\n\n销售额大幅增长，这在一定程度上弥补了公司在美国和其他发达市场面临的销售停滞问题。今年上半年，利洁时在新兴市场的销售额增长了8.5%，达到22.3亿英镑。\n\n从该公司位于上海的工作室，团队每天在应用程序上进行12小时的直播，同时，购物者通过聊天功能向他们发送大量评论和问题，聊天内容占据了屏幕的一部分。\n\n利洁时的主持人会迅速做出回应，这就像一种互动式的产品推销。一个团队负责监控直播，为主持人提供指导，并实时调整他们所介绍的产品。\n\n在其他工作室，他们会拍摄短剧和短片。这些视频通常时长不到一分钟，可能会描绘客厅中的场景，或者突出家庭纠纷。其中一个视频中，一位家长与自己的青少年孩子就鞋子的异味问题争论，这时滴露（Dettol）就派上用场了。\n\n去年，该公司的短视频团队为利洁时吸引了4000万新客户，这些客户被他们制作的视频所吸引，该公司表示这些视频的观看次数达到了150亿次。\n\n利洁时预测，社交电商将在其销售产品的100多个国家中以某种形式流行起来，并已开始在包括巴西、墨西哥和马来西亚在内的市场复制其在中国市场的成功模式。\n\n[图片说明：从该公司位于上海的工作室，团队每天在应用程序上进行12小时的直播。]",
  "context_cn": null,
  "context_attr": null
 },
 {
  "id": "E3-006",
  "page": "B1",
  "section": "Business & Finance",
  "title": "Robinhood Traders Share Tales of Wealth",
  "dek": null,
  "byline": "By Hannah Erin Lang and Rachel Mendelson",
  "body_en": "HOUSTON—Thirty minutes before Robinhood Markets Chief Executive Vlad Tenev took the stage, the crowd was already buzzing.\n\nSome 1,500 individual investors were packed into the seats of a Houston convention hall, snapping pictures and fiddling with their neon-green nametags. They came from different walks of life—plumbers and small-business owners, mingling with corporate middle managers and stay-at-home parents. But many had something in common: investment portfolios that had grown so large in recent years they had quit their jobs to play the market full time.\n\nBack on Wall Street, investors have been sweating higher oil prices and a global bond selloff that pushed Treasury yields to two-decade highs. But at Robinhood's Hood Summit, the party was still raging.\n\nRobinhood's conference for active traders is the largest of the brokerage's annual events. For two days in September, hundreds of everyday investors descend on a conference center in cities like Miami, Las Vegas and Houston to attend training sessions, swap trading tips and swarm the open bar.\n\nThe conference also offers an up-close view of a generational shift in financial markets: The do-it-yourself investor is playing a bigger role on Wall Street than ever before.\n\nEveryday traders have gained access to new, riskier assets, from event contracts and private companies to leveraged perpetual futures and even tokenized stocks. They powered market rebounds, rushing in to buy the dip when share prices swooned.\n\nMany made massive bets on popular tech names such as Tesla, Alphabet or Palantir—and, as the artificial-intelligence rally blew past record after record, got richer than they ever imagined.\n\nIn recent weeks, professional money managers have grappled with a punishing bond selloff, rising fuel costs and a stock-market rally that is increasingly dependent on a handful of tech stocks.\n\nIn Houston, nobody was all that worried.\n\n\"The trend is always going to be up and to the right,\" said Sara Brescia O'Rear, a former pharmacist who retired in her 40s thanks to her investing success. \"I'm willing to take on risk because I know it can pay off.\"\n\nThe Wall Street Journal spent two days talking to individual investors at Hood Summit, asking them about their riskiest investing moves, their concerns about the current market and what it means to make \"life-changing money\" by betting it all.\n\nMarkese Johnson said he never considered buying stocks until well into adulthood, when a childhood friend started showing him the ropes while Johnson was serving in the U.S. Army.\n\nHis lucky break came when he bought several thousand shares of cloud computing company IREN when its share price was roughly $4. The stock is now trading around $35.\n\n\"It's helping me live a lifestyle I never dreamed I would live,\" he said. Now, Johnson said he can afford experiences that once felt out of reach, like vacations to the Dominican Republic, or courtside seats to a Washington Wizards game.\n\n[Trader profiles: Markese Johnson, 36, Hometown: Dallas, Occupation: Logistics Account Coordinator, Trading since: 2019; Alexander Bittan, 33, Hometown: Englewood, N.J., Occupation: Entrepreneur, Stock picks: Alphabet, Uber; Sara Brescia O'Rear, 41, Hometown: St. Simons Island, Ga., Occupation: Full-time trader, Stock picks: Palantir, Robinhood; James Chen, 34, Hometown: San Diego and Detroit, Occupation: Full-time trader, Trading since: 2010; Colin Edsman, 36, Hometown: Waimea, Hawaii, Occupation: Stay-at-home dad, Stock picks: GoPro, E.l.f. Beauty, Trading since: 2017; Lance Zakrzewski II, 37 (with mom Kitty), Hometown: Jackson, Mich., Occupation: Business owner.]\n\n[\"I want [my children] to see you can build wealth the right way,\" he said. Alexander Bittan made millions betting on Tesla shares back in 2017 and has been a fan of going all-in on just a few stocks ever since. Most of his investments are still tied up in a couple of tech names.]\n\n[\"If you have a couple high-conviction names, that's all you need to become extremely wealthy,\" he said. \"But you have to be right.\" Six years ago, Sara Brescia O'Rear started buying as many shares of Palantir as she could afford, eventually tying about one-third of her portfolio to the company's shares. Since then, the stock has surged nearly 2,000%. \"Life-changing money,\" she called it. Brescia O'Rear said she is holding on to a little more cash these days, but the fears of a potential AI overbuild don't keep her up at night. \"I'm very pro-technology,\" she said.]\n\n[\"Every successful business from the beginning of time—they're going to have to take on debt,\" she said. — Sara Brescia O'Rear]\n\n[James Chen, who was born in China, lost his job about a year and a half ago and decided to focus on investing full time. Since then, he has made about five times his former salary trading options and stocks like Alphabet, Tesla and Robinhood. \"In the past, my goal was to make it in corporate America,\" he said. His new aim? \"Trade myself into the new American dream.\" It isn't something he would recommend to other traders.]\n\n[But in 2024, Colin Edsman bet half of his savings on a stock trading for less than a dollar a share. A year later, the company, mineral provider United States Antimony, saw its share price climb into the double digits. Edsman made more than $13,000—and has made several similarly successful trades since. \"I've never relied on luck,\" he said. \"I figured I could make my own.\"]\n\n[In March 2020, Lance Zakrzewski put every dollar he had into the market, even taking early withdrawals from his retirement account, to plow more money into tech and dividend-paying stocks. His net worth tripled during the monthslong rebound that followed. The steady gains in his portfolio have allowed for some splurges, like the nine-day Caribbean cruise he booked for him and his mother, Kitty Zakrzewski, once Hood Summit wrapped. Zakrzewski said he is somewhat concerned about the market's reliance on a handful of AI companies. But should stocks suddenly tank, he has a plan: \"Probably just buy more.\"]\n\n[Photo caption: Hood Summit attendees, above, listen to Robinhood CEO Vlad Tenev, below left, deliver the event's keynote address in Houston last month. Below right, merchandise at the summit. (Jeff Lautenberger for WSJ)]",
  "priority": "P2",
  "type": "article",
  "turn_to": "B2",
